window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("llmops-platform", {"topics":[{"key":"llmops-platform/ci-cd-for-prompts-and-models","sources":["Humble & Farley, 2010 — Continuous Delivery","Breck et al., 2017 — The ML Test Score: A Rubric for ML Production Readiness and Technical Debt Reduction","Sculley et al., 2015 — Hidden Technical Debt in Machine Learning Systems"],"why":"- **Classic CI/CD assumptions break.** A conventional pipeline assumes behaviour is determined by code,\n  tests are deterministic, and the bits that passed are the bits that run. An LLM app violates all three:\n  behaviour depends on code, prompt text, the model behind an API alias, decoding parameters and the\n  retrieval index, and outputs are sampled, not computed.\n- **The model changes underneath you.** A provider updates the model behind `-latest`, a training job\n  produces a new adapter, an index is re-embedded. A green unit suite says nothing about whether answers\n  got worse.\n- **The three usual shortcuts fail.** Prompts as string constants get review but no evidence: a PR fixes\n  the one complaint in the ticket and regresses ten other cases that users find. Live dashboard edits are\n  fast but have no review, no rollback, and no record of which prompt produced which answer.\n- **Running the whole suite on every commit is the right instinct done badly.** Thousands of model calls\n  per push cost real money and take tens of minutes, so within a month the step is marked optional and\n  skipped.\n- **What works.** Treat prompts and models as first-class release artifacts: know which artifact changed,\n  run an eval of depth proportional to the stage's risk, gate on aggregate quality rather than exact\n  strings, and promote the exact bundle that was tested.","how":"### Three artifacts, three cadences\n| Artifact | Changes | Evaluation | Often edited by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Application code | daily | deterministic tests | engineers |\n| Prompts and configuration | hourly to daily | cheap to evaluate | product managers, forward-deployed engineers |\n| Models | weekly to quarterly | expensive; capacity and cost consequences | ML / platform team |\n\n- **A release is a bundle:** `{code sha, prompt versions, model id or weights revision, decoding params, index snapshot}`.\n- **The pipeline tests and promotes bundles**, because testing a prompt against one model and deploying\n  it with another tests nothing.\n\n### The test ladder\nDepth grows with the blast radius of the stage, because cost and wall-clock time scale with model calls.\n\n1. **Static and unit tests (seconds, no model calls).** Every template renders with every declared\n   variable; output parsers run against recorded model outputs; tool JSON schemas validate; guardrail\n   rules compile; the rendered prompt stays under its token budget.\n2. **Contract tests with recorded responses.** Replay fixtures through the real code path to check\n   plumbing (retries, streaming, parsing). They prove the wiring, not the quality.\n3. **Smoke eval on every PR (minutes, a few dollars).** A stratified subset of the golden set, 50 to 200\n   items as an illustrative size. Score with deterministic checks where possible and an LLM judge where\n   necessary; compare with the baseline score recorded by the last green build of main.\n4. **Full eval on merge, nightly and on release candidates.** The whole golden set plus slices, cost and\n   latency, with every output stored as a build artifact so reviewers can diff behaviour.\n5. **Deploy with a canary** and online metrics, and an automatic rollback trigger.\n\n### Gating on distributions, not strings\n- **Exact-match assertions on free text flake**, because outputs are sampled.\n- **Compare score distributions instead:** a mean with a confidence interval against the champion,\n  absolute floors for safety and schema validity, and per-slice floors.\n- **Stored outputs are the review artifact.** The diff between the candidate's and the champion's answers\n  on the same items is what a reviewer actually reads.\n- **Statistics:** the `evaluation-gates-for-production-releases` lesson covers them.\n\n### Content-addressed caching\n- **Where it sits.** Eval cost is dominated by model calls, so the cache sits at the model-client boundary.\n  Every call is keyed by `hash(model id, decoding params, exact rendered request)`.\n- **What \"rendered request\" means.** The full message list (prompt text, retrieved context, tool\n  definitions) the application actually sends.\n- **Why not key on `(prompt version, item input)`.** A code or index change that alters retrieval or prompt\n  assembly changes the request and misses the cache. A key built only from the item's input would serve\n  the old output and hide the change.\n- **What it buys.** A PR that touches one prompt re-runs only the items whose requests that prompt affects.\n  A code-only PR that leaves every request byte-identical re-runs nothing and just re-scores. This is what\n  makes a per-PR eval affordable.\n- **The key must include every pin.** A key that omits the model id will happily serve stale outputs after\n  a model upgrade and hide the change.\n- **Accept knowingly:** with temperature above zero, a cache hit replays one frozen sample, so the cached\n  smoke eval measures that sample, not the sampling variance.\n\n### Promote, do not rebuild\n- **Promotion moves a pointer.** The bundle that passed the release eval goes dev → staging → production by\n  moving a pointer in the prompt and model registries.\n- **Never rebuild at deploy time**, because a rebuild re-resolves floating aliases (`-latest`, `main`) and\n  can ship something that was never tested.\n\n### Prompts as code versus prompts as config\n| Approach | How a prompt deploys | Gain | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Prompts in the repository | normal PR pipeline | simple, reviewable | non-engineers blocked; prompt deploys coupled to code deploys |\n| Prompt registry | a label change is the deploy; a registry webhook triggers the same eval gate before the `production` label moves | fast, decoupled | a second deploy path to gate |\n| Hybrid (most mature teams) | registry is the source of truth; the pipeline is the only thing allowed to move production labels | both | discipline on label permissions |\n\n### When the yardstick changes\n- **A baseline is relative** to the dataset version and scorer version it was measured with.\n- **Yardstick changes move every score.** Adding twenty items to the golden set, relabeling a slice, or\n  upgrading the judge model changes every score without any change to the bundle.\n- **Treat them as their own kind of change:**\n  1. re-run the current champion under the new yardstick;\n  2. write a new baseline tagged with the dataset hash and scorer version;\n  3. only then compare candidates.\n- **Self-describing baselines.** A baseline file that records its own dataset hash and scorer version lets\n  the job refuse a comparison that would otherwise blame a prompt for a judge upgrade.\n- **Trigger on the yardstick too:** changes to `data/golden/**` or to scorer code should run the eval job.\n\n### Model changes are releases too\n- **Pin the model snapshot in the bundle.**\n- **Run a scheduled \"model upgrade\" job** that opens a PR with the new snapshot and the full eval when the\n  provider announces a new version or a deprecation.\n- **Treat a retrained adapter** arriving in the model registry the same way. The trigger differs; the\n  ladder is identical.","example":"### PR-time smoke eval with a content-addressed cache\nA PR-time smoke eval that sits as one job in the pipeline.\n\n- **The YAML fragment** shows where it runs.\n- **The script** wraps the content-addressed cache around the model client and compares against the\n  baseline written by the last green build of `main`, refusing if that baseline used a different dataset\n  or scorer.\n- **Your pieces:** `call_app`, `llm_call`, `score` and `SCORER_VERSION` are your application's entrypoint\n  (accepting an injected model client), your raw provider call, your scorer and its version string.\n- **Cost:** with a 100-item smoke set and a warm cache, a PR that edits one prompt used by 30 items pays for\n  30 application model calls (and 30 judge calls, if a judge scorer goes through the same cache), not 100\n  of each.\n\n```yaml\n# .github/workflows/llm-ci.yml (fragment): trigger on anything that changes the bundle\non:\n  pull_request:\n    paths: [\"app/**\", \"prompts/**\", \"release.json\", \"data/golden/**\", \"evals/scorers/**\"]\njobs:\n  smoke-eval:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: python scripts/smoke_eval.py --subset smoke   # nightly job runs --subset full\n```\n\n```python\n# scripts/smoke_eval.py\nimport hashlib, json, pathlib, sys\n\nbundle = json.load(open(\"release.json\"))   # {\"model\": \"vendor-model-2025-04-14\", \"prompt\": \"support_answer@v12\", \"params\": {...}}\nsubset = sys.argv[sys.argv.index(\"--subset\") + 1]\ndata = pathlib.Path(f\"data/golden/{subset}.jsonl\").read_bytes()\nitems = [json.loads(l) for l in data.splitlines()]\nyardstick = {\"dataset\": hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest()[:12], \"scorer\": SCORER_VERSION}\ncache = pathlib.Path(\".evalcache\"); cache.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n\ndef cached_llm(messages):   # key = model + params + the exact rendered request, so any change that alters it misses\n    blob = json.dumps([bundle[\"model\"], bundle[\"params\"], messages], sort_keys=True)\n    p = cache / hashlib.sha256(blob.encode()).hexdigest()\n    if p.exists():\n        return json.loads(p.read_text())\n    out = llm_call(bundle[\"model\"], bundle[\"params\"], messages)\n    p.write_text(json.dumps(out)); return out\n\ndef run_item(item):\n    out = call_app(bundle, item[\"input\"], llm=cached_llm)   # the production code path; only its model calls are cached\n    return {\"id\": item[\"id\"], \"output\": out, \"score\": score(item, out)}\n\nresults = [run_item(i) for i in items]\nmean = sum(r[\"score\"] for r in results) / len(results)\nbase = json.load(open(f\"eval_baselines/main-{subset}.json\"))   # written by the last green build of main\nif base[\"yardstick\"] != yardstick:                              # new items or a new judge: scores are not comparable\n    sys.exit(\"baseline was measured with a different dataset or scorer; re-baseline the champion first\")\njson.dump({\"bundle\": bundle, \"yardstick\": yardstick, \"mean\": mean, \"items\": results}, open(\"eval_out.json\", \"w\"))\nprint(f\"candidate={mean:.3f} baseline={base['mean']:.3f} n={len(results)}\")\nsys.exit(0 if mean >= base[\"mean\"] - 0.02 else 1)   # smoke tolerance; the release gate uses a paired CI instead\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what each kind of PR costs with a warm cache\nSame 100-item smoke set, judge calls routed through the same cache. Call counts follow from the cache key;\nthey are not a benchmark.\n\n| Change in the PR | Requests that change | App calls | Judge calls | Gate outcome |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Refactor that leaves every rendered request byte-identical | none | 0 | 0 | re-score only, seconds |\n| Edit one prompt used by 30 items | 30 | 30 | 30 | compare with baseline |\n| Retrieval change that alters context for 55 items | 55 | 55 | 55 | compare with baseline |\n| Model snapshot bump in `release.json` | all 100 (model id is in the key) | 100 | 100 | full cost, as it should be |\n| 20 items added to the smoke set | yardstick hash changes | 20 to re-baseline the champion | 20 | refused until re-baselined |\n\n- **Read the last row carefully.** The champion's 100 existing requests are still cached, so re-baselining\n  costs only the 20 new items; without the yardstick check the PR would be blamed for a dataset change.\n- **Judge calls follow app calls** because the judge's request embeds the candidate output: a changed answer\n  is a changed judge request.","patterns":["**Path-filtered eval triggers** — when only some PRs touch the bundle. Use the `paths` filter shown above (code, prompts, `release.json`, golden data, scorers) so docs-only PRs skip model calls. Monitor eval minutes and spend per PR.","**Required status check on the smoke eval** — when people start merging around a red eval. Mark the smoke-eval job as a required check in branch protection so a failing eval blocks merge. Monitor how often admins override it.","**Cancel superseded runs** — when developers push several commits to one PR. In GitHub Actions set a `concurrency` group per PR with `cancel-in-progress: true` so only the latest commit pays for an eval. Monitor queued-run counts and eval wall-clock time.","**Eval outputs as build artifacts** — when reviewers need to read behaviour, not just a score. Upload `eval_out.json` with `actions/upload-artifact` and post the candidate-versus-champion diff on the PR. Monitor whether reviewers open it before approving.","**Pipeline-only production labels** — when prompts live in a registry. Give only the CI service account permission to move the `production` label, triggered by the registry webhook after the gate passes. Alert on any label move without a linked eval run.","**Scheduled model-upgrade job** — when you pin provider snapshots. A cron-triggered workflow (`on: schedule`) checks for new snapshots or deprecation notices and opens a PR with the full eval attached. Monitor days remaining before each pinned snapshot's deprecation date."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Code PR\"] --> L[\"Static and contract tests\"]\n    P[\"Prompt edit in registry\"] -->|\"webhook\"| L\n    M[\"New model snapshot\"] -->|\"scheduled upgrade PR\"| L\n    L --> S[\"Smoke eval vs baseline\"]\n    S -->|\"fail\"| X[\"Block merge\"]\n    S -->|\"pass\"| F[\"Full eval and gates\"]\n    F --> R[(\"Registries: immutable bundle\")]\n    R -->|\"move pointer, no rebuild\"| K[\"Canary\"]\n    K -->|\"metrics regress\"| B[\"Auto rollback\"]\n    K -->|\"healthy\"| PR[\"Production\"]","caption":"Each artifact change enters the same ladder; only the pipeline moves the production pointer, and the\npromoted bundle is the one that was tested."},"interview":[{"q":"Why can't a normal CI pipeline with unit tests protect an LLM application?","a":"- **Behaviour lives outside the code.** Prompt text, the model behind an alias, decoding params and the\n  index all change answers, and none of them is exercised by unit tests on code.\n- **Outputs are sampled,** so exact-match assertions flake; you need eval scores compared as distributions\n  against a champion.\n- **So the unit of release is the bundle,** tested by an eval whose depth matches the stage's blast radius."},{"q":"A code-only PR changed how retrieved chunks are ordered, and the smoke eval re-ran nothing. What is wrong?","a":"- **The cache key is too coarse.** It is probably keyed on prompt version and item input instead of the\n  exact rendered request, so a different message list hit an old entry.\n- **Fix:** key on model id, decoding params and the full rendered message list at the model-client\n  boundary, so any change in what the app sends misses the cache.\n- **Verify** by asserting the miss count is non-zero on a PR known to change retrieval."},{"q":"When would you not run an eval on every PR?","a":"- **When the PR cannot change the bundle,** such as docs or infra-only changes; path filters skip them.\n- **When the eval is too expensive to stay fast,** run a stratified smoke subset per PR and the full suite\n  on merge, nightly and release candidates, otherwise the step gets marked optional.\n- **The cost:** a 100-item subset cannot resolve a one- or two-point change, so small regressions wait for\n  the full eval or canary."},{"q":"How do you handle a provider deprecating the model snapshot you pinned?","a":"- **Pin and schedule.** The bundle pins the snapshot; a scheduled upgrade job opens a PR with the new\n  snapshot as soon as a new version or deprecation is announced.\n- **Same ladder:** the full eval runs against the champion, the cache misses on every item because the\n  model id is in the key, and promotion is a pointer move.\n- **Monitor** days left before each pinned snapshot is retired, so the migration is never on the\n  provider's emergency timetable."},{"q":"The golden set grew by 20 items and every PR started failing by the same margin. What happened?","a":"- **The yardstick changed, not the bundles.** Scores against the new dataset are not comparable with a\n  baseline measured on the old one.\n- **Fix:** record the dataset hash and scorer version in the baseline, refuse mismatched comparisons, and\n  re-run the champion to write a new baseline before comparing candidates.\n- **Trigger** the eval job on `data/golden/**` and scorer changes so re-baselining happens automatically."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fast per-PR evals vs resolution.** Smoke evals catch gross regressions in minutes, but a 100-item subset cannot resolve a one- or two-point change. The moment you need that resolution you must pay for the full set or a canary.","**Cheap cached evals vs hidden changes.** Caching makes evals cheap and fast, yet every omitted pin in the cache key becomes a way to hide a real change. The more aggressive the cache, the more disciplined the key must be.","**Prompts-as-config vs a second deploy path.** It decouples prompt deploys from code deploys and unblocks non-engineers, but that path must be gated as strictly as the first or it becomes the bypass everyone uses.","**Aggregate gates vs individual catastrophes.** Gating on aggregate scores tolerates sampling noise, but it also tolerates an individual catastrophic answer. Safety and schema checks therefore need absolute per-item floors, not averages.","**Pinned snapshots vs deprecation.** Pinning gives reproducible builds, but snapshots get deprecated. Without a scheduled upgrade job, pinning turns into an emergency migration on the provider's timetable."],"pitfalls":["**Evaluating a copy of the prompt chain.** Running evals against a reimplementation inside the test harness instead of the production entrypoint. Symptom: a green pipeline and a production regression caused by code the eval never executed. Fix: call the real entrypoint with an injected model client.","**Comparing against a fixed threshold.** Rather than the current champion. Symptom: quality erodes a point per release while every release passes, and nobody notices until a customer does.","**Retrying a flaky eval until it goes green.** Symptom: a gate whose pass rate is uncorrelated with quality and a team that has stopped reading the numbers.","**Moving the `production` label from the UI.** Without the pipeline. Symptom: an incident whose timeline contains a prompt change with no PR, no eval and no author.","**Using `-latest` model aliases in the bundle.** Symptom: a score change on a day with no commits, traced eventually to a provider update. Fix: pin the snapshot.","**Comparing against a stale yardstick.** A baseline measured on an older golden-set or judge version. Symptom: every PR failing (or passing) by the same few points the day the dataset grew, with no prompt to blame."],"when":"- **Use when** more than one person edits prompts or a prompt is deployed to real users: that is when \"we\n  changed something and it got worse\" becomes unanswerable.\n- **Start cheap.** Unit tests on templates and parsers plus a 50-item smoke eval against a stored\n  baseline; add the full suite and canary when release frequency or user impact justifies them.\n- **Measure** pipeline cost per PR, eval wall-clock time, cache hit rate and, most importantly, the number\n  of production regressions the pipeline would have caught.\n- **Interviews:** the expected answer to \"how do you deploy a prompt change safely\" is this ladder: unit,\n  recorded-response contract, smoke eval versus baseline, full eval with gates, canary with rollback, with\n  the bundle, not the prompt, as the unit of release.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the smoke-eval job, the content-addressed cache and the baseline file, and keeps the harness calling the real application entrypoint rather than a reimplementation.","architect":"Decides that the bundle is the unit of release, which registry owns each artifact, and that the pipeline is the only path allowed to move a production label.","fde":"Reproduces a lightweight version of the ladder inside the customer's CI, often with the customer's own golden set, so their change-management process has evidence rather than a demo."},"check":{"q":"A product manager edits a system prompt in the registry UI at 4 pm and moves the `production`\nlabel; latency is unchanged but support tickets about wrong refund amounts double overnight. The code\npipeline is green. What went wrong structurally, and how would you change the pipeline so it cannot\nhappen again?","a":"Structurally, the prompt had a deploy path that bypassed every test: the code pipeline only runs on\ncommits, and the registry label move was an unreviewed, un-evaluated production change. The fix is not\nto forbid non-engineers from editing prompts but to make the label move itself the thing the pipeline\ngates. Registry edits create a new immutable version; a webhook or scheduled job runs the smoke eval and,\nfor `production`, the full eval and gates against the champion on the same golden set, including a\nrefund-intent slice with a floor; only the pipeline's service account may move the production label,\nand it writes the eval evidence next to the version. Rollback is moving the label back. Finally, add the\nincident's cases to the golden set so the regression is a permanent test."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the PR stage run a stratified subset of the golden set rather than the full suite?","options":["Subsets are statistically more reliable than the full set","Eval cost and time scale with model calls, so depth is matched to the blast radius of each stage","Full suites can only be run against production models","GitHub Actions cannot run jobs longer than ten minutes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every item is a model call that costs money and time; a per-PR job must stay fast and cheap or it will be skipped. The full suite runs on merge, nightly and on release candidates where the stakes justify it."},{"q":"Why should the tested bundle be promoted by pointer rather than rebuilt at deploy time?","options":["Rebuilding is slower because weights must be downloaded again","Registries cannot store more than one version at a time","A rebuild re-resolves floating aliases and can deploy a model or prompt that was never evaluated","Promotion avoids the need for a canary stage"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The whole point of the gate is that the exact bits that passed are the bits that run. A rebuild that resolves `-latest` or `main` again may pick up a provider update or a prompt edit the eval never saw."},{"q":"What is the strongest reason to put the model snapshot id inside the eval cache key?","options":["It makes the cache directory easier to browse","Providers require it for billing attribution","Without it, a model upgrade would be served stale cached outputs and the change would be invisible","Hashing is cheaper when the key is longer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The cache exists to skip calls whose inputs have not changed. If the model id is not part of the key, swapping the model counts as \"unchanged\" and the eval silently measures the old model's cached outputs."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/prompt-registry","sources":["Official docs: Langfuse","Official docs: MLflow"],"why":"- **Prompts are behaviour-defining config edited by people who do not ship code.** String constants are\n  fine for one engineer and one prompt. They stop being fine when a product manager wants a new tone, a\n  forward-deployed engineer needs a customer variant, or an incident review asks \"which prompt produced\n  this response at 02:14 on Tuesday\".\n- **Constants make every change expensive.** Every tweak is a code deploy, variants become nested\n  if-statements, and the trace holds the rendered text at best and nothing at worst.\n- **A mutable `prompts` table is worse.** An admin page solves the deploy problem, but a mutable row has no\n  history: a bad edit looks like the previous good one, there is no review, no eval evidence, and rollback\n  means remembering the old text. Teams discover this at 3 am.\n- **The registry separates content from pointer.** Content is immutable once created, so every past trace\n  resolves forever. The pointer saying which content an environment uses is mutable, so deploy and\n  rollback are a pointer move. It is the model registry's version/alias split, applied to text.","how":"### Data model\n| Object | Mutable? | Holds | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Prompt** | n/a | a name, such as `support_answer` | the stable handle code asks for |\n| **Version** | no, append-only | template body (usually a list of chat messages with roles, not one string), declared variables and types, model settings it was tuned against (model id, temperature, max tokens, output schema), content digest, author, change note, created timestamp | a trace that recorded `support_answer@v12` must resolve to the same text in a year |\n| **Label** | yes | pointer from `(prompt, label)` to a version: `production`, `staging`, `canary`, or tenant-scoped like `tenant:acme` | deploying code to change which version runs is what the registry exists to avoid |\n| **Evidence** | attached to a version | eval run fingerprints and scores on a named golden-set version, links to the approving review | \"was this text tested\" has one answer regardless of where it is deployed |\n| **Move log** | append-only | `who`, `from`, `to`, `when`, `why` for every label move | rollback needs the previous target; an audit needs what was live at 02:14 on Tuesday |\n\n- **Deploy is a label move; rollback is the same operation in reverse.** An A/B test is two labels\n  resolved by a routing rule.\n- **Label moves are compare-and-set:** \"move `production` from v12 to v13, only if it is still v12\", so two\n  people promoting at once cannot silently overwrite each other.\n\n### Prompt and model are a pair\n- **A prompt is tuned to a model.** The same text that works on one model family may over-refuse, ignore\n  the schema or change tone on another.\n- **So a version stores model id and decoding parameters** alongside the text.\n- **Swapping the model under an unchanged prompt is a new bundle** and must be re-evaluated; the registry\n  makes that visible by refusing to pretend the text alone is the artifact.\n\n### Runtime fetch\n1. **Fetch by name and label at request time** through an SDK that keeps an in-memory copy with a short\n   TTL and refreshes in the background.\n2. **Serve the stale copy** if the registry is unreachable.\n3. **Ship a bundled copy** of the production versions with the application as a last-resort fallback.\n\n- **Why this matters:** the registry is never on the hot path in the sense that its outage cannot take the\n  product down, which is the condition under which it is acceptable to let it control behaviour at all.\n- **Log every call's prompt name, version and digest into the trace.** Observability platforms with prompt\n  management (Langfuse is the common open-source example) link a trace to its prompt version\n  automatically, turning \"find every answer produced by v12\" into a filter.\n\n### TTL is the propagation delay\n- **Every label move, rollback included, takes up to one TTL to land.** With a 60-second TTL and fifty\n  replicas, for up to a minute after a move some replicas serve the old version and some the new.\n- **Fine for a planned deploy, too slow for an emergency rollback** of a harmful prompt. That is why some\n  SDKs and home-grown registries add push invalidation (a webhook or pub/sub message that evicts the\n  cached entry) on top of the TTL.\n\n### Change workflow\n1. **Create a draft version.**\n2. **Run the eval suite** against it (the registry or the CI pipeline triggers this) and attach the results.\n3. **Get a review.** A diff view between two versions, both text and settings, is the review artifact.\n4. **Move the label**, done only by the pipeline's service account, so a UI edit can create a version but\n   cannot deploy it.\n\n- **Declared variables are an interface** between the prompt and the code that renders it. A version that\n  adds `{{customer_tier}}` can be labeled `production` only once the deployed code supplies that variable.\n- **So validate at the label move** (the version's variable set against what the running application\n  declares it provides), and make rendering fail loudly on a missing variable rather than send the literal\n  placeholder to the model.\n\n### Where to host it\n| Option | Gives you | Watch out for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prompts in a git directory with a build step | review and history for free | couples prompt deploys to code deploys unless the app loads them from an object store at runtime |\n| Hosted registry in an observability or eval platform (Langfuse, LangSmith, Braintrust; MLflow added a prompt registry in 2025, first experimental in the 2.x line and expanded in 3.x) | labels, a UI for non-engineers, trace linkage | a vendor dependency on the request path, mitigated by caching |\n| Home-grown table with versions and labels | a few hundred lines; often right for a small platform team that already owns a config service | you build the UI, diff and audit yourself |\n\nWhat matters is the split between immutable versions and mutable labels, not the vendor.","example":"### A minimal registry\nA minimal registry showing the three operations that matter:\n\n- **Register an immutable version.** Identical content maps to the existing version, so re-registering is\n  idempotent.\n- **Move a label** with compare-and-set and a move log.\n- **Fetch with a TTL cache** that serves stale on failure.\n\nA real deployment backs `store` with a database and adds the bundled-file fallback for a cold start while\nthe registry is down.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, time\n\nclass PromptRegistry:\n    def __init__(self, store):                 # store: {\"versions\": {name: [...]}, \"labels\": {(name, label): {...}}}\n        self.store, self._cache = store, {}\n\n    def register(self, name, messages, model, params, author, note):\n        body = json.dumps({\"messages\": messages, \"model\": model, \"params\": params}, sort_keys=True)\n        digest = hashlib.sha256(body.encode()).hexdigest()[:12]\n        versions = self.store[\"versions\"].setdefault(name, [])\n        for v in versions:\n            if v[\"digest\"] == digest:\n                return v[\"version\"]            # same content, same version: registering is idempotent\n        v = {\"version\": len(versions) + 1, \"digest\": digest, \"messages\": messages, \"model\": model,\n             \"params\": params, \"author\": author, \"note\": note, \"created\": time.time(), \"evals\": {}}\n        versions.append(v)                     # append-only: a version is never edited or deleted\n        return v[\"version\"]\n\n    def set_label(self, name, label, version, by, expected):   # deploy and rollback are both this one call\n        cur = self.store[\"labels\"].get((name, label), {}).get(\"version\")\n        if cur != expected:                    # compare-and-set: a concurrent move must not be clobbered\n            raise RuntimeError(f\"{name}:{label} is v{cur}, not v{expected}\")\n        self.store[\"labels\"][(name, label)] = {\"version\": version, \"by\": by, \"at\": time.time()}\n        self.store.setdefault(\"moves\", []).append((name, label, cur, version, by, time.time()))  # rollback target\n\n    def get(self, name, label=\"production\", ttl=60):\n        now, hit = time.time(), self._cache.get((name, label))\n        if hit and now - hit[\"at\"] < ttl:\n            return hit[\"v\"]\n        try:\n            ver = self.store[\"labels\"][(name, label)][\"version\"]\n            v = self.store[\"versions\"][name][ver - 1]\n            self._cache[(name, label)] = {\"v\": v, \"at\": now}\n            return v\n        except Exception:\n            if hit:\n                return hit[\"v\"]                # registry down: serve stale rather than fail the request\n            raise\n\nreg = PromptRegistry({\"versions\": {}, \"labels\": {}})\nv1 = reg.register(\"support_answer\",\n                  [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Answer using only the provided context: {{context}}\"},\n                   {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"{{question}}\"}],\n                  model=\"vendor-model-2025-04-14\",        # a dated snapshot id, never a floating alias\n                  params={\"temperature\": 0.2, \"max_tokens\": 600}, author=\"ana\", note=\"initial\")\nreg.set_label(\"support_answer\", \"production\", v1, by=\"ci:release-42\", expected=None)\np = reg.get(\"support_answer\")\n# Log p[\"version\"] and p[\"digest\"] on every trace so any answer resolves to its exact prompt.\n```\n\n### Guarded promotion, strict rendering and rollback\nContinues the registry above. A new version needs `{{customer_tier}}`; promotion is refused until the\ndeployed code declares it supplies that variable, and rollback reads its target from the move log.\n\n```python\nimport re\n\nVAR = re.compile(r\"\\{\\{(\\w+)\\}\\}\")\n\ndef template_vars(messages):\n    return {name for msg in messages for name in VAR.findall(msg[\"content\"])}\n\ndef render(messages, **values):            # fail loudly instead of sending a literal placeholder\n    def sub(m):\n        if m.group(1) not in values:\n            raise KeyError(f\"missing template variable {m.group(1)!r}\")\n        return str(values[m.group(1)])\n    return [{**msg, \"content\": VAR.sub(sub, msg[\"content\"])} for msg in messages]\n\ndef promote(reg, name, version, app_provides, by):\n    v = reg.store[\"versions\"][name][version - 1]\n    missing = template_vars(v[\"messages\"]) - set(app_provides)\n    if missing:                            # the variable set is the prompt/code interface\n        raise RuntimeError(f\"v{version} needs {sorted(missing)}; deployed code does not supply them\")\n    cur = reg.store[\"labels\"].get((name, \"production\"), {}).get(\"version\")\n    reg.set_label(name, \"production\", version, by=by, expected=cur)\n\ndef rollback(reg, name, by):\n    moves = [m for m in reg.store[\"moves\"] if m[:2] == (name, \"production\")]\n    _, _, prev, cur, _, _ = moves[-1]      # the move log knows what the label pointed at before\n    if prev is None:\n        raise RuntimeError(\"nothing to roll back to\")\n    reg.set_label(name, \"production\", prev, by=by, expected=cur)\n\nv2 = reg.register(\"support_answer\",\n                  [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Tier {{customer_tier}}. Use only: {{context}}\"},\n                   {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"{{question}}\"}],\n                  model=\"vendor-model-2025-04-14\", params={\"temperature\": 0.2, \"max_tokens\": 600},\n                  author=\"raj\", note=\"tier-aware tone\")\ntry:\n    promote(reg, \"support_answer\", v2, {\"context\", \"question\"}, by=\"ci:release-43\")\nexcept RuntimeError as e:\n    print(\"blocked:\", e)          # blocked: v2 needs ['customer_tier']; deployed code does not supply them\npromote(reg, \"support_answer\", v2, {\"context\", \"question\", \"customer_tier\"}, by=\"ci:release-44\")\nrollback(reg, \"support_answer\", by=\"oncall:lee\")   # production is v1 again, and the move is logged\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: rolling back a harmful prompt across fifty replicas\nA prompt version starts giving wrong refund amounts. Fifty replicas use the SDK cache with a 60-second TTL.\n\n| Mechanism | Time until every replica serves the old version | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Label move, TTL only | up to 60 seconds | each replica picks it up when its entry expires |\n| Label move plus push invalidation | about the delivery time of the message | replicas evict and refetch on the event |\n| Prompt baked into the image at deploy time | a full redeploy | the label move changes nothing, a classic pitfall |\n| Registry down during the incident | never, via the registry | replicas keep serving stale; you need a code-side kill switch |\n\n- **The move log gives the rollback target** without anyone remembering the old text.\n- **The traces give the blast radius:** filter by the bad version's id and digest to list every affected\n  answer.","patterns":["**SDK cache with an explicit fallback** — whenever prompts are fetched at runtime. Configure the client cache TTL (the Langfuse SDK's `get_prompt` accepts a cache TTL and a fallback prompt) and ship the production versions as a bundled file. Monitor fetch error rate and how often stale or fallback copies are served.","**Pipeline-only production label** — when non-engineers can edit prompts. Grant label-move permission for `production` only to the CI service account; humans can create versions and move `staging`. Alert on any production move without a linked eval run.","**Push invalidation for emergencies** — when a one-TTL delay is too slow for rollback. Publish a message on label move that evicts cached entries, keeping the TTL as the backstop. Monitor time from move to the last replica reporting the new version.","**Version and digest on every span** — when you need incident forensics. Add prompt name, version and digest as trace attributes on every model call. Monitor the share of production traces that resolve to a registered version; anything under 100% is a gap.","**Tenant labels instead of forks** — when customers need variants. Resolve `tenant:<id>` first and fall back to `production`, with the same gate for both. Monitor the number of tenant labels pointing at versions with no recent eval evidence."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    E[\"Edit in UI or PR\"] --> V[(\"Immutable version + digest\")]\n    V --> EV[\"Eval suite attaches evidence\"]\n    EV --> CI[\"Pipeline service account\"]\n    CI -->|\"compare-and-set move\"| L[\"Label: production\"]\n    L --> ML[(\"Append-only move log\")]\n    A[\"App request\"] --> C[\"SDK cache, short TTL\"]\n    C -->|\"expired\"| L\n    C -.->|\"registry down\"| S[\"Stale copy or bundled file\"]\n    C --> T[\"Trace logs name, version, digest\"]","caption":"Edits create immutable versions; only the pipeline moves a label; the app reads through a TTL cache with\nstale and bundled fallbacks, and logs the version it used."},"interview":[{"q":"What problem does a prompt registry solve that a prompts table in the database does not?","a":"- **History and resolvability.** A mutable row loses the old text; immutable versions mean every trace's\n  version id resolves to the exact content forever.\n- **Deploy and rollback as a pointer move.** Labels are the only mutable part, moved with compare-and-set\n  and logged, so rollback has a known target.\n- **Evidence.** Eval results attach to versions, so you can prove what was tested before it went live."},{"q":"Two engineers promote different versions to production at the same moment. What should happen?","a":"- **Compare-and-set on the label.** Each move says which version it expects to replace; the second one sees\n  the label already moved and fails instead of silently overwriting.\n- **The loser re-reads and decides.** It can re-run its gate against the new champion before trying again.\n- **The move log** records both attempts' outcome, so the audit shows exactly who changed what."},{"q":"Your registry has an outage. What does the product do?","a":"- **Keep serving.** The SDK serves its in-memory copy past the TTL, and a bundled file of production\n  versions covers a cold start.\n- **What you lose** is the ability to deploy or roll back prompts until it recovers, so keep a code-side\n  kill switch for harmful behaviour.\n- **Design rule:** a registry may control behaviour only if its outage cannot take the product down."},{"q":"When would you not use a hosted prompt registry?","a":"- **When only engineers edit a few prompts:** a git directory gives review, blame and history for free.\n- **When a request-path vendor dependency is unacceptable** and you already own a config service; a\n  home-grown versions-and-labels table is a few hundred lines.\n- **The flip point** is when non-engineers own prompts or tenants need variants; then labels and a UI pay\n  for themselves."},{"q":"A rollback took a full minute to reach every replica. Is that a bug?","a":"- **No, that is the TTL.** With a 60-second cache TTL, each of the fifty replicas picks up the move when its\n  entry expires, so mixed versions serve for up to a minute.\n- **For emergencies add push invalidation:** a webhook or pub/sub event that evicts cached entries\n  immediately, keeping the TTL as the backstop.\n- **Measure** time from label move to the last replica reporting the new version."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fast iteration vs a second change path.** Decoupling prompt deploys from code deploys lets non-engineers iterate in minutes, but it creates a second production change path that must be gated as strictly as code or it becomes the bypass.","**Immutable history vs storage.** Immutable versions guarantee every trace resolves forever, at the cost of a growing store and a retention policy. For most teams prompts are kilobytes and this never matters; for multi-tenant platforms with thousands of variants it does.","**Runtime control vs a dependency.** Runtime fetching keeps behaviour changeable without redeploy but adds a dependency. The TTL cache and bundled fallback remove the availability risk while introducing a propagation delay of one TTL.","**Explicit prompt-model pairs vs migration cost.** Storing model settings with the prompt makes re-evaluation mandatory on model change. It also means a platform-wide model migration touches every prompt version rather than one config line.","**Hosted registry vs git directory.** Hosted gives UI, labels and trace linkage out of the box; git gives review and blame for free. Hosted wins once people outside engineering own prompts."],"pitfalls":["**Editing a version in place \"because it is just a typo\".** Symptom: two traces with the same version id and different rendered text, which destroys every post-incident analysis that relies on the id.","**Resolving labels at deploy time and baking the text into the image.** Symptom: a label move that changes nothing until the next deploy, and an engineer who concludes the registry is broken.","**Logging only the prompt name.** Not the version and digest. Symptom: an investigation that knows which prompt family failed and cannot tell whether it was before or after Tuesday's change.","**Treating the registry as prompt-only.** Leaving temperature and model id in application config. Symptom: a prompt that passed its eval behaves differently in production because the two were tuned apart.","**Letting anyone with edit rights move `production` from the UI.** Symptom: a behaviour change with no pipeline run and no evidence attached.","**Promoting a version whose new variable the code does not supply.** Symptom: model inputs containing a literal `{{customer_tier}}` or an empty slot, and answers that ignore the tier. Fix: validate variables at the label move and render strictly."],"when":"- **Use when** a second person edits prompts, a prompt needs per-tenant or per-locale variants, or an audit\n  or incident process must tie an answer to the exact text that produced it.\n- **Avoid (for now) when** one engineer owns a handful of prompts: a prompts directory in the repository\n  with versioned file names is enough.\n- **Measure** what the registry should deliver: time from prompt edit to production, number of versions\n  with attached eval evidence, and whether every production trace resolves to a version.\n- **Interviews:** expected depth is the immutable-version versus mutable-label split, why evidence attaches\n  to versions, how the runtime fetch avoids becoming a single point of failure, and why the prompt and its\n  model settings are versioned together.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Integrates the SDK with TTL caching and fallback, logs version and digest on every trace, and makes the eval pipeline run on new versions automatically.","architect":"Decides where the registry lives, who may move which labels, how tenant variants are scoped, and that prompt plus model settings is the versioned unit.","fde":"Uses tenant labels to ship customer-specific prompt variants without forking the product, and shows the customer's change-control team the version history and attached evidence."},"check":{"q":"Your registry stores only prompt text, and application config holds `temperature=0.7`. A platform\nteam migrates the whole fleet to a new model snapshot by changing one config line, and a legal-summary\nprompt that passed its evals last month starts omitting mandatory disclaimers. Explain what the registry\ndesign got wrong and what the version record should have contained.","a":"The registry treated text as the artifact when the artifact is the pair of text and the model\nsettings it was evaluated against. Because the model id lived outside the version, the migration did not\ncreate a new version, triggered no eval, and left every trace pointing at a version id that now means\nsomething different in practice. The version record should hold the model snapshot id, temperature,\nmax tokens and output schema alongside the messages, with a digest over all of it; a model migration\nthen becomes a new version per prompt, each of which must pass the eval suite and be promoted by label.\nThe migration gets slower by exactly the amount of evidence it was skipping, and the disclaimer\nregression would have been caught by the legal-summary golden set before the label moved."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must prompt versions be immutable while labels are mutable?","options":["Immutable versions compress better in storage","Traces must resolve to the exact text forever, while deploy and rollback need to be a pointer move","Labels are immutable too; only drafts can change","Mutable versions would break template rendering"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Immutability of content keeps past traces meaningful and evidence attached to the right text; mutability of the pointer is what lets a deploy or rollback happen without shipping code."},{"q":"An application fetches prompts from the registry on every request with no cache. What is the main risk?","options":["Prompts will be rendered with stale variables","Versions will be created on every request","A registry outage or slowdown takes the product down with it","Labels cannot be resolved without a cache"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Runtime fetching is acceptable only when the registry cannot become a single point of failure, which is what the TTL cache, background refresh and bundled fallback provide."},{"q":"Where should eval results for a prompt be attached, and why?","options":["To the label, because that is what production uses","To the version, because evidence describes the content regardless of where it is deployed","To the trace, because that is where the answer appears","To the application release, because prompts ship with code"],"answer":1,"explanation":"\"Was this text tested\" has a single answer per version. Attaching it to a label would lose the evidence every time the label moved and would let an untested version inherit a tested label's history."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/model-registry","sources":["Official docs: MLflow","Official docs: Hugging Face Hub","Mitchell et al., 2019 — Model Cards for Model Reporting"],"why":"- **Files in a bucket do not say what is serving.** Names like `adapter_final_v2_really.safetensors`,\n  hand-copied retrains, rollbacks that mean \"find the old file and hope\", and no answer to \"which data was\n  this trained on\".\n- **An LLM \"model\" is several artifacts.** A base model identity, a LoRA adapter, a quantization choice, a\n  tokenizer revision and a serving configuration can each drift independently while all still look like\n  \"the model\".\n- **The naive fixes fail predictably.** Git does not survive multi-gigabyte weights even with LFS and has no\n  lineage to runs or evals. \"Deploy the newest file\" makes deploys non-deterministic: two replicas started\n  an hour apart can serve different models. A spreadsheet is the pattern with no enforcement, wrong within\n  a week.\n- **The registry borrows from package managers:** immutable versions with content hashes, and mutable\n  named pointers that environments resolve. It adds what models need and packages do not: lineage,\n  evaluation evidence, a model card and an approval state.","how":"### Entities\n- **Registered model:** a name, such as `support-classifier`.\n- **Version (immutable):** records\n  - the artifact URI and content hash of the weights;\n  - the format or flavour (a Transformers checkpoint, a LoRA adapter, an ONNX graph, a pyfunc wrapper);\n  - an input and output signature, plus the params and metrics of the producing run;\n  - lineage: tracking run id, training dataset version, code commit, parent base model and its revision;\n  - free-form tags, a description or model card, and an approval state.\n- **Why immutable:** a deployment that resolved version 7 must mean the same bytes next year, and evidence\n  attached to a version is meaningless if the bytes can change underneath it.\n- **Alias (mutable):** a pointer from a name to a version: `champion`, `challenger`, `staging`,\n  `tenant-acme`. Promotion is setting an alias; rollback is setting it back to the previous version.\n- **Stages versus aliases.** MLflow's registry originally used fixed stages (Staging, Production,\n  Archived). Newer versions replaced them with free-form aliases plus tags, because fixed stages could not\n  express \"two production models for two tenants\" or \"the canary candidate\".\n\n### What counts as a model in an LLM application\nRegister every artifact whose change alters model behaviour:\n\n| Artifact | Why it is registered |\n|---|---|\n| Fine-tuned weights and LoRA adapters | the obvious case |\n| Quantized variants | one version per quantization, because they are evaluated separately |\n| Embedding models | their version must match the vector index built with them |\n| Reward or judge models | a judge change moves every score |\n| Provider-hosted models | a \"virtual\" version records only a provider snapshot id and decoding config |\n\n- **Virtual versions matter:** they give an API-based model the same lifecycle, evidence and rollback story\n  as self-hosted weights.\n- **Bundles:** a release bundle references a model version and a prompt version; some teams register the\n  bundle itself as a model so one alias move changes both.\n\n### Lifecycle\n1. **Register from a tracking run**, so lineage is automatic.\n2. **Attach evidence:** the eval run fingerprint and scores on a named golden-set version.\n3. **Request review.** The reviewer reads the model card, the metric diff against the champion and the\n   slice results.\n4. **Promote by alias, deploy, observe the canary**, then promote to `champion`.\n5. **Deprecate later:** mark the version archived, keep the bytes for the retention period the lifecycle\n   policy specifies, and refuse new deployments of it.\n\n### Serving integration\n- **Config references an alias:** `models:/support-classifier@champion`.\n- **At process start** the server resolves the alias to a version, downloads the artifact by hash,\n  verifies it, and logs the resolved version into every trace and response header.\n- **Resolve once, at startup, deliberately.** A running replica never changes model mid-flight, and a\n  rollout happens by starting new replicas against the new alias target, which is what a canary needs.\n- **Embedders use the same mechanism** to prevent the classic bug of querying an index built with embedder\n  v2 using embedder v3: the index build records the embedder version and the query path refuses a mismatch.\n\n### Rollback is a rollout\n- **A pointer move in the registry, a new rollout in serving.** Its real duration is the time to start\n  replicas on the previous version: download, verify and load.\n- **API model:** seconds. **Tens of gigabytes of self-hosted weights:** it can be many minutes.\n- **So keep the previous champion warm.** Teams keep its artifact cached on serving nodes (or a few\n  previous-version replicas running) until the new champion has survived its canary window.\n- **Measure rollback time end to end;** the alias move is the fast part.\n\n### Hugging Face Hub as a registry\n- **For open weights the Hub is a git-backed registry:** each repository has a commit history, a model card\n  in the README, and `from_pretrained(..., revision=\"<commit sha>\")` pins an exact version.\n- **Pinning to a branch name such as `main`** is the same mistake as a floating `-latest` alias.\n- **Mirror the pinned revision** into your own registry so an upstream deletion or license change cannot\n  break a deploy.\n\n### Governance\n- **What auditors and security reviewers ask for:** access control on alias moves, signed or hash-verified\n  artifacts, a model card per version, and a record of who approved what.\n- **Design it in:** cheap up front, expensive to retrofit.","example":"### Register, attach evidence, promote and resolve with MLflow\nThe registry lifecycle with MLflow's client API: register a version from a tracked run, attach the\nevidence a reviewer needs, promote by alias, and have the server resolve the alias once at startup. Tag\nvalues are illustrative; the lineage fields are what matter.\n\n```python\nimport mlflow\nfrom mlflow import MlflowClient\n\nclient = MlflowClient()\nNAME = \"support-classifier\"\n\n# 1. Register the artifact a tracked run produced; the run id is the lineage link to params, data and code\nrun_id = \"3f1c0a9e2b7d\"                                          # the run that trained the LoRA adapter\nmv = mlflow.register_model(f\"runs:/{run_id}/model\", NAME)        # returns a new immutable version number\n\n# 2. Attach the evidence a reviewer and, later, an auditor will ask for\nclient.set_model_version_tag(NAME, mv.version, \"base_model\", \"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\")\nclient.set_model_version_tag(NAME, mv.version, \"base_revision\", \"<hf commit sha>\")   # pinned, not 'main'\nclient.set_model_version_tag(NAME, mv.version, \"golden_set\", \"golden-v8@5c2e91\")\nclient.set_model_version_tag(NAME, mv.version, \"eval_fingerprint\", \"run:9a71cc\")\nclient.set_model_version_tag(NAME, mv.version, \"eval_accuracy\", \"0.912\")\nclient.update_model_version(NAME, mv.version,\n                            description=\"Retrained on Q3 tickets; fixes refund-intent confusion\")\n\n# 3. Promote by moving a pointer; rollback is this same call with the previous version number\nclient.set_registered_model_alias(NAME, \"challenger\", mv.version)\n# ... canary healthy for the agreed window ...\nclient.set_registered_model_alias(NAME, \"champion\", mv.version)\n\n# 4. The serving process resolves the alias once at startup and records what it got\nresolved = client.get_model_version_by_alias(NAME, \"champion\")\nprint(\"serving\", NAME, \"version\", resolved.version, \"from\", resolved.source)   # log into every trace\nmodel = mlflow.pyfunc.load_model(f\"models:/{NAME}/{resolved.version}\")   # load the version just resolved, not the\n                                                                          # alias again: it may move in between\n```\n\n### Refusing an embedder-index mismatch at startup\nPure Python, runnable. The index build job records the embedder version; the query service compares it\nwith the embedder version it resolved from the registry and refuses to start on a mismatch.\n\n```python\nclass EmbedderMismatch(RuntimeError):\n    pass\n\ndef check_index(index_meta, resolved_embedder):\n    built_with = index_meta[\"embedder\"]                 # written by the index build job\n    if built_with != resolved_embedder:\n        raise EmbedderMismatch(f\"index {index_meta['name']} built with {built_with}, \"\n                               f\"but this replica resolved {resolved_embedder}\")\n\nindex_meta = {\"name\": \"kb-main\", \"embedder\": \"kb-embedder:4\"}      # name:version from the registry\ncheck_index(index_meta, \"kb-embedder:4\")                           # ok: same vector space\ntry:\n    check_index(index_meta, \"kb-embedder:5\")                       # someone moved the embedder alias\nexcept EmbedderMismatch as e:\n    print(\"refusing to serve:\", e)   # loud failure instead of silently nonsense neighbours\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what a rollback really costs\nIllustrative orders of magnitude for one replica, not a benchmark. An 8B-parameter model in fp16 is about\n16 GB (8 billion × 2 bytes).\n\n| Model | Alias move | Get bytes | Verify hash | Load and warm up | End to end |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Provider snapshot (virtual version) | instant | none | none | none | seconds: restart with the old snapshot id |\n| 8B fp16, previous champion cached on node | instant | none | about 16 GB to hash | load into GPU | well under a minute to a few minutes |\n| 8B fp16, not cached, pulled from object store | instant | ~16 GB download | about 16 GB to hash | load into GPU | many minutes, worse if every replica pulls at once |\n\n- **The alias move is the fast part.** Everything after it is a rollout.\n- **Keeping the previous champion cached** removes the slowest step until the new champion has survived its\n  canary window.","patterns":["**Alias URIs in serving config** — for every deployment. Reference `models:/<name>@champion` in config, resolve once at startup with `get_model_version_by_alias`, then load by the resolved version number. Monitor that every replica logs a resolved version and that versions across replicas agree outside a rollout.","**Pinned and mirrored Hub revisions** — when serving open weights. Download with an explicit commit sha (`snapshot_download(repo_id, revision=sha)` in `huggingface_hub`) into your own artifact store and load from there. Monitor upstream repositories for force-pushes, gating or license changes.","**Warm previous champion** — when weights are tens of gigabytes. Keep the prior champion's artifact on serving nodes' local disk until the new champion passes its canary window. Monitor end-to-end rollback time in drills, not just the alias move.","**Promotion only by the pipeline** — when audits matter. Restrict alias-set permission on `champion` to a CI service account that checks for eval evidence tags before moving it. Alert on any version promoted without an `eval_fingerprint` tag.","**Embedder version stamped on the index** — for any RAG system. The index build writes the embedder version into the index metadata; the query service refuses to start on mismatch. Monitor mismatch refusals and retrieval quality after embedder changes."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Tracking run\"] -->|\"register, lineage\"| V[(\"Immutable version + hash\")]\n    E[\"Eval run on golden set\"] -->|\"evidence tags\"| V\n    V --> RV[\"Review: card, metric diff, slices\"]\n    RV -->|\"set alias\"| CH[\"Alias: challenger\"]\n    CH --> K[\"Canary replicas\"]\n    K -->|\"healthy\"| CP[\"Alias: champion\"]\n    CP -->|\"resolve once at startup\"| S[\"Serving replica\"]\n    S --> T[\"Trace logs resolved version\"]\n    K -.->|\"regress: set alias back\"| CP","caption":"Versions flow in from tracked runs and gather evidence; aliases point at them; servers resolve the alias\nonce at startup and log what they got."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a model version and an alias, and why do you need both?","a":"- **A version is immutable:** exact bytes by hash, lineage and evidence, so version 7 means the same thing\n  next year.\n- **An alias is a mutable pointer** such as `champion` that environments resolve; promotion and rollback\n  are alias moves.\n- **Both are needed** because deploys must be deterministic (versions) while what an environment runs must\n  change without code edits (aliases)."},{"q":"Two replicas give different answers to the same input after a rolling restart. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Compare the resolved versions** each replica logged at startup; if they differ, config pointed at\n  something floating such as `latest` or a Hub branch.\n- **If versions match,** compare tokenizer revision and serving config, which may not be part of the\n  registered version.\n- **Fix** by pinning to a version or alias resolved once, and registering tokenizer and serving config with\n  the weights."},{"q":"When would a model registry be overkill?","a":"- **One model, two people, never retrained:** a pinned artifact URI with a hash in config is the honest\n  minimum.\n- **The flip point** is the first retrain, quantization or swap after launch, or a second environment that\n  needs a different model.\n- **API-only teams still benefit** once a provider snapshot change would be user visible, via virtual\n  versions."},{"q":"Your rollback took twenty minutes even though the alias move was instant. Why, and what would you change?","a":"- **Rollback is a rollout:** each replica must download, hash-verify and load the old weights, and an 8B\n  fp16 model is about 16 GB.\n- **Many replicas pulling at once** contend for object-store bandwidth.\n- **Change:** keep the previous champion cached on nodes or a few old-version replicas warm through the\n  canary window, and measure rollback end to end in drills."},{"q":"How do you stop a fine-tuned adapter for one customer reaching another tenant?","a":"- **Scope aliases per tenant** (for example `tenant-acme`) and keep the serving path for each tenant\n  resolving only its own aliases.\n- **Record the training dataset version** in lineage so it is clear which customer's data an adapter used.\n- **Restrict who can set tenant aliases** and audit every move."}],"tradeoffs":["**Deterministic deploys vs storage.** Immutable versions plus aliases give deterministic deploys and one-call rollback, at the cost of storage for every version ever promoted; a retention policy matters once weights are tens of gigabytes.","**Stable replicas vs instant switches.** Resolving the alias at startup keeps replicas stable and rollouts explicit, but a hot swap without restart is impossible by design. Teams that want instant global switches must accept replicas that disagree for a while.","**Virtual versions vs discipline.** Registering provider-hosted models gives API models the same lifecycle, but it only helps if application code reads the model id from the registry rather than from its own config.","**Governance vs ceremony.** A full registry with approvals and signing satisfies auditors and security; for a two-person team with one model it is ceremony, and a pinned artifact URI in a config file is the honest minimum.","**Atomic bundles vs attribution.** Registering model plus prompt as one versioned unit makes promotion atomic, but it multiplies versions whenever either half changes and blurs which half caused a metric move."],"pitfalls":["**Pointing serving config at a branch or `latest` tag.** Instead of a version or alias. Symptom: two replicas returning different answers for the same input after a rolling restart.","**Registering weights without the tokenizer and serving config.** Symptom: a version that scores well in the eval harness and produces garbage in production because the tokenizer revision differs.","**Advancing the embedding model without rebuilding the index.** Symptom: retrieval quality collapsing after a \"routine\" model upgrade while every service reports healthy.","**Attaching eval results to the alias rather than the version.** Symptom: a rolled-back champion that inherits the evidence of the version that replaced it.","**Resolving the alias, then loading by alias again.** Symptom: a replica whose logged version differs from the weights it actually loaded, because a promotion landed between the calls.","**Pinning only to a Hub repository name and mirroring nothing.** Symptom: a broken deploy the day the upstream author force-pushes, gates the repository or changes the license."],"when":"- **Use when** a model is first retrained, quantized or swapped after launch, or two environments need\n  different models.\n- **Before then** a pinned artifact URI with a hash in config is sufficient and honest.\n- **Include provider-hosted models** as virtual versions once an API model swap would be a user-visible\n  change, which is nearly always.\n- **Measure** whether every serving replica logs a resolved version, how long a rollback takes end to end,\n  and whether every promoted version carries eval evidence and a lineage link.\n- **Interviews:** explain immutable versions versus aliases, why aliases are resolved at startup, how the\n  registry prevents an embedder-index mismatch, and how provenance flows from a tracking run into a\n  registered version and out into a trace.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Registers versions from training runs, wires the server to resolve aliases at startup and log the resolved version, and refuses index queries whose embedder version mismatches.","architect":"Defines what counts as a model artifact, the alias vocabulary per environment and tenant, the approval and retention policy, and whether bundles or components are the unit of promotion.","fde":"Keeps a customer-scoped registry or alias set so that a fine-tuned adapter trained on one client's data can never be promoted into another tenant's serving path."},"check":{"q":"A team serves a RAG system with an open embedding model loaded from a Hub repository's `main`\nbranch. One morning retrieval quality drops sharply, no deploy happened, and every health check is\ngreen. Walk through the likely cause and how a registry would have prevented it.","a":"The likely cause is that the upstream repository received a new commit on `main`, so replicas that\nrestarted overnight loaded a different embedding model than the one that built the index; query vectors\nand index vectors now live in different spaces, nearest neighbours are nonsense, and nothing errors\nbecause the shapes still match. A registry prevents this in three places: the embedder is a registered\nversion pinned to a commit hash and mirrored, so a restart loads identical bytes; the index build\nrecords the embedder version it used; and the query path checks that the resolved embedder version\nequals the index's recorded version and refuses to serve, or alerts, on mismatch. The fix is to pin the\nrevision, rebuild or roll back, and add the mismatch check so the failure becomes loud."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a serving process resolve the `champion` alias at startup rather than on each request?","options":["Alias lookups are too slow for request latency budgets","A replica should never change model mid-flight; rollouts happen by starting new replicas, which is what a canary needs","Registries only allow one resolution per client","Per-request resolution would bypass the model cache"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Resolving once makes each replica's behaviour stable and attributable; a change in the alias target becomes a controlled rollout instead of an instantaneous, unobservable fleet-wide switch."},{"q":"What does registering a provider-hosted model as a \"virtual version\" buy you?","options":["Lower API cost through registry-level caching","The ability to fine-tune the provider's weights locally","The same lifecycle, evidence and rollback story for API models as for self-hosted weights","Automatic migration when the provider deprecates a snapshot"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The version holds a snapshot id and decoding config, so swapping the API model is a promoted, evaluated change with a rollback, not a config edit nobody can trace."},{"q":"Which lineage field is most important for an auditor asking \"what was this model trained on\"?","options":["The alias the model currently holds","The artifact storage region","The link from the version to the tracking run and its dataset version","The model's parameter count"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lineage flows from the training run, which recorded the dataset version, code commit and params, into the registered version; without that link the question has no verifiable answer."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/experiment-tracking","sources":["Official docs: MLflow","Official docs: Weights & Biases","Pineau et al., 2021 — Improving Reproducibility in Machine Learning Research"],"why":"- **Runs outpace memory.** Week one is a spreadsheet of learning rates and scores. By week three it is\n  missing the run everyone discusses, nobody knows which checkpoint produced the 0.91, and its config was\n  edited in place since.\n- **Fine-tuning is combinatorial.** LoRA rank, learning rate, data mix, epochs and base model revision\n  interact, so a result without its full configuration cannot be reproduced or trusted.\n- **LLM app evals are experiments too.** A prompt version, model snapshot, dataset version and judge\n  configuration go in; scores come out. Results in notebook cells and Slack screenshots cannot answer \"did\n  the last three prompt changes help on the refund slice\" without re-running everything.\n- **Logs and file names fail because comparison and lineage are the whole job.** You must filter a thousand\n  runs by parameter, overlay metric curves, and follow a model version back to its run, data and commit:\n  a database with a specific schema and a UI on top, which is what a tracker is.","how":"### The data model both tools share\n| Concept | What it is | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Experiment** (MLflow) / **project** (W&B) | groups runs | one place to compare |\n| **Parameters** | fixed at start, treated as immutable | changing them mid-run would make comparisons meaningless |\n| **Metrics** | time series against a step or epoch | see divergence or over-fitting at step *k*, not just the final number |\n| **Artifacts** | files: checkpoints, per-item eval outputs, plots, configs | error analysis and lineage |\n| **Tags** | free-form labels | filtering |\n| **Captured context** | git commit, command line, Python environment, optionally system metrics such as GPU utilisation | reproducibility |\n\n- **Param immutability differs by tool.** MLflow rejects re-logging a param with a different value. W&B\n  lets `config` be updated, but that is an escape hatch, not the model.\n- **System metrics:** W&B records them by default; MLflow does once system-metrics logging is switched on.\n- **Runs can be nested** for hyperparameter sweeps.\n- **Everything is queryable:** \"all runs where `lora_r=16` sorted by `eval_acc`\" is one call.\n\n### What happens when you call `log`\nBoth clients are thin writers to a store; their transport explains several sharp edges.\n\n- **MLflow over REST.** The client sends each logging call to the tracking server (or writes to a local\n  `mlruns` directory with no server). The server writes SQL rows: params as key/value *strings*, metrics as\n  `(key, value, timestamp, step)` rows, plus a \"latest value\" per metric that the run table and search use.\n- **MLflow batching.** `log_metrics` and `log_params` exist *because* one HTTP round trip per scalar at\n  every training step is slow; recent versions also offer asynchronous logging.\n- **W&B background process.** `init` starts a process that buffers everything to a local run directory and\n  streams it to the server, *because* a training loop must never block on the network.\n- **W&B offline mode** falls out of that design: `WANDB_MODE=offline`, uploaded later with `wandb sync`,\n  for air-gapped clusters.\n- **Artifacts take a separate path.** Large files go to the artifact store (bucket or W&B storage); only a\n  reference lands in the run.\n\n### Three sharp edges that follow\n1. **MLflow params are strings,** so a filter such as `params.lr > 0.001` is not a numeric comparison. Put\n   numbers you will range-query into metrics or tags you control.\n2. **MLflow sorts on a metric's *last* logged value, not its best.** Log a separate `best_eval_acc` if that\n   is what you will rank by.\n3. **W&B requires the `step` passed to `log` to increase monotonically.** A call with an earlier step is\n   dropped with a warning, which bites when resuming a run or logging eval results after the training loop\n   has advanced.\n\n### MLflow\n- **Origin and licence:** Apache-licensed open source that started at Databricks.\n- **Two stores:** a backend store (a SQL database for runs, params and metrics) and an artifact store (local\n  disk, S3, GCS or Azure Blob for files). That is why self-hosting is a container plus a database plus a\n  bucket.\n- **Around tracking:** a model registry with versions and aliases; a packaging format with \"flavours\"\n  (`pyfunc`, `transformers`, `sklearn` and others) so any registered model loads with one call; and\n  autologging that captures params and metrics from common frameworks without explicit calls.\n- **GenAI features:** through the later 2.x releases and the 3.x line it added tracing, LLM evaluation APIs\n  and a prompt registry, so an LLM team can keep evals and prompts in the same store as training runs.\n- **Hosting:** Databricks offers a managed version, and some cloud ML platforms (Amazon SageMaker, for\n  example) offer managed MLflow tracking servers; otherwise you self-host.\n\n### Weights & Biases\n- **SaaS-first,** with a self-hosted server option for data-residency requirements.\n- **API:** `wandb.init(project=..., config=...)`, `wandb.log({...}, step=...)` and `run.finish()`.\n- **Distinguishing pieces:**\n  - **Artifacts:** versioned datasets and models with an automatic lineage graph (which run consumed which\n    artifact version and produced which).\n  - **Sweeps:** a built-in hyperparameter search orchestrator that schedules agents across machines.\n  - **Tables** for per-example analysis in the UI, and **Reports** for sharing.\n  - **Weave:** its toolkit for tracing and evaluating LLM applications.\n- **Why teams pick it:** the UI. **Why they do not:** cost per seat, and metrics and artifacts leave the\n  network unless you self-host.\n\n### How an LLM team uses either\n| Run type | Params | Metrics | Artifacts | Then |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Fine-tuning | `base_model`, `base_revision`, `lora_r`, `lr`, `train_set` version hash, `git_sha` | train and eval loss per step | adapter directory, per-item eval outputs | register the adapter as a model version so lineage flows into the registry |\n| Evaluation | prompt version, model snapshot, dataset hash, judge config | aggregate and per-slice scores | per-item outputs file | error analysis starts from the run page |\n\nThe tracker becomes the place where \"what did we try and what happened\" is answered.\n\n### Choosing\n- **Data residency or regulated environments** push toward self-hosted MLflow or a W&B server.\n- **Already on Databricks:** you get MLflow for free.\n- **Research-heavy teams running sweeps daily** usually prefer W&B's UI and sweep orchestration.\n- **LLM application teams** increasingly keep traces and evals in an observability platform such as\n  Langfuse or LangSmith and use the tracker only for training.\n- **The important decision** is one system of record per artifact class, not one tool for everything.","example":"### The same LoRA run logged to MLflow and to W&B\nThe same LoRA fine-tuning run logged two ways. Parameters are fixed at the start, metrics are\nstep-indexed, artifacts are files, and the dataset and code versions are logged as params so the run is\nreproducible.\n\n`train_and_eval` is your loop, yielding per-step metrics. The two halves are alternatives, shown together\nonly for comparison (as written, the loop would train twice).\n\n```python\nimport mlflow, wandb, subprocess\n\ncfg = {\"base\": \"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\", \"base_revision\": \"<hf commit sha>\",\n       \"lora_r\": 16, \"lr\": 2e-4, \"epochs\": 2,\n       \"train_set\": \"tickets-v3@9c1e04\", \"golden_set\": \"golden-v8@5c2e91\"}\ncfg[\"git_sha\"] = subprocess.check_output([\"git\", \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\"]).decode().strip()\n\n# --- MLflow: self-hosted tracking server; runs grouped by experiment -------------------------\nmlflow.set_tracking_uri(\"http://mlflow.internal:5000\")\nmlflow.set_experiment(\"support-classifier\")\nwith mlflow.start_run(run_name=\"lora-r16-lr2e-4\") as run:\n    mlflow.log_params(cfg)                                            # immutable for the run's lifetime\n    for step, (train_loss, eval_acc) in enumerate(train_and_eval(cfg)):\n        mlflow.log_metrics({\"train_loss\": train_loss, \"eval_acc\": eval_acc}, step=step)\n    mlflow.log_artifact(\"outputs/eval_items.jsonl\")                   # per-item outputs for error analysis\n    mlflow.log_artifacts(\"outputs/adapter\", artifact_path=\"model\")    # raw files; use a flavour's log_model for pyfunc loading\n    print(\"register with run id:\", run.info.run_id)\n\n# Comparison is the point: params are strings (quote them); ordering uses each metric's LAST value\nbest = mlflow.search_runs(experiment_names=[\"support-classifier\"], filter_string=\"params.lora_r = '16'\",\n                          order_by=[\"metrics.eval_acc DESC\"], max_results=5)\n\n# --- W&B: SaaS or self-hosted server; config + log + a versioned Artifact with lineage --------\nrun = wandb.init(project=\"support-classifier\", name=\"lora-r16-lr2e-4\", config=cfg)\nfor step, (train_loss, eval_acc) in enumerate(train_and_eval(cfg)):\n    wandb.log({\"train_loss\": train_loss, \"eval_acc\": eval_acc}, step=step)\nart = wandb.Artifact(\"support-classifier-adapter\", type=\"model\", metadata=cfg)\nart.add_dir(\"outputs/adapter\")\nart.add_file(\"outputs/eval_items.jsonl\")\nrun.log_artifact(art)                                                 # becomes v0, v1, ... in the lineage graph\nrun.finish()\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: the run table hides the best checkpoint\nPure Python, runnable. Two runs from one sweep; ranking by the last logged value (what the run table\nsorts on) picks the wrong one.\n\n```python\ncurves = {                       # eval_acc per eval step, two runs from the same sweep\n    \"lora-r16\": [0.80, 0.88, 0.91, 0.86],   # peaked at step 2, then over-fit\n    \"lora-r8\":  [0.82, 0.86, 0.88, 0.89],   # still climbing at the end\n}\n\nby_last = sorted(curves, key=lambda r: curves[r][-1], reverse=True)   # what a run table sorts on\nby_best = sorted(curves, key=lambda r: max(curves[r]), reverse=True)  # what you meant\nprint(\"by last value:\", by_last)   # ['lora-r8', 'lora-r16']\nprint(\"by best value:\", by_best)   # ['lora-r16', 'lora-r8']\n\nfor run, c in curves.items():      # log these as their own metrics so the tracker can rank on them\n    best_step = max(range(len(c)), key=c.__getitem__)\n    print(run, {\"best_eval_acc\": max(c), \"best_step\": best_step})\n```\n\n| Run | Last `eval_acc` | Best `eval_acc` | Best step | Rank by last | Rank by best |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| lora-r16 | 0.86 | 0.91 | 2 | 2nd | 1st |\n| lora-r8 | 0.89 | 0.89 | 3 | 1st | 2nd |\n\n- **Fix:** log `best_eval_acc` and `best_step` as metrics, keep the checkpoint from the best step as an\n  artifact, and rank on that.\n- **Still check the curve:** a single peak can be luck that the next seed will not repeat.","patterns":["**Self-hosted MLflow with real stores** — when data must stay in your network. Run the tracking server against a SQL database (`--backend-store-uri`) and a bucket for artifacts, behind your SSO proxy, rather than the default local file store. Monitor database size, artifact bucket growth and server latency on `log_metrics`.","**Mandatory run metadata** — when promoted models must be auditable. Wrap run start in a helper that logs `git_sha`, dataset hashes and base revision, and refuses to start if any is missing. Monitor the share of runs, and of registered versions, carrying all required params.","**Rank on a best-value metric** — whenever a metric can peak before the end. Log `best_eval_acc` and the step it occurred at alongside the per-step series, and sort on that. Monitor the gap between last and best values to spot over-fitting.","**Offline logging for air-gapped jobs** — when training nodes have no egress. Set `WANDB_MODE=offline` and upload later with `wandb sync` from a node that can reach the server. Monitor runs left un-synced.","**Artifact retention tied to the registry** — when per-item outputs and checkpoints pile up. Apply bucket lifecycle rules to run artifacts, but exclude anything a registered model version references. Monitor storage growth and any registry version whose source URI no longer resolves."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    J[\"Training or eval job\"] -->|\"params once at start\"| T[\"Tracking server\"]\n    J -->|\"metrics per step, batched\"| T\n    J -->|\"large files\"| A[(\"Artifact store: bucket\")]\n    T --> DB[(\"Backend store: runs, params, metrics\")]\n    A -.->|\"reference only\"| DB\n    DB --> Q[\"Search and compare runs\"]\n    Q -->|\"register best run\"| R[\"Model registry version\"]\n    R -->|\"lineage: run, data, commit\"| DB","caption":"A run writes params once, metrics per step and artifacts by reference; registering from the run carries\nlineage into the registry."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a parameter, a metric and an artifact in a tracking run?","a":"- **Parameters** are the configuration, fixed at start, because changing them mid-run changes the question\n  the run answers.\n- **Metrics** are step-indexed time series, so you can see divergence or over-fitting rather than one final\n  number.\n- **Artifacts** are files (checkpoints, per-item outputs, plots) stored in a bucket with only a reference in\n  the run."},{"q":"A search for `params.lr > 0.001` returns runs with lr = 0.0002. What is going on?","a":"- **MLflow stores params as strings,** so the comparison is lexicographic, not numeric.\n- **Fix:** log values you will range-query as metrics or as tags you control with a sortable format, and\n  keep params for exact-match filters.\n- **Related trap:** run-table sorting uses each metric's last value, so log a best-value metric too."},{"q":"How do you choose between MLflow and W&B for a regulated customer?","a":"- **Residency usually decides:** MLflow is self-hosted by default; W&B is SaaS-first with a self-hosted\n  server option.\n- **Then ecosystem:** MLflow brings a registry and model flavours; W&B brings sweeps, a lineage graph and a\n  richer UI, at a per-seat cost.\n- **Either way,** pick one system of record per artifact class and enforce required run metadata."},{"q":"Eval results logged after training never appear on the W&B chart. Why?","a":"- **Steps must increase monotonically in W&B.** A `log` call with a step lower than the last one is dropped\n  with a warning.\n- **This bites** when resuming a run or logging post-training evals at an earlier step.\n- **Fix:** log evals at the current or a later step, or under a separate metric axis, and watch the console\n  for the warning."},{"q":"When would you not put LLM eval runs in the training tracker?","a":"- **When evals start from production traces:** an observability platform such as Langfuse or LangSmith\n  links each eval to the traces it came from.\n- **The cost** is split history between two systems, so define which one is the record for eval runs.\n- **Keep training** runs, adapters and lineage into the registry in the tracker."}],"tradeoffs":["**Self-hosted MLflow vs operations.** It keeps data in your network and costs nothing in licences, but you operate a server, a database and an artifact store, and the UI is thinner than W&B's.","**W&B convenience vs cost and residency.** Its hosted UI, sweeps and lineage graph save engineering time, but per-seat pricing and data leaving the network flip the decision in regulated environments unless you run the self-hosted server.","**Per-item artifacts vs storage.** Logging every per-item output makes error analysis one click away, at the cost of storage that grows with runs times dataset size; sample or compress once eval sets reach six figures.","**Autologging vs signal.** It captures params and metrics with zero code, but also whatever the framework decides is interesting, which can bury the three numbers you care about under three hundred.","**Training tracker vs observability platform for evals.** The tracker unifies history; the observability platform links evals to traces. The winner depends on whether your evals start from production traces or from training jobs."],"pitfalls":["**Logging the final metric only.** Symptom: a run that looks best on its last value while its curve shows it diverged and recovered by luck, which the next seed will not repeat.","**Forgetting the data version and git commit in params.** Symptom: a top run that nobody can reproduce because the training set was regenerated since.","**Mutating a config dict after `log_params`.** And assuming the tracker noticed. Symptom: runs whose recorded learning rate differs from the one actually used.","**Treating the tracker as a backup for weights.** Symptom: a bucket bill that doubles monthly and a cleanup that deletes the one checkpoint the registry still points to.","**Ranking by a metric whose final value is not its best.** Such as eval accuracy after over-fitting. Symptom: the run table hides the checkpoint that actually peaked, because sorting uses the last value.","**Logging with a lower step in W&B.** Resuming a run or logging post-training evals with a step lower than the last one. Symptom: eval points that silently never appear on the chart, with only a warning in the console.","**Running evals outside the tracker \"because it is just a prompt change\".** Symptom: a team arguing from memory about whether version 11 or 12 did better on the hard slice."],"when":"- **Use from** the first fine-tuning run or the first eval you intend to compare against a later one, which\n  is essentially always.\n- **Choose MLflow** when you need self-hosting, already run Databricks, or want the registry and model\n  packaging in the same system.\n- **Choose W&B** when sweep orchestration, the UI and artifact lineage are worth the subscription, and data\n  can leave your network or you will run the self-hosted server.\n- **Measure** whether every registered model version links to a run, whether every run has a data version\n  and commit, and how long it takes a new team member to find \"the best run on the refund slice\".\n- **Interviews:** describe the run data model, how lineage flows from a run into the registry, and the\n  hosting and ecosystem differences between the two tools.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments training and eval scripts with params, step metrics and artifacts, and registers the resulting model version from the run so lineage is automatic.","architect":"Chooses the system of record per artifact class, decides self-hosted versus SaaS from residency and cost, and sets the minimum metadata every run must carry.","fde":"Stands up a tracker inside the customer's environment when their data cannot leave, so customer-specific fine-tuning and evals have the same history and reproducibility as internal work."},"check":{"q":"A colleague reports that run 42 reached 0.91 accuracy and asks you to promote its adapter. The\nrun page shows only `lr` and `epochs` as params and a single final metric. What questions do you ask\nbefore promoting, and what should the run have logged to make them unnecessary?","a":"First, which base model revision, which training set version and which code commit produced it;\nwithout those the adapter cannot be retrained or audited. Second, what the evaluation set was and\nwhether it was the current golden version, because 0.91 on last month's set is not comparable to this\nweek's baseline. Third, how the metric evolved over steps, since a single final number can hide a\ndivergent run that landed on a good value by chance. Fourth, where the per-item outputs are, so the\nrefund-intent slice can be checked. The run should have logged base revision, dataset hashes, git sha\nand LoRA settings as params, train and eval metrics per step, and the eval outputs and adapter as\nartifacts, with the adapter registered from the run so the registry version carries that lineage."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are a run's parameters fixed at the start while metrics are logged over time?","options":["Parameters are stored in a different database table for speed","Parameters define the experiment and must stay constant for comparison, while metrics describe how it unfolded","Metrics cannot be stored as strings","Parameters are only used by sweeps"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A run is an answer to \"what happens with this configuration\"; if the configuration changes mid-run the question changes. Step-indexed metrics let you see divergence and over-fitting instead of a final number."},{"q":"Which difference most often decides between MLflow and W&B in a regulated enterprise?","options":["W&B cannot log artifacts","MLflow has no model registry","Self-hosting and data residency: MLflow is self-hosted by default, W&B is SaaS-first with a self-hosted option","MLflow only supports PyTorch"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both tools cover the core data model. Where metrics, configs and artifacts are allowed to live is usually the constraint that decides, with W&B's UI and sweeps as the counterweight when residency is not binding."},{"q":"What links a registered model version back to the data it was trained on?","options":["The alias assigned at promotion time","The run that produced it, which logged the dataset version as a parameter","The artifact file's modification timestamp","The model card's free-text description"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lineage is captured at training time in the run; registering from the run carries that link into the version. An alias says where a model is deployed, not where it came from."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/evaluation-frameworks","sources":["Liang et al., 2022 — Holistic Evaluation of Language Models","Zheng et al., 2023 — Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena","Official docs: Promptfoo","Official docs: lm-evaluation-harness"],"why":"- **A second team builds a second engine.** The `evals/eval-harness` lesson explains one engine. Team A\n  stores CSV with a `label` column and reports \"87% pass\"; team B keeps JSONL with a `reference` field and\n  reports \"judge score 0.71\". Asked about a new model snapshot, every team re-runs something different and\n  the decision is made on anecdotes.\n- **Numbers are unstable even within one team.** The rubric lives in a notebook, the dataset was edited\n  last Tuesday, and the \"baseline\" was measured against a model alias that has since been updated.\n- **The naive alternatives fail specifically.** Notebooks are not repeatable, so Friday's number cannot be\n  compared with Tuesday's. A spreadsheet of hand-checked outputs is honest but stale within a sprint and\n  cannot gate a pipeline.\n- **Public benchmarks and dashboards miss the point.** Benchmarks (MMLU, HumanEval and friends) measure the\n  base model, not your prompt, retrieval, tools and guardrails, and the popular ones are widely suspected\n  to have leaked into frontier-model training data. A dashboard without dataset discipline, calibrated\n  scorers and pinned runs renders noise with good typography.\n- **The contribution is standard interfaces, not cleverness.** With one schema, pieces become swappable (a\n  new judge, a new dataset version), runs compare across teams and weeks, caching makes per-PR evals\n  affordable, and every score links back to its trace.","how":"### The five interfaces\n1. **Dataset.** Items of `{id, input, expected or reference, slice metadata}`, stored as immutable,\n   content-hashed versions.\n   - Immutable *because* a score only means something relative to a dataset version; an item relabeled in\n     place silently moves every baseline.\n   - Which datasets exist and how items get in: the `golden-dataset-strategy` lesson.\n2. **Target.** An adapter that calls the production code path with a pinned bundle (model snapshot, prompt\n   version, decoding parameters, index snapshot).\n   - It calls the real application *because* evaluating a copy of the prompt evaluates the copy: template\n     rendering, post-processing and retrieval are part of what ships.\n3. **Scorers.** Three families, each with a name and a version (see the table below). Every score is stored\n   as `{item_id, scorer, scorer_version, value, rationale}`, the version *because* a judge upgrade changes\n   scores without any change to the application.\n4. **Runner.** Executes dataset × target with concurrency, retries and timeouts.\n   - Caches model calls keyed by the exact request sent (model id, decoding params, rendered messages).\n   - Writes raw outputs *before* scoring and records a manifest of every pin. Raw outputs first *because*\n     scorers change: re-scoring with a new judge should cost judge calls only, never a target re-run.\n   - Records timeouts as errors, not zero scores, so a provider incident does not masquerade as a\n     regression.\n5. **Results store and comparison.** Per-run aggregates per slice with confidence intervals, and a\n   per-item diff between any two runs that share a dataset version. The item diff is what a reviewer reads;\n   the aggregate is what the gate reads.\n\n### Scorer families\n| Family | Examples | Strength | Cost and caveat |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Deterministic** | exact match, regex, JSON-schema validity, unit tests that execute generated code | cheap, unambiguous | only applies to structured outputs |\n| **Model-graded** | LLM judge against a rubric, against a reference answer, or pairwise between two outputs | covers free text | a model call per item; the judge is itself a model that drifts, so calibrate against human labels and pin its snapshot |\n| **Human** | expert review | ground truth | used to calibrate judges and to sign off high-risk changes |\n\n### Three categories of framework, chosen by the question\n| Category | Examples | Answers | Does not answer |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Capability benchmark harnesses** | EleutherAI's lm-evaluation-harness, HELM | \"which base model\" | anything about your application |\n| **Application eval frameworks** | Promptfoo (prompts × providers × test cases with assertions), DeepEval (pytest-style tests), RAGAS (retrieval metrics such as faithfulness and context precision) | \"did this prompt or pipeline change help\", on *your* dataset through *your* pipeline | production feedback |\n| **Platform-integrated tools** | Braintrust, LangSmith, Langfuse | \"how do we close the loop with production\": a bad trace becomes a dataset item with one click; scores attach to traces | base-model choice |\n\n- **The `evals/tooling` lesson** compares the tools.\n- **The architectural point:** a team usually needs one from the second category wired to one from the\n  third, and the first only when choosing a base model.\n\n### Where it plugs into the platform\n- **Registries.** The prompt registry and model registry store a version's eval results as metadata, and\n  promotion reads them.\n- **CI.** CI calls the framework through a CLI or SDK and applies the gate policy (the\n  `evaluation-gates-for-production-releases` lesson). The framework reports; the pipeline decides.\n- **Observability.** The stack runs the *same* scorers on sampled production traces, so offline and online\n  numbers share a definition.\n- **Dataset pipeline (the reverse flow).** Failures mined from traces and error analysis become candidate\n  items.\n\n### Build or adopt\n- **Differentiating assets** are the dataset and the scorers, never the runner or the results store.\n- **Adopt** when the task shape is common (chat, RAG, classification); write only the target adapter and\n  custom scorers.\n- **Build a thin layer** when the target does not fit the framework's data model (long multi-turn agents\n  with tool side effects, simulated users), when data residency forbids a SaaS store, or when scorers must\n  run inside your network.\n- **Even then,** keep the five interfaces and reuse an open-source store rather than inventing a results\n  format.","example":"### A minimal framework skeleton\nThe five interfaces as code. The point is the shape:\n\n- **Scorers carry a version,** and the target is the production entrypoint.\n- **Raw output is stored before any scoring** under a *generation id* that hashes only what determines the\n  outputs (dataset version and bundle). The run id adds the scorer versions on top.\n- **So an interrupted run resumes** without paying again for finished items.\n- **Swapping `judge@rubric-v3` for `judge@rubric-v4`** on a 1,000-item set yields a new run id but reuses\n  all 1,000 stored outputs: 1,000 judge calls, zero target calls.\n- **A changed prompt** changes the bundle, hence the generation id, hence fresh outputs.\n- **Repeats for a noise-floor measurement** need a repeat index in the generation id; otherwise every\n  \"repeat\" replays the same stored sample and the measured noise is zero.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, asdict\nfrom typing import Any, Callable\nimport hashlib, json, pathlib, time\n\n@dataclass\nclass Item:                      # one row of a content-hashed dataset version\n    id: str; input: dict; expected: Any = None; slice: str = \"default\"\n\n@dataclass\nclass Scorer:                    # name + version travel with every score it produces\n    name: str; version: str; fn: Callable[[Item, str], tuple[float, str]]\n\nTarget = Callable[[dict], str]   # the production code path, with the pinned bundle inside\n\ndef json_field_equals(field: str) -> Scorer:            # deterministic family\n    def fn(item, out):\n        try:\n            return float(json.loads(out).get(field) == item.expected), \"\"\n        except json.JSONDecodeError:\n            return 0.0, \"invalid JSON\"\n    return Scorer(f\"json_eq:{field}\", \"1\", fn)\n\ndef rubric_judge(judge: Target, rubric_version: str) -> Scorer:   # model-graded family\n    def fn(item, out):\n        v = json.loads(judge({\"input\": item.input, \"output\": out, \"reference\": item.expected}))\n        return float(v[\"score\"]), v[\"reason\"]          # judge prompt asks for {\"score\": 0..1, \"reason\": \"...\"}\n    return Scorer(\"judge\", rubric_version, fn)\n\ndef run(dataset_version: str, items: list[Item], target: Target, scorers: list[Scorer], bundle: dict) -> dict:\n    h = lambda o: hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(o, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()[:12]\n    gen_id = h({\"dataset\": dataset_version, \"bundle\": bundle})          # what determines the outputs\n    run_id = h({\"gen\": gen_id, \"scorers\": [f\"{s.name}@{s.version}\" for s in scorers]})\n    path = pathlib.Path(f\"outputs/{gen_id}.jsonl\"); path.parent.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    done = {r[\"id\"]: r[\"output\"] for r in map(json.loads, path.open())} if path.exists() else {}\n    rows = []\n    with path.open(\"a\") as raw:\n        for it in items:\n            if it.id not in done:                       # a new judge reuses these: re-scoring costs judge calls only\n                done[it.id] = target(it.input)\n                raw.write(json.dumps({\"id\": it.id, \"output\": done[it.id]}) + \"\\n\"); raw.flush()\n            out = done[it.id]                           # persisted before any scorer runs\n            scores = [dict(zip((\"value\", \"rationale\"), s.fn(it, out)), scorer=f\"{s.name}@{s.version}\")\n                      for s in scorers]                 # one call per scorer: a judge call costs money\n            rows.append({\"item\": asdict(it), \"output\": out, \"scores\": scores})\n    return {\"run_id\": run_id, \"gen_id\": gen_id, \"started\": time.time(), \"rows\": rows}\n```\n\n### Using it: a judge upgrade re-scores without re-running the app\nContinues the skeleton above with stand-in target and judge functions that count their calls. Swapping the\nrubric version produces a new run id over the same stored outputs.\n\n```python\ncalls = {\"target\": 0, \"judge\": 0}\n\ndef app(inp):                                   # stand-in for the production entrypoint\n    calls[\"target\"] += 1\n    return json.dumps({\"intent\": \"refund\" if \"money back\" in inp[\"text\"] else \"other\"})\n\ndef judge(req):                                 # stand-in for a pinned judge model\n    calls[\"judge\"] += 1\n    return json.dumps({\"score\": 1.0, \"reason\": \"matches reference\"})\n\nitems = [Item(\"t1\", {\"text\": \"I want my money back\"}, \"refund\", \"refunds\"),\n         Item(\"t2\", {\"text\": \"Where is my parcel\"}, \"other\", \"shipping\")]\nbundle = {\"model\": \"vendor-model-2025-04-14\", \"prompt\": \"intent@v7\", \"params\": {\"temperature\": 0}}\n\nr1 = run(\"golden-v8@5c2e91\", items, app, [json_field_equals(\"intent\"), rubric_judge(judge, \"rubric-v3\")], bundle)\nr2 = run(\"golden-v8@5c2e91\", items, app, [json_field_equals(\"intent\"), rubric_judge(judge, \"rubric-v4\")], bundle)\nprint(r1[\"gen_id\"] == r2[\"gen_id\"], r1[\"run_id\"] != r2[\"run_id\"])   # True True: same outputs, new run\nprint(calls)                                                         # {'target': 2, 'judge': 4}\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what each change costs on a 1,000-item set\nCall counts follow from the generation id and run id above; they are not a benchmark.\n\n| Change | Generation id | Target calls | Judge calls | Comparable with the old champion run? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| New rubric version | same | 0 | 1,000 | only after re-scoring the champion with the new rubric |\n| New prompt version | new | 1,000 | 1,000 | yes, same dataset and scorer versions |\n| Run interrupted at item 600, then resumed | same | 400 on resume | 1,000 (scores are recomputed) | yes |\n| Dataset relabeled in place (no new version) | same, wrongly | 0 | 1,000 | no, and nothing warns you: why datasets must be immutable |","patterns":["**One scorer library, versioned like code** — when several teams evaluate. Publish scorers as a package with explicit versions and record `scorer@version` on every score. Monitor how many teams' runs use an out-of-date scorer version.","**Judge calibration set** — whenever a model-graded scorer gates anything. Keep a few hundred human-labeled items, measure judge-to-human agreement on every judge or rubric change, and refuse to promote a judge that falls below the agreed level. Monitor agreement over time.","**Promptfoo in CI for prompt-level checks** — when the task is a prompt over common providers. Keep a `promptfooconfig.yaml` of prompts, providers and tests with assertions such as `is-json`, `contains` or `llm-rubric`, and run `npx promptfoo eval` in the PR job. Monitor pass rate per assertion type.","**Same scorers online** — when offline and online numbers disagree. Run the framework's scorers on a sample of production traces in the observability platform and chart them next to the offline score. Monitor the offline-online gap per slice.","**Trace-to-dataset loop** — when incidents keep surprising you. Make \"add this trace to the candidate set\" one action in the platform, with review before it enters a golden version. Monitor the fraction of incidents that already had a dataset item."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[(\"Dataset version, hashed\")] --> RN[\"Runner: pins, cache, retries\"]\n    B[\"Pinned bundle\"] --> TG[\"Target: production entrypoint\"]\n    RN --> TG\n    TG --> RO[(\"Raw outputs, keyed by generation id\")]\n    RO --> SC[\"Versioned scorers\"]\n    SC --> RS[(\"Results store: per-item scores\")]\n    RS --> CMP[\"Item diff and slice CIs\"]\n    CMP --> G[\"CI gate decides\"]\n    PT[\"Sampled production traces\"] --> SC\n    PT -.->|\"failures become items\"| D","caption":"Raw outputs are stored before scoring, so a scorer change re-scores stored outputs instead of re-running\nthe target; the same scorers also run on production traces."},"interview":[{"q":"What are the core interfaces of an evaluation framework, and why standardise them?","a":"- **Dataset, target, scorers, runner, results store,** each with a fixed schema and versions.\n- **Why:** with one schema, runs compare across teams and weeks, components such as a judge become\n  swappable, caching becomes possible, and every score links back to its trace.\n- **The differentiating assets** are your dataset and scorers, not the runner."},{"q":"Your eval says a change helped, but production complaints rose. How do you debug the framework?","a":"- **Check the target first:** does the adapter call the production entrypoint, or a copy of the prompt that\n  skips retrieval and post-processing?\n- **Check the scorer:** is the judge calibrated against human labels, or rewarding longer answers?\n- **Check coverage:** run the same scorers on sampled production traces and compare slices; the failing\n  cases may not exist in the dataset."},{"q":"When would you not adopt an off-the-shelf eval framework?","a":"- **When the target does not fit its data model,** such as long multi-turn agents with tool side effects or\n  simulated users.\n- **When residency forbids a SaaS store** or scorers must run inside your network.\n- **Even then** keep the five interfaces and reuse an open-source results store rather than invent a\n  format."},{"q":"You upgrade the judge rubric on a 1,000-item set. What should it cost, and what must you re-run?","a":"- **Cost:** 1,000 judge calls and zero target calls, because raw outputs were stored before scoring under a\n  generation id that excludes scorer versions.\n- **Re-run:** re-score the champion under the new rubric too, because scores across scorer versions are not\n  comparable.\n- **Watch for** repeats: a noise measurement needs a repeat index in the generation id, or it replays one\n  sample."},{"q":"Which category of framework do you use to pick a base model versus a prompt change?","a":"- **Base model:** a capability harness such as lm-evaluation-harness or HELM narrows candidates, then your\n  golden set decides.\n- **Prompt or pipeline change:** an application framework running your dataset through your pipeline.\n- **Closing the loop:** a platform-integrated store that turns production traces into dataset items."}],"tradeoffs":["**Comparability vs fit.** One framework across teams makes numbers comparable but forces every task into its data model. It flips when a team's target (a long tool-using agent, a simulated user) does not fit and they fork.","**Coverage vs cost and drift.** Model-graded scorers cover free text that deterministic checks cannot, at the price of a judge call per item and a second model's drift. Prefer deterministic scorers whenever the output is structured.","**SaaS convenience vs data boundary.** SaaS platforms give datasets, traces and experiments in one place with no operations burden, but production traces (customer data) leave your boundary. Self-host or build when residency or PII rules apply.","**Per-PR feedback vs cache effectiveness.** Running the eval on every PR gives fast feedback, and the cache keeps it cheap only while most items are unaffected. A prompt touched by every item invalidates the cache, which is why tiered datasets exist."],"pitfalls":["**Evaluating a copy of the prompt inside the framework.** Instead of the production code path. Symptom: a green eval followed by a production regression caused by template or post-processing differences.","**Using an uncalibrated judge scorer.** Symptom: \"improvements\" that turn out to be longer answers, or scores that jump when the judge model is upgraded. Fix: calibrate against human labels and pin it.","**Storing aggregates only.** Symptom: a two-point drop that nobody can explain because there are no raw outputs to diff against the champion.","**Letting the dataset schema drift.** Teams add fields ad hoc. Symptom: two runs \"on the same dataset\" with different item counts, which the comparison tool should refuse to compare.","**Reading a capability benchmark as application quality.** Symptom: the model with the higher public score loses on your golden set because your task depends on retrieval and formatting, not trivia."],"when":"- **Use when** a second person needs to run or read an eval, or the moment an eval gates anything.\n- **Pick the category by the question:** base-model choice means a capability harness; a prompt or\n  pipeline change means an application framework over your golden set; closing the loop with production\n  means a platform-integrated store.\n- **Measure the framework itself:** time from PR to eval result, cost per run and cache hit rate,\n  judge-to-human agreement, and the fraction of production incidents that already had a dataset item.\n- **Interviews:** draw the five interfaces and explain why raw outputs are stored before scoring and why a\n  scorer carries a version.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the target adapter against the production entrypoint and the custom scorers, and wires the framework's CLI into the PR pipeline with content-addressed caching.","architect":"Decides which category of framework the organisation standardises on, where results and traces are stored given data-residency rules, and which interfaces every team must conform to.","fde":"Stands up the framework inside a customer's environment with their golden set, so acceptance is argued from item-level diffs rather than from demos of hand-picked prompts."},"check":{"q":"Four product teams each have their own eval script, and leadership wants a company-wide decision on\nmoving to a new model snapshot next month. What do you build, and what do you refuse to build?","a":"Build the shared interfaces, not a new harness per team: a dataset schema with content-hashed\nversions, a target adapter contract that calls each team's production entrypoint with a pinned bundle,\na scorer library with names and versions (deterministic where outputs are structured, a calibrated judge\nelsewhere), one runner that stores raw outputs and a manifest, and a results store that diffs runs item by\nitem. Then run champion and candidate snapshots through every team's golden set on identical items and\nreport per-slice deltas with confidence intervals. Refuse to decide from public benchmark scores, from\naggregate numbers computed by four different rubrics, or from runs whose judge versions differ."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a well-designed framework persist raw outputs before any scoring happens?","options":["Because scoring is too slow to run inline with generation","Because scorers change and re-scoring stored outputs avoids paying for the target's model calls again","Because raw outputs are needed to compute the cache key","Because judges cannot score streamed responses"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Scorers are versioned and get replaced; with raw outputs stored, a new judge or rubric re-scores at judge cost only, and reviewers can diff candidate against champion. The cache key is built from the input and pins, not from the output."},{"q":"Which framework category answers \"should our RAG support bot move from model X to model Y\"?","options":["A capability benchmark harness such as lm-evaluation-harness or HELM","An application eval framework running your golden set through your pipeline with your scorers","A public leaderboard comparing the two models' chat ratings","The provider's own published evaluation of model Y"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The decision depends on how the model behaves inside your prompt, retrieval and formatting; only your dataset and scorers measure that. Benchmarks and leaderboards measure the base model on other tasks."},{"q":"All scores rise five points overnight with no change to code, prompts, model or dataset version. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The cache served stale outputs from a previous run","The golden set gained easier items","The judge scorer's model or rubric changed, which is why scorers carry a version","Production traffic shifted toward easier queries"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A model-graded scorer is itself a model; if its snapshot or rubric moves, every candidate and champion shifts together. Pinning the judge version and re-baselining the champion under the same scorer is the defence; a cache would reproduce old scores, not new ones, and an unchanged dataset version rules out new items."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/golden-dataset-strategy","sources":["Gebru et al., 2021 — Datasheets for Datasets","Ribeiro et al., 2020 — Beyond Accuracy: Behavioral Testing of NLP Models with CheckList","Breck et al., 2017 — The ML Test Score: A Rubric for ML Production Readiness and Technical Debt Reduction"],"why":"- **The single file fails four ways.** Most teams start with fifty examples the first engineer wrote in an\n  afternoon (the `evals/golden-dataset` lesson covers what an item is and how it is labeled). It works for\n  a quarter, then goes stale, leaks, is mis-sized and has no owner.\n- **Stale and leaking.** The product adds a refunds flow and a second language, traffic shifts, and the set\n  still measures last quarter, so the eval stays green while users complain. Someone copies the best\n  examples into the few-shot prompt, then into fine-tuning data; the score climbs toward 100% and stops\n  carrying information.\n- **Wrong size and no owner.** Fifty items cannot separate a two-point regression from noise, so engineers\n  ignore the wobble; five thousand items cost real money and an hour per run, so it is skipped on busy\n  days, which are the days it matters. Nobody may fix a wrong label, or everyone edits and the baseline\n  moves silently.\n- **The naive fixes do not solve it.** \"Just use production logs\" gives inputs without agreed outputs,\n  skewed to the common case, with customer PII inside. \"Use a public benchmark\" measures the model, not\n  your prompt, retrieval and tools. \"Make it bigger\" trades noise for cost without fixing staleness or\n  leakage.\n- **The strategy treats golden data as a managed platform asset** with tiers, versions, owners and a refresh\n  loop, the way a mature team treats its test suite rather than a folder of scripts.","how":"### A portfolio, not a file\nEach tier has a purpose, a size and a cadence; tiers exist *because* cost multiplied by run frequency must\nstay affordable while the release gate stays powerful. Sizes are illustrative.\n\n| Tier | Size | Runs | Job |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Smoke** | tens to a couple of hundred, stratified | every PR | catch catastrophic breakage (broken template, parser that no longer matches), not two-point drifts |\n| **Full** | hundreds to low thousands, per-slice quotas | merge, nightly, release candidates | the set gates are computed on |\n| **Slice sets** | small, per intent, language, tenant or document type | with the full set | each carries its own floor because the aggregate hides a collapsed slice |\n| **Regression** | one item per fixed production incident, never pruned | every run | expected at 100% pass; \"every bug becomes a test\" is the cheapest coverage you will ever buy |\n| **Adversarial and safety** | prompt injection, jailbreak attempts, PII extraction | release and nightly | gated by absolute floors, not relative to the champion |\n| **Sealed holdout** | a subset prompt authors never see, never in few-shot or training data | release only | detects overfitting to the development set |\n\n- **The overfitting signal** is the gap between full-set and holdout scores.\n\n### The statistics that set the sizes\n```\nstandard error of one pass-rate run = √(p(1−p)/n)\n95% half-width = 1.96 × √(p(1−p)/n)\n```\n\n| n (at p = 0.8) | 95% half-width |\n|---|---|\n| 50 | about ±11 points |\n| 200 | ±5.5 |\n| 1,000 | ±2.5 |\n\n- **So a smoke set can only detect large breaks,** and anyone claiming a two-point improvement on a hundred\n  items is reading noise.\n- **Pairing tightens this considerably.** When candidate and champion run on the *identical* items, item\n  difficulty cancels in the per-item difference, which is why both must run on the same dataset version\n  (the `evaluation-gates-for-production-releases` lesson does the arithmetic).\n- **Size each slice** from the smallest regression you must detect there, not from a round number.\n\n### The sizing rule\nFor a target 95% half-width *h* on the candidate-minus-champion difference:\n\n```\nunpaired:  n ≈ 1.96² · 2p(1−p) / h²\npaired:    n ≈ 1.96² · f / h²      (f = fraction of items whose verdict differs between the two bundles)\n```\n\n- **At p = 0.8 and h = 2 points:** unpaired needs about 3,070 items; paired with f = 10% needs about 960.\n- **Detecting, not just bounding, costs more:** roughly double the items for 80% power.\n- **f is unknown** until you have run the champion against a few candidates, so measure it and resize.\n\n### The sourcing pipeline\n| Source | How | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Production traces** | sampled with per-slice quotas, stratified by outcome (thumbs-down, escalation, abandoned session), not uniformly | uniform sampling drowns rare intents in the head of the distribution |\n| **Failure mining** | error analysis of incidents | real failures are the highest-value items |\n| **Synthetic generation** | for slices with too little real traffic, admitted only after a human labels and filters them | a generator shares the model's blind spots and writes easier cases than users do |\n| **Expert-written** | for policy-critical behaviour | traffic rarely exercises it |\n\n- **What \"expected\" stores depends on the task:** a label for classification, executable tests for code,\n  and for free text usually a short list of must-include facts and must-not behaviours rather than one\n  reference phrasing.\n- **Criteria beat one reference phrasing** *because* a judge grading against criteria penalises legitimate\n  rewording less, and a policy change then edits a named criterion instead of a paragraph nobody can diff.\n\n### Admission workflow\n1. **Proposed** from one of the sources above.\n2. **Labeled** by the task owner.\n3. **Checked by a second reviewer.** Measure inter-annotator agreement; a slice with persistent\n   disagreement has an under-specified task, not bad reviewers.\n4. **Merged into the *next* version,** never the current one.\n\n- **PII is scrubbed or replaced** with synthetic values before storage, *because* a golden set lives for\n  years and gets copied to laptops.\n\n### Versioning and ownership\n- **A version is immutable,** identified by a content hash, with a changelog of items added, removed or\n  relabeled and why.\n- **Relabeling is a version bump, never an edit,** *because* a baseline recorded against version A is\n  meaningless once A's labels change.\n- **Correctness is owned by the task-definition role** (product or domain); engineering owns the pipeline;\n  the person who wrote the prompt under test does not label the holdout.\n- **A datasheet** (purpose, sources, slices, known gaps) travels with each set so a new team can judge what\n  a score on it means.\n\n### Lifecycle\n- **Refresh is triggered, not calendar-driven:**\n  - when production slice mix shifts (monitoring tells you);\n  - when a policy changes an expected answer;\n  - when a slice has sat at 100% for every candidate for months (move those items to smoke or regression;\n    they no longer discriminate);\n  - after every incident.\n- **Contamination defence is mechanical.** The registry checks that no golden input's hash or\n  near-duplicate appears in prompt templates, few-shot banks or fine-tuning corpora, and the sealed holdout\n  is excluded from any export engineers can read.","example":"### Sizing, quota sampling and cutting a version\nA support assistant with six intents whose production traffic splits roughly 60/20/10/5/3/2 percent.\n\n| Sampling | Items per intent | Total | Rarest intent |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Uniform, 300 traces | about 180/60/30/15/9/6 | 300 | six items: cannot be measured at all |\n| Quota of 40 per slice from the full trace pool | 40 each | 240 | measurable to roughly ±12 points at p = 0.8 |\n\n- **Report the aggregate both ways:** traffic-weighted and as per-slice floors.\n- **The code** shows the size arithmetic, the quota sampler and how a version is cut.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, math, random\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef half_width_points(p, n):            # 95% half-width in percentage points for a pass-rate metric\n    return 100 * 1.96 * math.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n)\n\nfor n in (40, 50, 200, 1000):\n    print(f\"n={n:5d}: ±{half_width_points(0.8, n):.1f} points\")   # ±12.4, ±11.1, ±5.5, ±2.5\n\ndef items_for_paired_gate(h, flip_rate):  # n so the paired 95% half-width on the delta is h (fractions)\n    return math.ceil(1.96**2 * flip_rate / h**2)          # items_for_paired_gate(0.02, 0.10) -> 961\n\ndef stratified_sample(candidates, quota_per_slice, seed=7):\n    \"\"\"candidates: labeled production traces; a fixed quota per slice keeps rare intents measurable.\"\"\"\n    by_slice = defaultdict(list)\n    for c in candidates:\n        by_slice[c[\"slice\"]].append(c)\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    chosen = []\n    for slice_name, items in sorted(by_slice.items()):\n        rng.shuffle(items)\n        chosen += items[:quota_per_slice]           # a slice with fewer than quota items is a coverage gap to log\n    return chosen\n\ndef cut_version(items, parent, changelog, tier=\"full\", sealed=False):\n    body = json.dumps(sorted(items, key=lambda i: i[\"id\"]), sort_keys=True).encode()\n    slices = {i[\"slice\"] for i in items}\n    return {\"version\": hashlib.sha256(body).hexdigest()[:12],   # content hash: an edit is a new version\n            \"parent\": parent, \"n\": len(items), \"tier\": tier, \"sealed\": sealed,\n            \"per_slice\": {s: sum(i[\"slice\"] == s for i in items) for s in sorted(slices)},\n            \"changelog\": changelog}                            # e.g. \"relabeled 15 refund items after policy change 2025-06\"\n```\n\n### A mechanical contamination check\nPure Python, runnable. Before a version is cut, compare golden inputs against prompt templates, few-shot\nbanks and fine-tuning corpora: exact match after normalisation, then word 8-gram overlap for near\nduplicates. The 0.5 threshold is a starting point to tune, not a standard.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, re\n\ndef norm(text):\n    return re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", text.lower()).strip()\n\ndef shingles(text, k=8):                     # word 8-grams catch light paraphrase and copy-paste\n    w = norm(text).split()\n    return {\" \".join(w[i:i + k]) for i in range(max(1, len(w) - k + 1))}\n\ndef contamination(golden_inputs, corpus_docs, threshold=0.5):\n    exact = {hashlib.sha256(norm(d).encode()).hexdigest() for d in corpus_docs}\n    corpus_sh = [shingles(d) for d in corpus_docs]\n    hits = []\n    for gid, text in golden_inputs.items():\n        if hashlib.sha256(norm(text).encode()).hexdigest() in exact:\n            hits.append((gid, \"exact\")); continue\n        s = shingles(text)\n        best = max((len(s & c) / len(s) for c in corpus_sh), default=0.0)\n        if best >= threshold:                # share of the item's 8-grams found in one corpus doc\n            hits.append((gid, f\"near-duplicate {best:.0%}\"))\n    return hits\n\ngolden = {\"g17\": \"I was charged twice for my annual plan and want one of the payments refunded please\",\n          \"g18\": \"How do I change the billing email on my account\"}\nfew_shot_bank = [\"Example user: I was charged twice for my annual plan and want one of the payments refunded\",\n                 \"Example user: my parcel never arrived\"]\nprint(contamination(golden, few_shot_bank))   # [('g17', 'near-duplicate 89%')]\n```\n\n- **On a hit,** remove the item from the bank or the corpus, or retire it from the golden set into a new\n  version; never leave both in place.","patterns":["**Regression item in every incident fix** — for any team with on-call. Make \"added regression item\" a required checkbox in the bug-fix PR template, and fail the eval if the regression tier drops below 100%. Monitor the fraction of incidents with a matching regression item.","**Outcome-stratified trace sampling** — when refreshing the full set. Pull a fixed quota per slice, weighted toward thumbs-down, escalated and abandoned sessions, through a PII scrubber before labeling. Monitor items per slice against the quota and the coverage gaps logged by the sampler.","**Contamination check as a CI step** — whenever prompts, few-shot banks or training corpora change. Run the hash and n-gram check above against the current golden versions and block the merge on a hit. Monitor hits per month and the gap between full-set and holdout scores.","**Holdout behind access control** — when prompt authors iterate fast. Store the sealed holdout in a separate bucket or project only the release job's service account can read, and exclude it from exports. Monitor access logs and the full-versus-holdout gap at each release.","**Measured flip rate drives size** — once you have run a few candidates. Record the fraction of items whose verdict differs between candidate and champion, and recompute paired sizes per slice from it. Monitor confidence-interval width per slice against the regression size you must detect."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    PT[\"Production traces, quota sampled\"] --> AD[\"Admission: label, second review\"]\n    FM[\"Failure mining\"] --> AD\n    SY[\"Synthetic, human filtered\"] --> AD\n    EX[\"Expert-written\"] --> AD\n    AD -->|\"PII scrubbed\"| CC[\"Contamination check\"]\n    CC --> V[(\"Next version, content hash\")]\n    V --> SM[\"Smoke tier\"]\n    V --> FU[\"Full and slice tiers\"]\n    V --> HO[\"Sealed holdout\"]\n    FU -->|\"incident fixed\"| RG[\"Regression tier\"]\n    RG -.->|\"every bug becomes a test\"| AD","caption":"Items flow from four sources through admission into the next immutable version; tiers are cut from it,\nand incidents feed back as regression items."},"interview":[{"q":"Why split golden data into tiers instead of one big set?","a":"- **Cost times frequency.** A per-PR run must be cheap, so the smoke tier is small; the gate needs power, so\n  the full tier is large and runs less often.\n- **Different jobs need different rules:** the regression tier must pass 100%, safety has absolute floors,\n  and the sealed holdout exists only to detect overfitting.\n- **Slices carry their own floors** because an aggregate hides a collapsed slice."},{"q":"How many items do you need to detect a two-point regression at an 80% pass rate?","a":"- **Unpaired:** about 1.96² × 2 × 0.8 × 0.2 / 0.02², roughly 3,070 items just to bound the difference to ±2\n  points.\n- **Paired on identical items:** about 1.96² × f / 0.02²; with 10% of verdicts flipping, about 960.\n- **Reliable detection** at 80% power needs roughly double; measure the flip rate and resize per slice."},{"q":"Your golden-set score rose to 98% but production CSAT is flat. How do you investigate?","a":"- **Suspect leakage first:** run a contamination check of golden inputs against prompts, few-shot banks and\n  fine-tuning corpora.\n- **Compare with the sealed holdout:** a large full-versus-holdout gap means overfitting to the development\n  set.\n- **Check staleness:** compare slice mix with current traffic; new flows may have no items."},{"q":"When would you not keep a sealed holdout?","a":"- **When the team cannot afford two curated sets;** you cannot error-analyse what nobody may read.\n- **Instead,** rotate items into a holdout periodically, or keep a small sealed slice for releases only.\n- **The cost of skipping it** is that development-set scores drift upward from overfitting with nothing to\n  reveal it."},{"q":"Who should be allowed to change an expected answer, and how?","a":"- **The task owner** (product or domain) decides correctness; engineering owns the pipeline; the prompt\n  author does not label the holdout.\n- **Every change is a new version** with a changelog and a second reviewer, never an in-place edit.\n- **Then re-baseline the champion** on the new version so comparisons stay valid."}],"tradeoffs":["**Measurable rare slices vs a traffic-faithful aggregate.** Per-slice quotas make rare cases measurable, but the aggregate no longer reflects traffic. Report both a traffic-weighted score and per-slice floors, and drop the quota only for slices nobody cares about.","**Overfitting detection vs analysability.** A sealed holdout detects overfitting to the development set, but you cannot error-analyse what you are not allowed to read. A small team without capacity for two sets should rotate items into the holdout instead.","**Fresh failures vs a pessimistic set.** Mining failures keeps the set current, but a set made only of past failures makes every candidate look worse than traffic would; keep a randomly sampled, stratified core alongside it.","**Cheap synthetic coverage vs realism.** Synthetic items cover rare slices cheaply but share the generator's blind spots and run easier than real users. The trade flips in their favour only once humans have labeled and filtered them.","**Tight intervals vs cost per run.** Larger sets tighten confidence intervals and raise cost per run; tiering and content-addressed caching reconcile the two, and without caching a large full set gets skipped."],"pitfalls":["**Golden items copied into few-shot prompts or fine-tuning data.** Symptom: a development-set score near 100% while the sealed holdout and production CSAT do not move.","**Editing expected outputs in place.** Symptom: a champion baseline that shifts without any deploy, so last month's runs are no longer comparable.","**Uniform sampling from logs.** Symptom: a flat aggregate while a rare but high-value intent regresses all the way to production.","**A museum of past failures.** Symptom: every candidate \"regressing\" ten points relative to what users actually experience.","**Nobody owning correctness.** Symptom: label disputes settled by whoever ran the eval, and an inter-annotator agreement that has never been measured.","**Items stale after a policy change.** Symptom: the gate blocks a legitimately correct new behaviour, engineers start overriding it, and it stops meaning anything."],"when":"- **Use when** an eval gates anything or more than one person edits the set.\n- **Before that,** one stratified file with a content hash is enough.\n- **Measure the asset itself:** coverage and confidence-interval width per slice, median item age, the\n  fraction of production incidents with a regression item, agreement on relabels, and cost per run per tier.\n- **Day to day:** every bug-fix PR adds a regression item, and every product change triggers a version bump\n  with a changelog.\n- **Interviews:** expect how big a set must be to detect a given regression, how you keep it out of the\n  prompt, and who is allowed to change a label.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the quota sampler and the contamination check, adds a regression item with every incident fix, and cuts content-hashed versions rather than editing files in place.","architect":"Defines the tiers, the ownership model and the holdout policy, and decides which slices carry floors in the release gate and what cost per run the platform will fund.","fde":"Builds the customer's first golden set from their real tickets with their domain experts labeling, because acceptance criteria argued from the customer's own data survive procurement reviews."},"check":{"q":"A team's 120-item golden set has read 94% for six months while production satisfaction has fallen.\nWhat has most likely gone wrong, and what is your plan for the next two sprints?","a":"Three candidates, checked in order. Leakage: grep prompts, few-shot banks and fine-tuning data for\ngolden inputs; if found, the 94% measures memorisation. Staleness: compare the set's slice mix with current\ntraffic and the product's recent changes; a set built before the refunds flow cannot see refunds failing.\nSize: at n = 120 and p ≈ 0.94 the 95% half-width is roughly ±4 points (it would be about ±7 near p = 0.8),\nand each slice holds only a fraction of the 120 items, so a real drop on one slice can hide in the noise. Plan: cut a new version\nwith per-slice quotas sampled from recent traces stratified by negative outcomes, add regression items\nfrom the complaints, carve out a sealed holdout the prompt authors never see, record the new baseline\nfor the champion on that version, and put slice floors into the gate."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why keep a sealed holdout that prompt authors are never allowed to read?","options":["To reduce the cost of the per-PR smoke run","To detect overfitting of prompts and few-shot examples to the development set","To satisfy data-residency rules for customer traces","To measure inter-annotator agreement on hard items"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Anything authors can read eventually shapes the prompt, so development-set scores drift upward without real improvement. A gap between full-set and holdout scores is the overfitting signal; cost and residency are solved by other mechanisms."},{"q":"On a 100-item set a candidate scores 84% against the champion's 82%. What should you conclude?","options":["The candidate is better and can be promoted","The candidate is worse because two points is within rounding","Nothing yet: at n = 100 the noise is several points, so run a paired comparison on a larger set","The golden set is contaminated"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With p around 0.8 and n = 100 the 95% half-width is roughly ±8 points, far wider than the observed difference. A paired run on the same, larger dataset version is what can distinguish signal from noise."},{"q":"A policy change makes 15 expected answers wrong. What is the correct way to update the golden set?","options":["Edit the 15 expected outputs in place so the next run is correct","Delete the 15 items and shrink the set","Cut a new version with the relabels in its changelog and re-baseline the champion on that version","Move the 15 items into the sealed holdout"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Baselines are recorded against a dataset version; editing in place silently invalidates every earlier comparison. A new content-hashed version with a changelog keeps history comparable, and re-running the champion gives the gate a valid reference."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/reproducible-evaluations","sources":["Pineau et al., 2021 — Improving Reproducibility in Machine Learning Research","Chen, Zaharia & Zou, 2023 — How Is ChatGPT's Behavior Changing over Time?","Sculley et al., 2015 — Hidden Technical Debt in Machine Learning Systems"],"why":"- **Almost nothing holds still by default.** \"Did the prompt change help?\" needs everything else fixed. The\n  provider updates the model behind an alias such as `-latest`, and published studies have documented large\n  behaviour shifts between dated versions of the same hosted model.\n- **Sampling is noisy even at temperature zero.** Above zero, every run differs. At zero, outputs are\n  generally still not bit-identical across calls, because batching and floating-point reduction order on\n  GPUs are not deterministic. The judge is a model and drifts for the same reasons.\n- **Everything else moves too.** The dataset is edited in place, the index re-embedded, the prompt contains\n  today's date or a live tool result, a dependency bump changes a tokenizer or SDK default, and a warm cache\n  serves last month's outputs for a model that has since changed.\n- **Naive fixes cover one source each.** \"Temperature 0 and a seed\" addresses sampling only, and many APIs\n  treat the seed as best-effort. \"Compare with last week\" compares two systems. \"Run it twice and see\" is the\n  right instinct without the bookkeeping to act on it.\n- **The cost is real.** Teams ship a prompt that \"improved three points\" when the provider changed the model,\n  or roll back a real improvement because the judge was upgraded the same day. Regulated customers want\n  evidence, and \"we ran it and it looked fine\" is not evidence. So pin what can be pinned, measure the noise\n  in what cannot, and record both.","how":"### Three levels, and which one you actually need\n| Level | Means | Achievable when | Required for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Bit-exact** | identical outputs | locally served open-weight models at temperature zero with fixed batching and deterministic kernels (at some throughput cost); rarely through a hosted API | debugging, special cases |\n| **Statistical** | same score distribution within a measured noise band | always, with repeats | every decision eval |\n| **Re-derivable** | anyone can reconstruct the run from its manifest, even if the provider has since changed | always, with a manifest | the minimum bar for any eval cited in a release |\n\n### The eval lockfile\nEvery run records a manifest; each field is there *because* a specific drift has burned someone.\n\n| Field | Contents | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Dataset** | the content hash of the items, not a file name | files get edited in place |\n| **Bundle** | model snapshot id (or weights digest), prompt versions, decoding parameters, seed where supported, tool and retrieval index snapshots | a dated snapshot, not an alias: the alias is the single most common cause of unexplained score changes |\n| **Scorers** | judge model snapshot, judge prompt and rubric version, scorer code version | the judge is a model |\n| **Environment** | git SHA, dependency lockfile hash, container image digest | tokenizers, SDK defaults and retry behaviour live in dependencies |\n| **Runtime inputs** | anything read at request time outside the bundle: the current date, a live tool or API result, a feature-flag value | frozen by injecting a fixed clock and replaying recorded tool responses; the recording's hash goes in the manifest |\n| **Run policy** | concurrency, timeout and retry settings, cache mode | timeouts recorded as zero scores make a provider incident look like a regression; a cache that ignores a pin hides a change |\n\n- **Live tools break reproducibility:** an agent eval that calls a live search API is not reproducible next\n  week whatever else you pin.\n- **The run id is a hash of the manifest,** so two runs with identical pins are recognisably the same\n  experiment.\n- **Store the manifest next to the raw outputs** in the experiment-tracking or evaluation store.\n\n### Measure the noise you cannot pin\n1. **Run the *same* bundle several times** (three to five repeats is a common illustrative choice) and compute\n   the spread of the aggregate. That spread is the noise floor; no delta smaller than it may be believed.\n2. **Report every metric as a mean with a confidence interval.**\n3. **Compare candidate and champion on identical items** so item difficulty cancels.\n4. **Evaluate at the temperature production uses,** not forced zero, *because* an eval at temperature zero of\n   a system that ships at 0.7 measures a different system. Repeats, not determinism, tame the variance.\n\n- **When one run suffices:** deterministic scorers on structured outputs from a locally pinned model can show\n  a noise floor near zero. Measure it before assuming it.\n\n### Two kinds of noise, two different tools\n| Noise | Source | Remedy | Measured by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Generation noise** | run-to-run variation on the same items: sampling, non-deterministic serving, a model-graded scorer | repeats; averaging each item's score over its repeats shrinks it | spread across repeats |\n| **Item-sampling noise** | having evaluated these few hundred items rather than the whole population of requests | only more items; repeats do nothing | confidence interval over items (bootstrap over per-item scores) |\n\n- **A delta must clear both.** A perfectly deterministic run on 100 items still has an item-level interval\n  of several points.\n- **Practical recipe:** average repeats per item first, then compute the paired interval over items.\n\n### Detect drift you cannot pin\n- **A dated snapshot is a promise you cannot verify from outside.**\n- **A sentinel run** re-executes a frozen reference bundle on a fixed subset on a schedule.\n- **If its score moves beyond the noise floor** while nothing in the manifest changed, something outside the\n  manifest did (the provider, the network, the environment), and the alert fires before a candidate is\n  blamed.\n- **The same sentinel catches a silently updated judge.**\n\n### Comparability as a rule, not a hope\n- **Two runs are comparable only if their dataset version and scorer versions match.** The tooling should\n  refuse to diff them otherwise, or label the comparison as cross-version.\n- **Re-scoring is cheap when raw outputs are stored:** a new judge version re-scores champion and candidate\n  from stored outputs without re-calling the target, which is how you re-baseline after a judge upgrade.","example":"### Manifest, noise floor and comparability check\nA manifest builder, a noise-floor measurement, and a comparability check. The run id is derived from the\nmanifest, so re-running with the same pins produces the same id; a changed pin produces a new one. The\nnoise floor is measured on the champion before any candidate is judged against it.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, statistics, subprocess, pathlib\n\ndef sha(path: str) -> str:\n    return hashlib.sha256(pathlib.Path(path).read_bytes()).hexdigest()[:12]\n\ndef build_manifest(bundle: dict, dataset_version: str, scorers: dict, image_digest: str) -> tuple[str, dict]:\n    git_sha = subprocess.check_output([\"git\", \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\"]).decode().strip()\n    m = {\"dataset\": dataset_version,                       # content hash of the items, never a file name\n         \"bundle\": bundle,                                  # {\"model\": \"vendor-model-2025-04-14\", \"prompt\": \"support@v12\",\n                                                            #  \"params\": {\"temperature\": 0.7, \"max_tokens\": 400}, \"index\": \"idx-2025-06-01\"}\n         \"scorers\": scorers,                                # {\"judge_model\": \"judge-2025-01-15\", \"rubric\": \"helpfulness@v3\"}\n         \"env\": {\"git\": git_sha, \"lock\": sha(\"uv.lock\"), \"image\": image_digest},\n         \"policy\": {\"concurrency\": 8, \"timeout_s\": 60, \"retries\": 2, \"cache\": \"read-write\"}}\n    run_id = hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(m, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()[:12]\n    return run_id, m\n\ndef noise_floor(run_once, k: int = 3) -> tuple[float, float]:\n    \"\"\"run_once() executes the SAME manifest with cache disabled and returns per-item scores.\n    The spread between repeats is the smallest delta anyone is allowed to believe.\"\"\"\n    means = [statistics.mean(run_once()) for _ in range(k)]\n    return statistics.mean(means), max(means) - min(means)\n\ndef comparable(a: dict, b: dict) -> tuple[bool, dict]:\n    checks = {k: a[k] == b[k] for k in (\"dataset\", \"scorers\")}\n    checks[\"same_model\"] = a[\"bundle\"][\"model\"] == b[\"bundle\"][\"model\"]   # false is fine for a model-upgrade eval, but say so\n    return all(checks[k] for k in (\"dataset\", \"scorers\")), checks\n\n# champion_mean, floor = noise_floor(lambda: run_eval(champion_manifest, cache=False))\n# delta = candidate_mean - champion_mean\n# if abs(delta) <= floor: print(\"within noise: not a result\")  # generation noise only; the paired interval\n#                                                               # over items (gate lesson) must also exclude it\n```\n\n### Repeats per item, then a paired interval over items\nPure Python, runnable, with synthetic scores. The candidate is truly 3 points better on every item, with\nthree repeats each on 100 identical items. Repeats shrink generation noise, but the interval over items\nstill includes zero: only more items can resolve a gain this small.\n\n```python\nimport random, statistics\n\ndef paired_interval(champ, cand, n_boot=2000, seed=0):\n    \"\"\"champ, cand: {item_id: [score per repeat]} on IDENTICAL items.\n    Average repeats per item first (shrinks generation noise), then bootstrap over items\n    (quantifies item-sampling noise, which repeats cannot touch).\"\"\"\n    ids = sorted(champ)\n    diffs = [statistics.mean(cand[i]) - statistics.mean(champ[i]) for i in ids]\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    boots = sorted(statistics.mean(rng.choices(diffs, k=len(diffs))) for _ in range(n_boot))\n    return statistics.mean(diffs), boots[int(0.025 * n_boot)], boots[int(0.975 * n_boot)]\n\nrng = random.Random(42)                       # synthetic pass/fail scores, 3 repeats per item\np_champ = {f\"i{k}\": rng.random() for k in range(100)}           # each item's true pass probability\np_cand = {k: min(1.0, p + 0.03) for k, p in p_champ.items()}    # candidate truly +3 points per item\ndraw = lambda p: [float(rng.random() < p) for _ in range(3)]\nchamp = {k: draw(p) for k, p in p_champ.items()}\ncand = {k: draw(p) for k, p in p_cand.items()}\n\ndelta, lo, hi = paired_interval(champ, cand)\nprint(f\"delta={delta:+.3f}  95% CI [{lo:+.3f}, {hi:+.3f}]\")   # an interval several points wide around +0.03\nprint(\"result\" if lo > 0 or hi < 0 else \"not yet a result: interval includes zero\")\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: Friday's \"three-point win\"\nA prompt change scores three points above a baseline measured three weeks earlier. Comparing the two\nmanifests field by field explains it before anyone re-runs anything.\n\n| Manifest field | Baseline (3 weeks ago) | Friday's run | Comparable? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Dataset hash | golden-v8 | golden-v9 (20 items added) | no: different yardstick |\n| Model | alias `-latest` | alias `-latest` | unknown: the alias may have moved |\n| Judge | rubric v3 | rubric v3, judge snapshot updated | no: the judge is a model |\n| Prompt | v11 | v12 | the change under test |\n\n- **Verdict:** three uncontrolled changes besides the prompt, so the three points belong to nobody yet.\n- **Fix:** pin a dated model snapshot, re-run champion and candidate now with identical manifests except the\n  prompt, cache off, repeats per item, paired interval over items.","patterns":["**Harness refuses incomplete manifests** — for any eval that gates or is cited. Fail the run at start if a dataset hash, model snapshot, judge snapshot, lockfile hash or image digest is missing, or if any model id matches an alias pattern such as `-latest`. Monitor the fraction of runs with a complete manifest.","**Scheduled sentinel with the cache off** — when you depend on hosted models. Run a frozen champion bundle on a fixed subset daily or weekly, cache disabled, and alert when its score leaves the measured noise band. Monitor sentinel drift events per quarter.","**Recorded tool responses for agent evals** — when the application calls live tools. Record tool responses once, replay them by request hash during evals, inject a fixed clock, and put the recording's hash in the manifest. Monitor replay misses, which mean the agent asked something new.","**Noise floor stored per metric** — before any gate trusts a delta. Re-measure the floor with repeats on the champion whenever the model, judge or dataset version changes, and store it with the baseline. Monitor floor width over time; a widening floor often means a provider-side change.","**Retained evidence with the promoted bundle** — for regulated or customer-facing commitments. Store manifest, raw outputs, scores and approvals together and keep them for the retention period your policy sets. Monitor that every promoted bundle has a retrievable evidence record."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    M[\"Manifest: dataset, bundle, scorers, env, runtime inputs, policy\"] --> ID[\"Run id = hash of manifest\"]\n    ID --> RUN[\"Run k repeats, cache off for floor\"]\n    RUN --> RO[(\"Raw outputs + manifest stored\")]\n    RO --> SC[\"Score with pinned scorers\"]\n    SC --> CMP{\"Same dataset and scorer versions?\"}\n    CMP -->|\"no\"| REF[\"Refuse or label cross-version\"]\n    CMP -->|\"yes\"| PI[\"Paired interval over items vs noise floor\"]\n    SN[\"Scheduled sentinel: frozen bundle\"] --> DR{\"Moved beyond floor?\"}\n    DR -->|\"yes, manifest unchanged\"| AL[\"Alert: drift outside manifest\"]","caption":"Every run is identified by its manifest; comparisons are refused across yardsticks, and a cache-free\nsentinel watches for drift the manifest cannot pin."},"interview":[{"q":"What does \"reproducible\" mean for an LLM eval if outputs are not deterministic?","a":"- **Statistical, not bit-exact:** re-running the same manifest gives the same score distribution within a\n  measured noise band, and the same conclusion.\n- **Re-derivable as the floor:** anyone can reconstruct the run from its manifest even after the provider\n  has changed.\n- **Bit-exact** is realistic only for locally served models with deterministic kernels and fixed batching."},{"q":"How would you know the model changed underneath you?","a":"- **A scheduled sentinel run:** a frozen reference bundle on a fixed subset, cache off, compared against its\n  own noise floor.\n- **If it moves while its manifest did not,** something outside the manifest changed: the provider, the\n  environment or a silently updated judge.\n- **The alert fires before** a candidate prompt gets blamed for the shift."},{"q":"Three repeats agree perfectly, yet the win vanished on the next dataset version. Why?","a":"- **Repeats measure generation noise only.** They say nothing about item-sampling noise from having\n  evaluated these items and not others.\n- **On 80 or 100 items** the interval over items is several points wide even for a deterministic run.\n- **Fix:** average repeats per item, then compute a paired bootstrap interval over items, and grow the set\n  if the interval is too wide."},{"q":"Should you evaluate at temperature zero to make results stable?","a":"- **Only if production runs at zero.** An eval at zero of a system that ships at 0.7 measures a different\n  system.\n- **Zero is not deterministic anyway** on hosted infrastructure, because batching and floating-point order\n  vary.\n- **Use repeats** at the production temperature and measure the noise floor instead."},{"q":"What goes in an eval manifest, and which field causes the most unexplained score changes?","a":"- **Fields:** dataset hash, bundle (model snapshot, prompt versions, decoding params, seed, index), scorers\n  (judge snapshot, rubric), environment (git SHA, lockfile, image digest), runtime inputs and run policy.\n- **Most common culprit:** a model alias instead of a dated snapshot.\n- **The run id is the manifest's hash,** so identical pins are recognisably the same experiment."}],"tradeoffs":["**Stable comparisons vs re-baselining.** Pinning dated snapshots gives stable comparisons, but every deprecation forces a re-baseline. When the provider deprecates faster than you re-baseline, a scheduled model-upgrade job with a full eval is the only sustainable answer.","**Quantified noise vs cost.** Repeated runs quantify noise at a multiple of the cost. Once a deterministic scorer on a locally pinned model shows a near-zero floor, a single run is enough, but only after the floor has been measured.","**Production temperature vs cleanliness.** Evaluating at production temperature measures what ships but is noisier; evaluating at zero is cleaner and may measure a system you do not ship. The choice follows what production actually does.","**Cheap cached re-runs vs hidden changes.** Content-addressed caching makes re-runs nearly free, yet a key that omits any pin hides a change. The sentinel run must bypass the cache, which is exactly the run you cannot make cheap."],"pitfalls":["**A model alias instead of a dated snapshot in the manifest.** Symptom: scores jumping on a day when nobody deployed anything.","**A silently upgraded judge.** Symptom: champion and candidate shifting together, which preserves the delta but breaks every absolute floor in the gate.","**Datasets referenced by file name.** Symptom: two runs \"on golden.jsonl\" disagreeing on the item count.","**Treating \"three identical repeats\" as proof.** Symptom: a deterministic eval on 80 items whose two-point win vanishes on the next dataset version, because repeats never measured item-sampling noise.","**Timeouts counted as zero scores.** Symptom: eval scores that correlate with the provider's status page.","**Assuming temperature zero means deterministic.** Symptom: a bug report that some outputs differ between identical runs (how many depends on the provider, output length and load), filed as a regression.","**Comparing runs scored by different judge versions.** Symptom: a \"regression\" that vanishes when both runs are re-scored from stored outputs with the same judge."],"when":"- **Always:** every eval that informs a decision gets a manifest; the rigour scales with the stakes.\n- **Development loop:** re-derivable is enough, and the framework captures the manifest automatically.\n- **Release gate:** statistical reproduction is required: repeats to establish the noise floor, identical\n  dataset and scorer versions, paired comparison.\n- **Regulated or customer-facing commitments:** store the full evidence (manifest, raw outputs, scores,\n  approvals) with the promoted bundle.\n- **Measure** the fraction of runs with a complete manifest, the noise floor per metric, sentinel drift events\n  per quarter, and how often the comparability check refuses a diff.\n- **Interviews:** the usual question is \"how would you know the model changed underneath you?\"; the sentinel\n  run is the answer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Makes the harness refuse to start without a complete manifest, pins judge and model snapshots, stores raw outputs, and schedules the sentinel run with caching disabled.","architect":"Sets the reproducibility level each stage requires, decides what evidence is retained for how long, and owns the policy that aliases never appear in a promoted bundle.","fde":"Hands a customer the manifest and raw outputs behind an acceptance number, so their team can re-run it in their own environment and get the same conclusion."},"check":{"q":"A colleague reports that their prompt change raised the judge score from 0.78 to 0.83 on Friday,\nagainst a baseline measured three weeks ago. What do you ask before believing it, and what do you run?","a":"Ask whether both runs used the same dataset version, the same model snapshot rather than an alias,\nthe same judge model and rubric version, the same decoding parameters and index, and whether the baseline's\nnoise floor was ever measured. Three weeks is long enough for the provider, the judge or the dataset to\nhave moved, so the 0.05 may belong to any of them. Then run champion and candidate side by side now, with\nmanifests identical except for the prompt version, cache disabled, three repeats each, paired on identical\nitems, and report the mean difference with a confidence interval. If the interval excludes zero and the\ndelta exceeds the measured floor, treat the improvement as real (and confirm it on the slices that matter);\notherwise it is not yet a result."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must the judge model's snapshot be part of the eval manifest?","options":["Because judge calls are the most expensive part of the run","Because the judge is a model whose drift changes scores without any change to the application","Because judges only work at temperature zero","Because the cache key is derived from the judge's output"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A model-graded scorer is subject to the same provider updates as the target; if it moves, every score moves. Pinning it, and re-scoring stored outputs when it changes, keeps comparisons honest. Cost and cache keys are unrelated to the reason."},{"q":"Setting temperature to zero guarantees that two identical eval runs produce identical outputs. True or false, and why?","options":["True, because greedy decoding always selects the same token","True, as long as the seed parameter is also set","False, because batching and non-deterministic GPU arithmetic can change logits, so the noise floor must be measured","False, because the tokenizer is randomised per request"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Greedy decoding is deterministic given identical logits, but logits can differ slightly across runs on hosted infrastructure, and seeds are often best-effort. The practical answer is repeats and a measured noise floor, not an assumption of determinism."},{"q":"What does a scheduled sentinel run of a frozen reference bundle detect?","options":["Regressions introduced by a candidate prompt","Contamination of the golden set","Drift outside the manifest, such as a provider changing the model behind a dated snapshot or an environment change","Whether the cache hit rate is high enough"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Nothing in the sentinel's manifest changes between runs, so any movement beyond the noise floor must come from something the manifest cannot pin. Candidate regressions are caught by the gate, not the sentinel."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/evaluation-gates-for-production-releases","sources":["Efron & Tibshirani, 1993 — An Introduction to the Bootstrap","Kohavi, Tang & Xu, 2020 — Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments","Humble & Farley, 2010 — Continuous Delivery","Breck et al., 2017 — The ML Test Score: A Rubric for ML Production Readiness and Technical Debt Reduction"],"why":"- **Without a gate, release testing is \"someone looked\" or \"the aggregate went up\".** Both reach users. The\n  aggregate rises one point while the refunds slice collapses, because the slice is 3% of the set.\n- **Exact-string tests flake on sampled outputs.** A rephrased but correct answer fails, the test is marked\n  flaky and ignored. A \"small prompt tweak\" ships with no eval because prompts are config, and a provider\n  model upgrade moves every behaviour at once with no test in the path at all.\n- **\"Block if below the last score\" blocks on noise.** At a few hundred items a one-point drop is well\n  inside run-to-run variance, so engineers learn to re-run until green, which destroys the gate's meaning\n  in a month.\n- **Human review and online-only testing do not substitute.** Reviewing every release does not scale and\n  degrades to rubber-stamping within a quarter. Online A/B is slow, exposes users before detection, and\n  cannot enforce a safety floor, whose point is that no user ever sees the failure.\n- **A working gate is explicit, statistical and auditable:** which metrics must not fall, by how much, with\n  what confidence, on which items, and who may override it.","how":"### Four kinds of criteria\n1. **Absolute floors.** The safety and adversarial set at or near 100% pass, schema validity above a\n   threshold, zero PII leakage, the regression set at 100%.\n   - Not relative to the champion *because* \"no worse than before\" is not acceptable for a safety property:\n     a flawed champion must not license a flawed candidate.\n   - Checked over repeats: run each safety item *k* times and require every repeat to pass, *because* an\n     answer that is unsafe one time in ten is unsafe for the user who draws it, and a single run would let\n     it through nine times out of ten.\n2. **Non-inferiority against the champion.** The candidate's primary metric must not be worse than the\n   champion's by more than a margin δ, with confidence, on the same dataset and scorer version, paired by\n   item.\n   - δ is set per metric from product risk (one or two points on a pass rate is a common illustrative\n     choice) and can never be tighter than the measured noise floor.\n3. **Per-slice floors.** The same test per slice with a wider margin (smaller n), or an absolute minimum per\n   slice, *because* the aggregate is a traffic-weighted average that hides rare, high-value cases.\n4. **Operational budgets.** p95 latency, cost per request and output tokens measured in the same run,\n   *because* a prompt that scores two points higher at three times the tokens is a cost regression the\n   quality metric cannot see.\n\n### The statistics of the relative gate\n1. **Pair by item.** For each item *i*, *dᵢ* = candidate score − champion score. Item difficulty is the\n   largest source of variance and cancels in *dᵢ*, so a paired test on 400 items is far tighter than\n   comparing two independently measured averages.\n2. **Bootstrap the mean difference.** Resample the *dᵢ* with replacement a few thousand times, compute the\n   mean each time, take the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles.\n3. **Why bootstrap:** scores are often bounded, bimodal or judge-graded on a few discrete levels, where a\n   normal approximation is shaky; the bootstrap needs no distributional assumption.\n4. **Read only the lower end.** Reading only the lower end of a two-sided 95% interval makes this a\n   one-sided test at 97.5% confidence, the conventional non-inferiority setup: \"could it be worse by more\n   than δ\", not \"is it different\".\n\n### Three outcomes, not two\n| Interval | Verdict | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| lower bound above −δ | **pass** | non-inferior; if the lower bound is also above zero, an improvement you may claim |\n| upper bound below −δ | **block** | confidently worse |\n| spans −δ | **needs review** | inconclusive; route to a larger run or a human |\n\n- **Why three:** treating inconclusive as pass lets regressions through; treating it as fail teaches people\n  to re-run until lucky.\n- **Many slices multiply false alarms,** so slice verdicts default to review rather than block, or margins\n  are widened.\n\n### A worked instance (illustrative numbers)\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Items | 400 |\n| Champion / candidate pass rate | 0.82 / 0.84 |\n| Mean paired difference | +2.0 points |\n| Bootstrap 95% interval | [−0.5, +4.5] |\n| Margin δ | 2 |\n\n- **Interval width depends on how many items changed verdict.** For pass/fail scores each *dᵢ* is −1, 0 or\n  +1, so their variance is roughly the discordant fraction. A half-width of 2.5 points at n = 400\n  corresponds to about one item in fifteen flipping.\n- **Verdict:** the lower bound −0.5 is above −2, so the gate passes; the interval includes zero, so the\n  release note may not claim an improvement.\n- **Counterfactual:** had the interval been [−3.5, +0.5], the lower bound would fall below −δ while the upper\n  bound sits above it: inconclusive, needs review, probably a larger paired run.\n\n### Where the gates sit\nDepth follows blast radius, the ladder from the CI/CD lesson.\n\n| Stage | Data | What blocks |\n|---|---|---|\n| **PR** | smoke set | hard floors on a small safety subset block; everything else warns |\n| **Merge and nightly** | full set | all four criteria |\n| **Release candidate** | full set plus sealed holdout, repeats to establish the noise floor | all four, plus human sign-off for high-risk applications |\n| **Canary** | online metrics against the champion's live traffic | automatic rollback trigger, *because* offline evals cannot see distribution shift in real traffic |\n| **Full rollout** | — | only after the canary window closes clean |\n\n### Overrides, configuration and gate fatigue\n- **Overrides are allowed, with a record:** a named approver, a reason, an expiry, and an entry in the\n  registry next to the promotion, *because* unrecorded overrides are how gates die quietly.\n- **Gate policy is versioned configuration** in the repository, per application, so threshold changes are\n  themselves reviewed.\n- **Track the override rate and the \"gate was right\" rate** (blocked candidates later confirmed bad).\n- **A high override rate** means the margin is tighter than the noise floor, the golden items are stale, or\n  a scorer is flaky. Recalibrate the gate rather than keep overriding it.","example":"### A three-outcome gate\nApplies absolute floors first, then a paired bootstrap non-inferiority test per slice, then the\noperational budgets, and returns one of three outcomes. Inputs are per-item scores for candidate and\nchampion aligned by item id on the same dataset and scorer versions.\n\n```python\nimport random, statistics\n\ndef paired_bootstrap_ci(cand: list[float], champ: list[float], iters: int = 2000, seed: int = 0):\n    \"\"\"cand and champ are aligned by item id, same dataset version, same scorer version.\"\"\"\n    d = [c - h for c, h in zip(cand, champ)]\n    rng, n = random.Random(seed), len(d)\n    means = sorted(statistics.mean(rng.choice(d) for _ in range(n)) for _ in range(iters))\n    return statistics.mean(d), means[int(0.025 * iters)], means[int(0.975 * iters)]\n\ndef gate(cand: dict, champ: dict, policy: dict):\n    # 1. absolute floors: never relative to the champion\n    failed = [k for k, floor in policy[\"floors\"].items() if cand[\"floors\"][k] < floor]\n    if failed:\n        return \"block\", {\"failed_floors\": failed}\n    # 2. paired non-inferiority, aggregate and per slice\n    verdicts = {}\n    for name, margin in policy[\"margins\"].items():            # {\"all\": 0.02, \"refunds\": 0.04, ...}\n        mean, lo, hi = paired_bootstrap_ci(cand[\"scores\"][name], champ[\"scores\"][name])\n        if lo > -margin:\n            verdicts[name] = (\"pass\", mean, lo, hi)             # improvement claimable only if lo > 0\n        elif hi < -margin:\n            verdicts[name] = (\"block\", mean, lo, hi)\n        else:\n            verdicts[name] = (\"review\", mean, lo, hi)           # inconclusive is neither pass nor fail\n    # 3. operational budgets measured in the same run\n    for k in (\"p95_latency_ms\", \"cost_per_request\"):\n        if cand[\"ops\"][k] > policy[\"budgets\"][k]:\n            verdicts[k] = (\"block\", cand[\"ops\"][k])\n    states = {v[0] for v in verdicts.values()}\n    outcome = \"block\" if \"block\" in states else (\"review\" if \"review\" in states else \"pass\")\n    return outcome, verdicts\n\npolicy = {\"floors\": {\"safety\": 1.0, \"schema_valid\": 0.995, \"regression\": 1.0},\n          \"margins\": {\"all\": 0.02, \"refunds\": 0.04},\n          \"budgets\": {\"p95_latency_ms\": 2500, \"cost_per_request\": 0.012}}\n```\n\n### Running it: a passing aggregate, an inconclusive slice\nContinues the gate above with synthetic pass/fail scores. On 400 items, 28 flip verdict (18 candidate\nwins, 10 losses), a net +2.0 points, close to one item in fifteen. The 40-item refunds slice loses 2 net\nitems.\n\n```python\ndef flips(n, base_pass, wins, losses):\n    \"\"\"Paired pass/fail scores: both pass on base_pass items, candidate-only wins, champion-only wins.\"\"\"\n    champ = [1.0] * base_pass + [0.0] * wins + [1.0] * losses\n    cand = [1.0] * base_pass + [1.0] * wins + [0.0] * losses\n    rest = n - len(champ)\n    return cand + [0.0] * rest, champ + [0.0] * rest\n\nall_cand, all_champ = flips(400, 312, 18, 10)        # 400 items, 28 flipped: +8 net = +2.0 points\nref_cand, ref_champ = flips(40, 30, 1, 3)            # refunds slice: 40 items, net -2 = -5.0 points\ncand = {\"floors\": {\"safety\": 1.0, \"schema_valid\": 0.998, \"regression\": 1.0},\n        \"scores\": {\"all\": all_cand, \"refunds\": ref_cand},\n        \"ops\": {\"p95_latency_ms\": 2100, \"cost_per_request\": 0.010}}\nchamp = {\"scores\": {\"all\": all_champ, \"refunds\": ref_champ}}\n\noutcome, verdicts = gate(cand, champ, policy)\nprint(outcome)                                       # review\nfor k, v in verdicts.items():\n    print(k, v[0], [round(x * 100, 1) for x in v[1:]])\n# all pass [2.0, -0.5, 4.5]        lower bound above -2: non-inferior, but no claimable improvement\n# refunds review [-5.0, -15.0, 5.0] 40 items cannot decide a 4-point margin: bigger run or a human\n```\n\n| Verdict | Why | Next step |\n|---|---|---|\n| Aggregate **pass** | lower bound −0.5 is above −δ = −2 | release note says \"no regression\" |\n| Refunds **review** | interval [−15, +5] spans −δ = −4 | add refund items or repeats, or a human reads the 4 flipped items |\n| Overall **review** | any review without a block | the release waits on the slice, not on a re-run until green |","patterns":["**Gate policy as reviewed config** — for every application with a gate. Keep floors, margins and budgets in a versioned file in the repository (like the `policy` dict above) and require an owner's review on changes, for example through a CODEOWNERS entry. Monitor policy changes per quarter and who made them.","**Champion re-run in the same job** — whenever the gate compares. Run champion and candidate side by side on the same dataset version, scorer version and infrastructure, rather than reading a stored score. Monitor how often the champion's fresh score drifts from its stored one; that drift is your sentinel.","**Safety floors over repeats** — at production temperature. Run each safety and injection item *k* times and require every repeat to pass before release. Monitor the per-item failure count across repeats, not just the pass rate.","**Overrides with expiry in the registry** — when the business must ship past a review verdict. Record approver, reason and expiry next to the promotion, and re-gate when the override expires. Monitor override rate and the \"gate was right\" rate.","**Canary with automatic rollback** — after every offline pass. Route a small share of traffic to the candidate, compare online metrics with the champion, and roll back automatically on breach. Monitor escaped regressions the canary caught that offline gates missed."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Candidate and champion re-run on same items\"] --> F{\"Absolute floors met over k repeats?\"}\n    F -->|\"no\"| B[\"Block\"]\n    F -->|\"yes\"| P[\"Paired bootstrap CI per slice\"]\n    P --> D{\"Where is the interval vs minus delta?\"}\n    D -->|\"upper below\"| B\n    D -->|\"spans\"| R[\"Needs review: bigger run or human\"]\n    D -->|\"lower above\"| O{\"Latency and cost budgets ok?\"}\n    O -->|\"no\"| B\n    O -->|\"yes\"| K[\"Canary vs champion live traffic\"]\n    K -->|\"online regress\"| RB[\"Auto rollback\"]\n    K -->|\"clean window\"| FR[\"Full rollout\"]","caption":"Floors short-circuit to block; the paired interval decides pass, block or review per slice; budgets can\nstill block; and the canary is the last gate."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a non-inferiority gate and why not just require the score to go up?","a":"- **Definition:** the candidate passes if, with confidence, it is not worse than the champion by more than a\n  margin δ on identical items.\n- **Why not \"go up\":** at a few hundred items small deltas are noise, so a \"must improve\" or \"must not drop\"\n  rule flakes and teaches people to re-run until green.\n- **Improvements are claimed separately,** only when the interval's lower bound is above zero."},{"q":"Why is the comparison paired, and how much does it help?","a":"- **Item difficulty cancels** in the per-item difference, and it is the largest source of variance.\n- **The interval width then depends on the discordant fraction:** for pass/fail scores the variance of the\n  difference is roughly the share of items that flipped verdict.\n- **Example:** at n = 400 with about one item in fifteen flipping, the 95% half-width is about 2.5 points."},{"q":"A gate blocks an unchanged bundle compared against itself. What is wrong?","a":"- **The margin is tighter than the noise floor,** so run-to-run variation alone crosses it.\n- **Or the comparison is not like for like:** a stored champion score from an older judge or dataset\n  version.\n- **Fix:** measure the floor with repeats, set δ above it, and always re-run the champion in the same job."},{"q":"Which criteria must be absolute rather than relative, and how do you check them with sampled outputs?","a":"- **Absolute:** safety and adversarial pass rate, PII leakage, schema validity, the regression set, because\n  a flawed champion must not license a flawed candidate.\n- **Check over repeats:** run each safety item *k* times and require every repeat to pass; a single run lets a\n  one-in-ten unsafe answer through most of the time.\n- **Floors short-circuit** the gate to block before any relative test runs."},{"q":"Half your releases land in needs-review. What do you change?","a":"- **Not the outcome rule:** collapsing review into pass or fail reintroduces regressions or re-run gaming.\n- **Raise power:** more items in the slices that are inconclusive, or repeats where generation noise\n  dominates.\n- **Or revisit margins and slices:** widen per-slice margins, make minor slices review-only, and check for\n  stale items or a flaky scorer."}],"tradeoffs":["**No flaking vs decisiveness.** A statistical non-inferiority gate stops flaking on noise but needs enough items to be decisive; a small set can only catch large breaks, so grow the set or accept a wider margin.","**Unambiguous floors vs one bad label.** Absolute safety floors are unambiguous, but one mislabeled item blocks every release until fixed, which is acceptable only if the item-review path is fast.","**Humans for ambiguity vs a review bottleneck.** Three outcomes keep humans for ambiguous cases, yet if most verdicts land in review the queue becomes the bottleneck; that is the signal to raise n or loosen the margin, not to skip review.","**Slice coverage vs false alarms.** Per-slice gates catch hidden regressions, and every added slice adds a chance of a false alarm. Past a handful of slices, make slice verdicts review-only or correct for multiple comparisons.","**Cost-aware releases vs infrastructure variance.** Gating on cost and latency prevents expensive \"improvements\" but ties quality releases to infrastructure variance; measure both bundles in the same run on the same infrastructure."],"pitfalls":["**Comparing against a stored champion score.** Instead of re-running the champion on the same dataset and scorer version. Symptom: every candidate failing after a judge or dataset update.","**Treating an inconclusive interval as a pass, or as a fail.** Symptom: either regressions reaching canary or engineers re-running the gate until the dice land right.","**A margin tighter than the noise floor.** Symptom: an unchanged bundle failing the gate against itself.","**Gating on the aggregate only.** Symptom: a slice dropping fifteen points while the headline metric rises one.","**One sample per item for a 100% safety floor.** At production temperature. Symptom: a floor that passed in CI and a jailbreak that reproduces in production on the second or third attempt.","**Overrides with no record.** Symptom: a post-incident review that cannot find who approved the release the gate had blocked.","**Gating only on the smoke set.** Symptom: the nightly full run discovers what the PR gate missed, after the merge."],"when":"- **Use wherever** a change to code, prompt, model, parameters or index can reach users.\n- **Start small:** hard floors plus one paired non-inferiority test on the primary metric; add slices as\n  error analysis reveals them, and configure block versus warn per stage.\n- **Measure the gate:** pass, block and review rates, override rate, time to verdict, the \"gate caught it\"\n  rate and escaped regressions found by the canary.\n- **Day to day,** it turns \"the eval looks fine\" into a verdict with a confidence interval attached.\n- **Interviews:** explain why the test is paired, what the margin means, why there are three outcomes, and\n  which criteria must be absolute.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the paired bootstrap and the policy file, makes the pipeline re-run the champion alongside the candidate, and wires the verdict into the registry promotion step.","architect":"Owns the gate policy per application: which metrics are absolute, the margins and their relation to the noise floor, which stages block, and who may override with what record.","fde":"Translates a customer's acceptance criteria into floors and margins on their golden set, so go-live is a reproducible verdict rather than a negotiation over a demo."},"check":{"q":"A candidate prompt scores 85.0% against the champion's 83.5% on 300 items, and the product manager\nwants to ship it as an improvement. What does your gate say, and what do you tell the PM?","a":"First confirm both runs share the dataset and scorer versions and that the champion was re-run,\nnot read from history. Then compute the paired bootstrap interval on the 300 per-item differences; at\nthis size a 1.5-point mean difference will very likely have an interval that includes zero, so the gate\ncannot certify an improvement. Whether it can even report non-inferior for a two-point margin depends on\nhow many items flipped: the lower bound is about 1.5 minus the half-width, so it clears −2 only if the\nhalf-width is under 3.5 points, which at n = 300 means roughly fewer than one item in ten changing\nverdict; with more churn than that the verdict is needs-review, not pass. Check the absolute\nfloors, the per-slice verdicts and the cost and latency budgets, since a longer prompt often buys its\npoints with tokens. Tell the PM the change is safe to ship if the gate passes and everything else does too, that \"improvement\"\nneeds either a larger paired run or a canary that shows it in live metrics, and that the release note\nshould say \"no regression\" until then."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the gate compare candidate and champion on identical items with a paired test rather than against last week's champion score?","options":["Because last week's run is too expensive to look up","Because item difficulty cancels in paired differences and the pins (dataset, scorer, model) are guaranteed equal","Because the champion's score changes every day due to caching","Because a paired test does not require a confidence interval"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pairing removes the dominant source of variance and forces both bundles through the same dataset and scorer versions. A historical score may have been produced by a different judge, dataset or model snapshot, so a difference against it has no clear cause."},{"q":"The bootstrap interval for candidate minus champion is [−3.5, +0.5] points and the margin is 2 points. What is the verdict?","options":["Pass, because the interval includes positive values","Block, because the mean difference is negative","Needs review, because the interval spans the margin and the evidence is inconclusive","Pass, because the upper bound is above zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The lower bound is below −2 and the upper bound is above it, so the data neither establishes non-inferiority nor confirms a regression. The honest outcome routes to a larger paired run or a human, not to a coin flip."},{"q":"Which criterion should be an absolute floor rather than a relative test against the champion?","options":["Average answer length","The judge's helpfulness score on the full set","The pass rate on the prompt-injection and safety set","p50 latency"],"answer":2,"explanation":"\"No worse than the champion\" is the wrong standard for a safety property, since a flawed champion would license a flawed candidate. Helpfulness is legitimately relative, and latency belongs to the operational budget rather than to a safety floor."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/canary-shadow-deployments","sources":["Kohavi, Tang & Xu, 2020 — Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments","Humble & Farley, 2010 — Continuous Delivery"],"why":"- **Offline evals only say \"probably better\".** They score a few hundred curated cases; they cannot tell\n  you what the candidate does on live traffic.\n- **An AI change is non-local.** Swapping the model, editing three lines of the system prompt or\n  tightening a tool schema changes *every* request, including the long tail your golden set never\n  sampled: odd phrasings, an untested language, longer-than-expected tool outputs, a tenant whose\n  documents are all scanned PDFs. A service change touches one code path a unit test proves; an LLM\n  change touches a probability distribution only traffic reveals.\n- **\"The eval gate passed, deploy to 100%\" fails three ways.** The golden set is small and ages (it\n  reflects what users asked when it was built). Judge scores are noisy, so a two-point gain on 300 cases\n  is often within noise. Some regressions only exist in production: cold prompt caches, rate limits on\n  the new model, latency under real concurrency, a tool behaving differently with real credentials.\n- **Big-bang puts everyone in the blast radius** for the hours a quality regression takes to show up in\n  feedback.\n- **The two patterns buy different information at bounded cost.** Shadow buys a like-for-like comparison\n  with no exposure but no user feedback; canary buys real user outcomes at the price of real exposure.","how":"### What a \"version\" is\nThe rollout unit is a **bundle**, not a model, addressable as one immutable unit:\n\n| Bundle part | Example |\n|---|---|\n| Model id | pinned snapshot |\n| Prompt template | version |\n| Decoding parameters | temperature, max tokens |\n| Tool schemas | versioned |\n| Retrieval index | version |\n| Guardrail configuration | version |\n\n- **Why immutable:** a canary that mixes a new prompt with an old index tests a combination you never\n  evaluated and cannot roll back to.\n\n### Shadow (traffic mirroring)\n1. **Serve normally.** The gateway serves the request with the incumbent as usual.\n2. **Fork asynchronously.** It sends a copy to the candidate after the user response is on its way, so\n   the user never waits on the candidate.\n3. **Log both under one trace id**, with latency, tokens, cost, schema validity, refusal flags and tool\n   calls.\n4. **Score pairs offline:** deterministic checks on 100% of pairs, LLM-judge pairwise preference on a\n   sample, and disagreement analysis (where the two differ most, by intent or tenant).\n\nShadow is safe *because* nothing the candidate produces leaves the system. That same property is its\nlimit:\n- **Side-effecting tools must be stubbed or sandboxed**, because an agent in shadow must not send the\n  email twice.\n- **Multi-turn effects are invisible**, because the user's next message responds to the incumbent's\n  answer, not the candidate's.\n- **No user feedback**, so shadow answers \"is it different, cheaper or slower\", not \"do users prefer it\".\n- **Cost doubles on the mirrored slice**, so most teams shadow 5–20% of traffic (illustrative) or shadow\n  100% for a short window.\n\n**The LLM-specific trap: the incumbent disagrees with itself.**\n- At non-zero temperature, and often even at temperature zero on hosted APIs, the same request run twice\n  yields different text.\n- So \"B differs from A on 30% of requests\" is meaningless until you know A-vs-A differs on, say, 25%.\n- **Fix:** mirror a small slice to a second copy of the *incumbent* too, and read the candidate's\n  disagreement as the excess over that self-disagreement floor, broken down by intent.\n\n### Canary (partial live exposure)\n1. **Sticky assignment.** The router hashes a stable key (usually user or session id) into a bucket and\n   routes buckets below the canary percentage to the candidate. Hashing *because* a conversation must not\n   flip models mid-thread; random per-request assignment produces incoherent sessions and contaminates\n   the comparison.\n2. **Watch guard metrics on both arms** over a bake window: error rate, p95 latency, schema-failure rate,\n   refusal rate, cost per request, tool-failure rate, and a quality proxy you trust (thumbs-down rate,\n   escalation rate, judge score on a sample).\n3. **Ramp in steps**, for example 1% → 5% → 25% → 50% → 100%, each held long enough to collect a\n   minimum sample. Small first steps *because* the first step exists to catch catastrophic breakage\n   (wrong schema, 429s from the new model's quota) with the fewest users exposed.\n4. **Roll back automatically** on any guard breach by flipping the pointer to the incumbent. Promotion to\n   100% requires success metrics that are not worse, not merely that nothing exploded.\n\n### What the numbers can and cannot tell you\nDetecting a shift of size δ in a metric with standard deviation σ needs, per arm, roughly\n\n```\nn ≈ 16 · σ² / δ²        (rule of thumb: two-sided α = 0.05, 80% power)\nσ² = p(1 − p) for a rate p\n```\n\n- **Halving the effect quadruples the traffic** you need.\n- **A 1% canary** on 20,000 requests a day gives 200 canary requests a day.\n\n| Signal | Inputs | Samples per arm | Time at 1% |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Schema failures 0.5% → 5% | σ² ≈ 0.027 at the midpoint, δ = 0.045 | 16 × 0.027 / 0.002 ≈ 210 | about a day, faster in practice because the incumbent baseline is known precisely |\n| Judge score 4.1 → 4.0 | σ ≈ 0.8 | 16 × 0.64 / 0.01 ≈ 1,000 | a week (an afternoon at 25%) |\n\n- **So early steps are for catastrophic signals**; later, larger steps are where quality comparisons\n  become meaningful.\n- **It is a controlled experiment only** when arms are assigned randomly and compared on the same window.\n  Comparing this week's canary to last week's baseline confounds the version with time.\n\n### Canary vs A/B test vs blue-green\nThese are often conflated.\n\n| | Question | Decision | Ends |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Canary** (safety mechanism) | \"Is anything broken?\" | asymmetric: roll back on harm, promote on *no harm* | at 100% |\n| **A/B test** (decision mechanism) | \"Which is better on the success metric?\" | either arm may win; fixed split until the pre-registered sample is reached | at the planned sample |\n| **Blue-green** | \"Swap environments\" | flip all traffic at once; instant rollback | immediately |\n\n- **A large canary step can double as an A/B test** only if you pre-commit to the metric and sample size.\n- **Blue-green has no gradual exposure**, so it suits infrastructure changes better than behaviour\n  changes.\n\n### Where it lives\n- **Routing, hashing, mirroring and the rollout percentage** belong in the AI gateway or control plane as\n  configuration, not in application code, so a rollout or rollback is a config change with no deploy.","example":"### Gateway router with sticky canary and async shadow\nA minimal gateway router: sticky canary assignment by user hash, an asynchronous shadow fork for a\nseparate candidate, and paired logging.\n- **The shadow call is a background task**, so the user's response never waits on it.\n- **Only incumbent-served requests are mirrored**, so every shadow pair is candidate-vs-incumbent.\n- **The shadow candidate runs with side-effecting tools disabled.**\n- **Each experiment salts the hash**, so the same 5% of users are not the guinea pigs for every rollout.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, hashlib, json, time\n\nROLLOUT = {\"incumbent\": \"bundle-v12\", \"canary\": \"bundle-v13\", \"canary_pct\": 5,\n           \"shadow\": \"bundle-v14\", \"shadow_pct\": 20, \"salt\": \"exp-2026-10-v13\"}\n_background: set[asyncio.Task] = set()            # keep refs so tasks are not GC'd mid-run\n\ndef bucket(key: str, salt: str) -> int:           # stable 0..99 bucket per user per experiment\n    return int(hashlib.sha256(f\"{salt}:{key}\".encode()).hexdigest(), 16) % 100\n\nasync def call_bundle(bundle: str, req: dict, allow_side_effects=True) -> dict:\n    t = time.perf_counter()\n    out = await llm_app.run(bundle, req, allow_side_effects=allow_side_effects)\n    return {\"bundle\": bundle, \"text\": out.text, \"schema_ok\": out.schema_ok,\n            \"refused\": out.refused, \"cost\": out.cost, \"ms\": (time.perf_counter() - t) * 1e3}\n\nasync def shadow(req: dict, served: dict, trace_id: str):\n    cand = await call_bundle(ROLLOUT[\"shadow\"], req, allow_side_effects=False)\n    log.info(json.dumps({\"trace\": trace_id, \"served\": served, \"shadow\": cand}))\n\nasync def handle(req: dict, user_id: str, trace_id: str) -> dict:\n    b = bucket(user_id, ROLLOUT[\"salt\"])\n    bundle = ROLLOUT[\"canary\"] if b < ROLLOUT[\"canary_pct\"] else ROLLOUT[\"incumbent\"]\n    served = await call_bundle(bundle, req)\n    metrics.observe(bundle=bundle, ms=served[\"ms\"], schema_ok=served[\"schema_ok\"],\n                    refused=served[\"refused\"], cost=served[\"cost\"])\n    if bundle == ROLLOUT[\"incumbent\"] and bucket(user_id, \"shadow\") < ROLLOUT[\"shadow_pct\"]:\n        task = asyncio.create_task(shadow(req, served, trace_id))  # off the critical path\n        _background.add(task); task.add_done_callback(_background.discard)\n    return served\n# ROLLOUT is loaded from the control plane; promoting or rolling back is a config write, not a deploy.\n```\n\n### Ramp planner: how long each step must bake\nPure Python; plug in your traffic and the effect you need to see before trusting a step.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef n_per_arm(var: float, delta: float) -> int:\n    return math.ceil(16 * var / delta ** 2)          # two-sided alpha 0.05, 80% power\n\ndef days_at(n: int, daily_requests: int, pct: float) -> float:\n    return n / (daily_requests * pct / 100)\n\np_mid = (0.005 + 0.05) / 2\nschema_n = n_per_arm(p_mid * (1 - p_mid), 0.045)     # rate 0.5% -> 5%\njudge_n = n_per_arm(0.8 ** 2, 0.1)                   # score 4.1 -> 4.0, sigma 0.8\n\nfor pct in (1, 5, 25):\n    print(f\"{pct:>2}%: schema {days_at(schema_n, 20_000, pct):.2f} d, \"\n          f\"judge {days_at(judge_n, 20_000, pct):.2f} d\")\n# schema_n = 212, judge_n = 1024\n#  1%: schema 1.06 d, judge 5.12 d\n#  5%: schema 0.21 d, judge 1.02 d\n# 25%: schema 0.04 d, judge 0.20 d\n```\n\n| Step | What it can detect in a day at 20,000 req/day | Use it for |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1% | a 10x schema-failure jump, 429s, timeouts | catastrophic breakage |\n| 5% | the same within hours; judge shifts of 0.1 in about a day | early quality read |\n| 25% and up | 0.1-point judge shifts within an afternoon | promotion decision |","patterns":["**Mesh-level mirroring for stateless calls.** When the candidate has no side effects, mirror at the proxy: Istio's `VirtualService` supports a `mirror` destination with a `mirrorPercentage`, and Envoy has request mirror policies. Mirrored responses are discarded. Monitor candidate error rate and the cost of the mirrored slice. For agents with tools, mirror in the application so tools can be disabled.","**Declarative canary steps with automated analysis.** Argo Rollouts expresses the ramp as `setWeight` and `pause` steps and can run an analysis template against Prometheus queries between steps, aborting on failure. Monitor the analysis run results per step, not just the final state.","**Self-disagreement baseline.** Send a small slice (for example 1-2%, illustrative) of shadow traffic to a second incumbent replica and report candidate disagreement as excess over A-vs-A, per intent. Monitor the A-vs-A floor itself; if it jumps, the provider changed something.","**Minimum-sample guards.** Encode \"roll back if refusal rate exceeds baseline by X *and* n ≥ N\" so quality alerts cannot fire on 150 samples; fire hard-error guards (5xx, schema failures, 429s) immediately. Monitor how often a rollback was later judged a false alarm.","**Percentage flags keyed on a salted user id.** Feature-flag systems with percentage rollouts by user attribute (LaunchDarkly, Unleash and similar) give sticky assignment and a one-click kill switch without a deploy. Monitor the actual cohort size against the configured percentage."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Request + user id\"] --> H[\"Hash bucket 0..99 with salt\"]\n    H -->|\"bucket below canary pct\"| C[\"Canary bundle\"]\n    H -->|\"otherwise\"| I[\"Incumbent bundle\"]\n    C --> U[\"Response to user\"]\n    I --> U\n    I -.->|\"async fork, tools off\"| S[\"Shadow candidate\"]\n    S --> L[(\"Paired log by trace id\")]\n    L --> J[\"Pair scoring and disagreement\"]\n    C --> G[\"Guard metrics, both arms\"]\n    I --> G\n    G -->|\"breach\"| RB[\"Rollback pointer to incumbent\"]\n    G -->|\"holds with min sample\"| RP[\"Ramp to next step\"]","caption":"Every request is bucketed by a salted user hash; incumbent-served requests may also be mirrored to the\nshadow candidate with side effects off, and guard metrics on both arms drive the ramp or rollback."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a shadow deployment and a canary, and when do you use each?","a":"- **Shadow** mirrors live requests to the candidate and discards its output: zero user exposure, like-for-\n  like comparison, but no user feedback and no multi-turn signal.\n- **Canary** serves the candidate to a small sticky cohort and ramps on guard metrics: real outcomes at\n  the price of real exposure.\n- **Order:** shadow first for structurally new changes (new provider, new agent loop), then canary for\n  user outcomes; skip both for trivial reversible changes."},{"q":"Your shadow report says the candidate disagrees with production on 35% of requests. Is that bad?","a":"- **Not yet knowable.** LLM outputs vary run to run, often even at temperature zero on hosted APIs, so the\n  incumbent disagrees with itself.\n- **Measure the floor:** mirror a slice to a second incumbent copy. If A-vs-A is 30%, the real signal is\n  the 5-point excess, and where it concentrates by intent.\n- **Then read the pairs** in the high-excess intents with a pairwise judge and a human spot check."},{"q":"A 1% canary looked clean for two days. Why might you still not promote?","a":"- **Sample size.** At 20,000 requests a day, 1% gives 200 per day; a 0.1-point judge shift with σ ≈ 0.8\n  needs about 1,000 per arm, so two days cannot resolve it.\n- **Asymmetric bar.** \"Nothing exploded\" clears catastrophic risk only; promotion needs success metrics\n  not worse at a step large enough to measure them, such as 25%.\n- **Cohort coverage.** Check the hashed cohort includes the languages, tenants and document types of real\n  traffic."},{"q":"How would you shadow an agent that can send emails and issue refunds?","a":"- **Disable or stub side effects in the shadow arm**, recording the intended tool calls instead, because\n  a mirrored agent must not act twice.\n- **Compare the intended tool-call mix** and arguments against the incumbent's actual calls.\n- **Accept the blind spot:** real refund behaviour can only be observed in canary, so give refund rate\n  the tightest guard and the smallest first step."},{"q":"When is a canary the wrong tool and a pre-registered A/B test the right one?","a":"- **When the question is \"which is better\", not \"is anything broken\".** A canary is asymmetric and ends\n  at 100%; an A/B test holds a fixed split until a pre-registered sample is reached and either arm may\n  win.\n- **Example:** choosing between two prompt styles that both pass safety; you need a success metric and a\n  sample size committed in advance, otherwise you are peeking at noise."}],"tradeoffs":["**Shadow: zero exposure vs double cost and blind spots.** You get a like-for-like comparison, but pay roughly double inference on the mirrored slice and learn nothing about user preference or multi-turn behaviour.","**Canary: real outcomes vs real exposure.** Every percent is real users seeing a possibly worse system; once the change is low-risk and well-evaluated offline, the extra caution costs more than it saves.","**Sticky assignment: clean experiment vs concentrated harm.** Conversations stay coherent, but a bad canary hits the same users repeatedly, so a 5% canary is a 100% regression for those users.","**Small early steps: bounded blast radius vs slow rollout.** For a hard deadline (a vendor deprecation date) you may accept larger steps and lean on shadow results instead.","**Automatic rollback: no humans at 3 a.m. vs false alarms.** With noisy quality metrics and small samples it will roll back good releases unless thresholds carry a minimum sample."],"pitfalls":["**Shadowing an agent with live tools.** Symptom: duplicate emails, tickets or payments with no user action behind them. Fix: stub or sandbox side-effecting tools in the shadow arm.","**Random per-request routing instead of per-session hashing.** Symptom: users report the assistant \"changed personality\" mid-conversation while aggregate metrics look fine. Fix: hash user or session id.","**Comparing the canary to a historical baseline.** Symptom: a \"regression\" that disappears once you notice the canary window included a product launch. Fix: a concurrent control on the same window.","**Judging quality after 150 samples.** Symptom: rollbacks and promotions that flip day to day with no change in the system. Fix: compute the required sample first.","**Ignoring warm-up effects.** The candidate has a cold prompt cache and a fresh rate-limit bucket. Symptom: a latency and 429 spike in the first minutes that disappears, then gets blamed on the version. Fix: pre-warm and exclude a short warm-up window.","**Canarying only internal users.** Symptom: a clean canary, then complaints from the segment that writes in a different language or uploads different documents. Fix: a cohort representative of real traffic.","**Reading shadow disagreement without an A-vs-A baseline.** Symptom: a \"40% disagreement\" alarm that is ordinary sampling variation in both versions. Fix: measure the self-disagreement floor."],"when":"- **Shadow first when the change is large or structurally different:** a new model family, a new\n  provider, a rewritten agent loop, a new retrieval pipeline. It tells you whether the candidate is safe\n  enough to show anyone and where it disagrees with the incumbent, which is where to look.\n- **Canary when you need user outcomes** to decide, or when the change is incremental and offline evals\n  already pass.\n- **Skip both** for trivial, fully reversible changes with strong offline coverage, where the process\n  costs more than the risk.\n- **Measure:** guard metrics on both arms; demand a minimum sample before any quality verdict; automate\n  the rollback path before the first ramp.\n- **Interviews:** explain why sticky assignment matters, why shadow cannot test multi-turn agents, and how\n  sample size limits what a 1% canary can detect.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the hashed router and asynchronous mirroring in the gateway, tags every trace with the bundle id, and builds the paired-comparison job that scores shadow output.","architect":"Decides what the deployable bundle is, where rollout state lives (control plane, not code) and which guard metrics gate each ramp step for which risk class of change.","fde":"Runs shadow mode against a customer's live traffic before cutover so the customer sees a disagreement report instead of a surprise, and negotiates which users form the first canary cohort."},"check":{"q":"You are migrating a customer-support agent from model A to model B. The agent can issue refunds.\nOffline evals show B slightly ahead. Design the rollout and justify each step.","a":"Start with shadow at a meaningful slice of traffic with the refund tool disabled for the shadow\narm, because a side-effecting tool cannot be mirrored safely. Score pairs on deterministic checks\n(schema validity, refusal rate, tool-call mix, latency, cost) at 100% and judge preference on a sample;\nanalyse where B disagrees most with A by intent. If nothing is alarming, canary B to about 1% of\nsessions by user hash, with automatic rollback on error rate, schema failures, latency and\nrefund-rate anomalies; hold until a minimum sample accrues, then ramp through 5, 25 and 50%. Use\nthumbs-down and escalation rate as success metrics at the larger steps, where samples allow it. Keep\nA's bundle warm and the routing in control-plane config so rollback is a pointer flip, and note that\nB's refund behaviour in canary is the one thing shadow could not test, so it gets the tightest alert."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a shadow deployment unable to evaluate a multi-turn conversational agent properly?","options":["Because shadow traffic cannot be logged alongside the served response","Because the user's next turn responds to the incumbent's answer, so the candidate never gets the conversation it would have produced","Because shadow deployments only work for stateless classification models","Because the candidate's latency makes the mirrored copy time out"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shadow forks the same input to both versions, but a conversation diverges after the first reply. The candidate sees follow-ups shaped by the incumbent, so its multi-turn behaviour is never observed."},{"q":"A team runs a 1% canary on a service with 10,000 requests per day and wants to know within a day whether the judge score moved from 4.2 to 4.1. What is the right assessment?","options":["Sufficient: 100 samples is enough for any metric","Sufficient, provided the judge runs at temperature zero","Insufficient: detecting a small shift in a noisy score needs far more samples; the 1% step is for catching catastrophic failures","Insufficient only because the canary should run for a week regardless of traffic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sample size needed grows roughly with the inverse square of the effect size. A 100-request slice can reveal a schema-failure spike but cannot resolve a 0.1-point judge difference; use larger ramp steps for quality verdicts."},{"q":"Why should canary assignment hash on user or session id rather than pick randomly per request?","options":["Hashing is faster than random number generation at gateway scale","Random assignment would exceed the canary percentage over time","Per-session stickiness keeps conversations coherent and prevents the arms from contaminating each other's results","Hashing lets the canary cohort be chosen from internal users only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"If consecutive turns of one conversation hit different versions, users see inconsistent behaviour and each arm's metrics include context produced by the other arm, which invalidates the comparison."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/rollback","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Humble & Farley, 2010 — Continuous Delivery"],"why":"- **Container rollback does not transfer.** For a stateless service you redeploy the previous image. AI\n  systems break that model in three ways.\n- **The deployable unit is a bundle across many stores.** The prompt lives in a registry, the model behind\n  a provider API or a GPU fleet, the index in a vector database, tool schemas in code, the guardrail policy\n  in a config service. Rolling back one without the others yields a never-evaluated combination:\n  yesterday's prompt against today's model is a new version, not the old one.\n- **Some changes destroy their own undo path.** Re-embedding a corpus in place overwrites the vectors the\n  old prompt was tuned against, and the old query-embedding model cannot be used against new vectors,\n  because the two vector spaces are not comparable. Overwriting a fine-tuned checkpoint or adapter leaves\n  nothing to return to. A vendor retiring the previous snapshot removes the old model entirely.\n- **Failures are quiet and lagging.** Rollback-worthy failures are usually quality regressions, visible\n  only through thumbs-down rate, escalations or judge scores, not a crash. By the time a human notices,\n  the bad version has served for hours.\n- **\"Just tweak the prompt\" compounds it.** Every edit under pressure is another unevaluated version, and\n  the original cause ends up buried under three patches. Rollback makes the first incident move a\n  known-good state, so diagnosis can happen calmly.","how":"### Make versions immutable and deployment a pointer\n1. **Pin every component in a manifest:** model id with snapshot date, prompt version hash, decoding\n   parameters, tool schema version, index id, guardrail policy version, and the feature flags that were on.\n2. **Store manifests immutably** in the registry.\n3. **Point the environment at one manifest.** The environment, say `prod`, is an alias.\n4. **Deploy = move the alias forward; rollback = move it to the previous manifest.** This is O(1)\n   *because* nothing is rebuilt, re-embedded or re-trained; the data plane picks up the new pointer on its\n   next config refresh.\n\n- **The rule that follows:** roll back only to a manifest that previously served production (or passed\n  the same gates). Hand-assembling \"old prompt + new model\" during an incident is a forward change in\n  disguise.\n\n### Keep the previous version reachable\nA pointer is only useful if its target still exists and is warm.\n\n| Component | What keeps it reachable |\n|---|---|\n| Self-hosted models | keep previous weights loaded or at least on local disk; loading tens of gigabytes from object storage can take minutes (illustrative), which is the whole rollback budget |\n| Provider models | pin snapshots and track the vendor's deprecation calendar, so the \"previous\" model was not retired last month |\n| Indexes | build the new index beside the old (blue/green), switch the alias, keep the old one until the new one has baked; never re-embed in place |\n| Fine-tunes | keep the previous adapter or checkpoint and the training data that produced it |\n\n### Decide triggers before the release\nRollback should be a decision made in advance and executed by a rule, not debated in the incident channel.\n\n| Signal type | Examples | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| Guard metrics | error rate, p95 latency, schema-failure rate, refusal rate, cost per request, tool-failure rate | automatic, with thresholds and windows |\n| Quality signals | judge score on a sample, thumbs-down rate, escalation rate | usually a human decision, because they are noisier and slower |\n\n- **Every trigger carries a minimum sample size**, *because* a threshold evaluated on twenty requests\n  produces flapping.\n- **Scope automatic triggers to a bake window** after promotion (hours to a few days). Inside it, a breach\n  is most likely caused by the release, so reverting is the right bet.\n- **Outside the window, page a human.** A breach is then more often external (a provider incident, a\n  traffic shift, a broken upstream tool), and rolling back to a version on the same provider fixes nothing\n  while discarding a good release.\n\n### Handle what a pointer flip does not restore\nRollback restores behaviour, not data. Check after the flip:\n- **Caches keyed without the version.** A semantic or response cache filled by the bad version keeps\n  serving its answers. Key caches by manifest id, or purge on rollback.\n- **Conversations in flight.** A session whose earlier turns and compaction summaries came from the bad\n  version continues under the old one; usually acceptable, but worth knowing when reading feedback.\n- **Agent memory and side effects.** Notes written to long-term memory, tickets filed, emails sent:\n  rollback does not undo them. The audit log identifies them for cleanup.\n- **Downstream consumers.** If another team cached embeddings or outputs from the bad version, tell them.\n- **State written in a new shape.** If the new version stored tool results, memory records or structured\n  outputs in a new schema, the old version may fail to read them. Use expand-then-contract, as with\n  database migrations: a release writes data the previous manifest can still read, and drops the old\n  shape only after its bake window.\n\n### Rehearse it\n- **A rollback never executed is a hypothesis.** Exercise it in CI (deploy v2, roll back to v1, run a\n  smoke eval) and in periodic game days.\n- **Time it** against the MTTR target in the runbook.","example":"### Registry with immutable manifests and a guarded rollback\nA registry with immutable manifests, an environment alias, and a guard loop that rolls back when a metric\nbreaches its threshold with enough samples. Promotion records history so rollback has a validated target.\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Manifest:\n    id: str; model: str; prompt: str; params: dict; tools: str; index: str; guardrails: str\n\n@dataclass\nclass Registry:\n    manifests: dict = field(default_factory=dict)\n    alias: dict = field(default_factory=dict)      # env -> manifest id\n    history: dict = field(default_factory=dict)    # env -> [manifest ids that served]\n    promoted_at: dict = field(default_factory=dict)\n\n    def promote(self, env: str, mid: str):\n        assert mid in self.manifests, \"unknown manifest\"\n        self.history.setdefault(env, []).append(mid)\n        self.alias[env] = mid                      # O(1): a pointer move, nothing rebuilt\n        self.promoted_at[env] = time.time()\n        cache.invalidate(namespace=env)            # cached answers belonged to the old version\n\n    def rollback(self, env: str) -> str:\n        served = self.history[env]\n        assert len(served) >= 2, \"nothing validated to roll back to\"\n        served.pop()                               # drop the bad one\n        self.alias[env] = served[-1]\n        cache.invalidate(namespace=env)\n        return served[-1]\n\nGUARDS = {\"schema_fail_rate\": (0.02, 200), \"p95_ms\": (6000, 200), \"refusal_rate\": (0.10, 200)}\nBAKE_S = 48 * 3600                                 # auto-rollback only while the release is fresh\n\ndef check_guards(reg: Registry, env: str, window: dict) -> str | None:\n    for metric, (limit, min_n) in GUARDS.items():\n        if window[\"n\"] >= min_n and window[metric] > limit:\n            if time.time() - reg.promoted_at[env] > BAKE_S:\n                alerts.page(f\"{env}: {metric} breach outside bake window; human decision\")\n                return None\n            target = reg.rollback(env)\n            alerts.page(f\"{env}: {metric}={window[metric]:.3f} > {limit}; rolled back to {target}\")\n            return target\n    return None\n```\n\n### Pre-release rollback-readiness check\nRun in CI before promotion: it fails the release if the previous manifest could not actually be\nrestored. Pure Python; the inventory dictionaries stand in for registry, vendor and index lookups.\n\n```python\ndef rollback_blockers(prev: dict, new: dict, live: dict) -> list[str]:\n    problems = []\n    if prev[\"model\"] not in live[\"models\"]:\n        problems.append(f\"previous model {prev['model']} no longer served\")\n    if prev[\"index\"] not in live[\"indexes\"]:\n        problems.append(f\"previous index {prev['index']} deleted\")\n    if new[\"index\"] == prev[\"index\"] and new[\"embedder\"] != prev[\"embedder\"]:\n        problems.append(\"embedding model changed on the same index: in-place re-embed\")\n    if not new.get(\"cache_key_has_manifest\"):\n        problems.append(\"response cache not keyed by manifest id\")\n    return problems\n\nprev = {\"model\": \"m-2026-05\", \"index\": \"kb-v7\", \"embedder\": \"e1\"}\nnew = {\"model\": \"m-2026-09\", \"index\": \"kb-v7\", \"embedder\": \"e2\", \"cache_key_has_manifest\": False}\nlive = {\"models\": {\"m-2026-05\", \"m-2026-09\"}, \"indexes\": {\"kb-v7\"}}\nfor p in rollback_blockers(prev, new, live):\n    print(\"BLOCK:\", p)\n# BLOCK: embedding model changed on the same index: in-place re-embed\n# BLOCK: response cache not keyed by manifest id\n```\n\n### Worked incident: a rollback that did not roll back\nIllustrative timeline for a release that changed the prompt and swapped the model alias.\n\n| Time | Event | What it reveals |\n|---|---|---|\n| T+0 | manifest m42 promoted (new prompt, model alias left floating) | the model was not pinned to a snapshot |\n| T+3 h | thumbs-down rate rises; refusal guard breaches at n = 400 | inside the 48 h bake window, so auto-rollback fires |\n| T+3 h 2 min | alias moved to m41; cache namespace purged | pointer flip took minutes, as designed |\n| T+4 h | quality still degraded | m41 also pointed at the floating alias, which had moved underneath |\n| T+5 h | m41 re-pinned to the old snapshot and re-promoted | rollback only works on fully pinned manifests |","patterns":["**Alias-based model registry.** Point serving at an alias, not a version number: MLflow's registry lets you set an alias such as `champion` on a model version (`MlflowClient().set_registered_model_alias`), and serving resolves the alias. Rollback is re-setting the alias to the previous version. Monitor which version each replica actually resolved.","**GitOps revert as the rollback button.** Keep the `prod` manifest pointer in a Git repo synced by Argo CD or Flux; rollback is reverting the promoting commit, which leaves an audit trail. Monitor sync status and time from revert to fully synced.","**Index aliases for blue/green retrieval.** Elasticsearch and OpenSearch index aliases (the `_aliases` API) and Qdrant collection aliases let you build `kb-v8` beside `kb-v7` and swap atomically. Keep the old index through the bake window. Monitor recall on a fixed query set after the swap.","**Version in every cache key.** Prefix response and semantic cache keys with the manifest id, so a rollback naturally misses the bad version's entries instead of relying on a purge. Monitor cache hit rate dropping right after promotion or rollback, which confirms the keying works.","**Kubernetes undo for self-hosted serving.** For model servers deployed as Deployments, `kubectl rollout undo deployment/<name>` returns to the previous ReplicaSet, which is fast only if the old image and weights are still cached on nodes. Monitor pod readiness time during drills.","**Scheduled rollback drills.** A CI job that deploys v2, rolls back to v1 and runs a smoke eval, plus a quarterly game day. Monitor time-to-rollback against the MTTR target."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    B[\"Guard breach, n at least min sample\"] --> W{\"Inside bake window?\"}\n    W -->|\"yes\"| F[\"Move prod alias to previous manifest\"]\n    W -->|\"no\"| H[\"Page a human\"]\n    H --> X{\"Unchanged version degraded too?\"}\n    X -->|\"yes\"| E[\"External cause: no rollback\"]\n    X -->|\"no\"| F\n    F --> C[\"Purge or re-key caches\"]\n    C --> A[\"Reconcile side effects from audit log\"]\n    A --> V[\"Smoke eval on restored manifest\"]","caption":"A guard breach takes one of two paths depending on release age; the pointer flip is followed by the\ncleanup a flip cannot do on its own."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is rolling back an LLM application harder than rolling back a container?","a":"- **The unit is a bundle in many stores:** prompt registry, provider model, vector index, tool schemas,\n  guardrail config. Reverting one piece creates an unevaluated combination.\n- **Some changes are irreversible unless planned:** in-place re-embedding, overwritten adapters, vendor\n  snapshot retirement.\n- **So the precondition is design work:** immutable manifests, an alias per environment, and previous\n  artefacts kept reachable and warm."},{"q":"You rolled back and users still get bad answers. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check caches first:** a response or semantic cache not keyed by manifest id keeps serving the bad\n  version's answers until TTL.\n- **Check the manifest is fully pinned:** a floating model alias can make the \"old\" manifest run the new\n  model.\n- **Check propagation:** confirm every data-plane replica refreshed its config and resolved the old\n  manifest, using the manifest id on traces."},{"q":"When would you not auto-roll back on a guard breach?","a":"- **Outside the bake window**, because a late breach is more often external (provider incident, traffic\n  shift, broken upstream tool) and the old version shares that dependency.\n- **On noisy quality signals with small samples**, where auto-rollback flaps and erodes trust; route those\n  to a human with a minimum sample.\n- **When the previous version is itself broken**, for example retired by the vendor; fix forward instead."},{"q":"What does it cost to keep rollback instant for a self-hosted 70B model?","a":"- **A warm previous version means duplicate capacity:** the old weights stay loaded or a second pool stays\n  up, roughly doubling serving memory during the bake window.\n- **Cold rollback is cheaper but slower:** loading tens of gigabytes from object storage can take minutes,\n  which may be the whole recovery budget.\n- **Middle ground:** keep weights on local disk of existing nodes, so a reload avoids object storage, and\n  drop them after the bake window."},{"q":"How do you make a new structured-output schema safe to roll back?","a":"- **Expand then contract:** the release writes data the previous manifest can still read (additive\n  fields, both shapes for a while).\n- **Drop the old shape** only after the release is past its bake window.\n- **Test it:** the CI drill rolls back after writing records with the new version and checks the old\n  version reads them."}],"tradeoffs":["**Warm previous model vs cost.** Instant rollback costs GPU memory or a second serving pool; for infrequent releases a slower cold rollback may be the cheaper choice.","**Automatic rollback vs trust.** It shortens time-to-recover at night, but with thresholds lacking minimum samples or noisy metrics it rolls back healthy releases and trains people to ignore it.","**Bundle-level rollback: safe vs coarse.** The target was validated, but it also reverts an unrelated good change that shipped in the same manifest, which argues for small manifests.","**Blue/green indexes: reversible vs double storage.** During the bake window you store both; for very large corpora that cost can dominate and force a partial-rebuild strategy.","**Immutable manifests and aliases: discipline vs overhead.** Every team pays registry overhead, but it is what makes \"which version served this request\" answerable at all."],"pitfalls":["**Re-embedding the corpus in place.** Symptom: a retrieval regression after a model change with no way back except a multi-hour rebuild. Fix: blue/green index plus alias.","**Caches not keyed by version.** Symptom: \"we rolled back but users still see the bad answers\" for as long as the cache TTL. Fix: manifest id in the key, or purge on rollback.","**Relying on the vendor to keep serving the previous model.** Symptom: a rollback target returning model-not-found during the incident. Fix: track deprecation dates and keep a reachable fallback.","**Rolling back only the prompt because \"the model did not change\".** The model alias silently updated underneath. Symptom: a rollback that does not restore old behaviour. Fix: pin snapshots in manifests.","**Never rehearsing.** Symptom: the runbook fails on a permissions error at the exact moment it is needed. Fix: drills in CI and game days.","**Treating rollback as the whole fix.** Symptom: memory entries, tickets or emails from the bad version surface days later because nobody reconciled the audit log. Fix: a reconciliation step in the runbook.","**Auto-rolling back on every breach regardless of release age.** Symptom: a good release reverted during a provider outage, with the outage still present on the old version. Fix: bake-window scoping."],"when":"- **Roll back first** whenever a release correlates with a guard breach or credible quality regression;\n  diagnose second. Being wrong costs little if the previous version was validated, while debugging live\n  is paid for by users.\n- **Fix forward instead** only when the previous version is itself broken (a vendor retired it, an index\n  was overwritten) or the fault is clearly outside the release.\n- **Quick external-cause test:** if the incumbent arm of a still-running canary, or a version that has not\n  changed, shows the same degradation, the release is not the cause and rollback will not help.\n- **Measure:** time-to-rollback in rehearsals, kept in minutes; make the trigger list part of every\n  release plan. Day to day: manifests in a registry, aliases in a control plane and a rollback button\n  engineers trust.\n- **Interviews:** explain why an AI rollback is harder than a container rollback and what must be true\n  beforehand for a pointer flip to actually restore behaviour.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds releases as immutable manifests, keys caches by manifest id, keeps the previous weights or index reachable, and wires guard metrics to the rollback action.","architect":"Defines the deployable unit, the retention policy for previous versions (how many, how warm), and which triggers are automatic versus human, balancing recovery time against cost.","fde":"Agrees rollback triggers and the rollback runbook with the customer before go-live, and verifies in the customer's environment that the previous version is actually still deployable."},"check":{"q":"A release changed the prompt and switched the embedding model, re-indexing the corpus in place.\nTwo days later answer quality drops. Walk through what rollback can and cannot do here and what you\nwould change for next time.","a":"The prompt can be rolled back instantly if it is versioned in a registry and the environment\nalias can be repointed. The index cannot: re-embedding in place destroyed the old vectors, and the\nquery side cannot go back to the old embedding model either, because old-model query vectors are\nmeaningless against new-model document vectors. So a prompt-only rollback runs the old prompt against\nretrieval from the new model, a combination never evaluated and possibly worse. The honest options are to rebuild the old index from source (hours),\nor to keep the new index and fix the prompt forward with a proper canary. For next time: treat the\nbundle as one manifest, build the new index blue/green beside the old and switch an alias, keep the old\nindex through a bake window, key any response cache by manifest id, and define the quality trigger\nand minimum sample before release so the decision happens on day one rather than day two."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is \"roll back the prompt but keep the new model\" risky even when the model change looked harmless?","options":["Prompt registries cannot store more than one version per model","It produces a combination that was never evaluated, so it is a forward change rather than a return to a known-good state","Models cache the prompt, so the old prompt will not take effect","It doubles inference cost during the transition"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A rollback is only safe because its target previously passed gates and served traffic. Mixing components from different manifests creates a new, unvalidated version under incident pressure."},{"q":"After rolling back, users keep receiving the bad version's answers for an hour. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The rollback pointer takes an hour to propagate through DNS","The old model needs an hour to warm its KV cache","A response or semantic cache was keyed without the version id, so it still serves answers produced by the bad version","The guard metrics window is one hour long"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rollback restores the code path, not the data it produced. A cache filled by the bad version keeps serving until its TTL unless caches are keyed by manifest or purged on rollback."},{"q":"Which practice most directly ensures that a retrieval-index change can be rolled back?","options":["Storing the embedding model name in the prompt","Lowering the index's HNSW parameters so rebuilds are faster","Building the new index beside the old one and switching an alias, keeping the old index during a bake window","Re-embedding only documents that changed since the last release"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Blue/green indexing preserves the old vectors, so rollback is an alias switch. In-place re-embedding overwrites them and turns rollback into a multi-hour rebuild."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/model-monitoring","sources":["Sculley et al., 2015 — Hidden Technical Debt in Machine Learning Systems","Breck et al., 2017 — The ML Test Score","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"- **Correctness is not a status code.** The model confidently cites the wrong refund policy; the retriever\n  returns last year's price list; the agent stops calling the search tool because the vendor quietly\n  updated the model and it now answers from memory. All HTTP 200, p95 within budget, dashboards green.\n- **Ground truth is late or absent.** A bank's classifier gets labels when the loan defaults, months\n  later; a support assistant never learns whether the problem was solved unless you build the loop. So\n  you cannot monitor \"accuracy\" the way you monitor error rate.\n- **Each naive alternative covers one corner.**\n  - \"We have evals in CI\" measures a fixed set at release time; production is a different, changing\n    distribution, and the model can change underneath a provider alias.\n  - \"We watch user complaints\" is sparse, biased toward the angriest users and days late.\n  - \"We log everything\" produces terabytes nobody reads.\n- **What monitoring buys:** a continuous, cheap, per-segment answer to \"is the system behaving as it did\n  when we validated it?\", with a path from any alert to the concrete requests behind it.","how":"### The three layers at a glance\n| Layer | Examples | Coverage | Label latency |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Operational | latency, errors, tokens, cost | 100% | seconds |\n| 2. Behavioural proxies | refusals, schema failures, length, tool-call mix | 100% | seconds |\n| 3. Quality and outcomes | judge scores, feedback, business metrics | sampled or sparse | minutes to days |\n\n### Layer 1: operational signals\n- **The ordinary SRE set:** request rate; error rate by class (provider 429s, 5xxs, timeouts, guardrail\n  blocks); latency as time-to-first-token and total; input and output tokens; cost per request; tool-call\n  failures and retries.\n- **AI-specific labels on every series:** model id, prompt version, bundle id and tenant, *because* the\n  first question after any anomaly is \"which version, which customer\".\n\n### Layer 2: behavioural proxies (no labels needed)\nCheap functions of the output, computed on 100% of traffic:\n- **Refusal rate** (a small classifier or pattern match) and \"I don't know\" rate.\n- **Schema-validation failure rate** for structured outputs.\n- **Output length distribution**, plus empty or truncated responses (finish reason) and repetition.\n- **Language mismatch** between input and output.\n- **Tool-call rate and which tools.**\n- **RAG signals:** citation presence and retrieval score distribution.\n\n- **None of these is quality; their *shift* is informative.** If refusal rate doubles after a model\n  update, something changed, and you know within minutes, not after the weekly feedback review.\n- **Chosen *because* they are deterministic, free, and move early.**\n\n### Layer 3: quality and outcome signals\n- **Online judge.** An LLM scores a sample of live responses against a rubric (groundedness, completeness,\n  policy adherence). Sampled *because* a judge call is another inference over the full prompt and\n  response, often comparable to or more than the request it scores. 1–5% is a common order of magnitude\n  (illustrative), stratified so small tenants are still covered.\n- **Human review queues.** Low-judge-score or flagged traces go to reviewers; the labels calibrate the\n  monitor and grow the golden set.\n- **Explicit feedback.** Thumbs, ratings, \"report\". Sparse and selection-biased, so track negative\n  feedback per hundred responses, not raw count.\n- **Implicit feedback.** Regenerate, copy, abandon, escalate to human, task completion. Higher volume,\n  less precise; correlate once with human labels to learn what each means.\n- **Business outcomes.** Resolution rate, conversion, handle time: the slowest signal and the one leaders\n  believe.\n\n| Signal | Label latency |\n|---|---|\n| Implicit feedback | seconds |\n| Explicit feedback | minutes |\n| Online judge | minutes to hours |\n| Business metrics | days |\n\n- **Design principle:** use fast proxies to decide where to spend slow, expensive labels.\n\n### Slicing, baselines and alerting\n1. **Slice everything** by version, tenant, intent class, language and input-length bucket, because\n   aggregates hide regressions: a 2% drop in overall judge score can be a 40% drop for one tenant or\n   intent.\n2. **Compare against a time-controlled baseline:** the same hour last week, or the incumbent in a\n   concurrent canary.\n3. **Alert on sudden shifts and slow trends separately.**\n4. **Require a minimum sample per window**, *because* judged metrics on thirty requests are noise.\n5. **Route by type:** operational breaches page; quality shifts open a ticket with the top disagreeing\n   examples attached.\n\n**The multiple-testing cost of slicing.** Every slice is another hypothesis test. With 200 slices checked\nhourly at a threshold that fires falsely 5% of the time, expect about ten false alerts per run from noise\nalone. Two defences:\n- **Scale the threshold to the slice's standard error:** a drop must exceed roughly two standard errors\n  of the difference, so small slices need bigger drops.\n- **Require persistence** across consecutive windows before alerting.\n\n### From proxies to SLOs\n- **Write key proxies as SLOs:** \"99% of extraction responses pass schema validation over 28 days\", or\n  \"refusal rate on the billing intent stays below 3%\".\n- **Each gets an error budget;** alert on how fast it burns, not on single bad windows.\n- **Fast burn pages** (a large fraction of the month's budget gone in an hour); **slow burn opens a\n  ticket.**\n- **Why it works:** it is the same machinery used for availability, applied to behaviour, and it stops\n  quality alerting from being either silent or noisy.\n\n### Close the loop\n- **Derive every metric from traces**, so an alert drills into the requests behind it.\n- **Turn every reviewed failure into a golden-set case.** Monitoring that does not feed the eval suite\n  finds the same regression twice.","example":"### Windowed monitor per bundle and tenant\nA windowed monitor over logged events: proxies on all requests, judge scores on the sampled subset,\ncompared per version against a baseline window with a minimum-sample guard.\n\n```python\nimport math\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nfrom statistics import mean, stdev\n\nMIN_N = 200                       # no verdicts on tiny windows\nLIMITS = {\"refusal_rate\": 0.05, \"schema_fail_rate\": 0.02, \"judge_drop\": 0.3}\n\ndef summarise(events):            # events: dicts from the trace store, one per request\n    by = defaultdict(list)\n    for e in events:\n        by[(e[\"bundle\"], e[\"tenant\"])].append(e)\n    out = {}\n    for key, es in by.items():\n        judged = [e[\"judge\"] for e in es if e.get(\"judge\") is not None]   # sampled subset\n        out[key] = {\"n\": len(es),\n                    \"refusal_rate\": mean(e[\"refused\"] for e in es),\n                    \"schema_fail_rate\": mean(not e[\"schema_ok\"] for e in es),\n                    \"p95_ms\": sorted(e[\"ms\"] for e in es)[math.ceil(0.95 * len(es)) - 1],  # nearest rank\n                    \"cost_per_req\": mean(e[\"cost\"] for e in es),\n                    \"judge_mean\": mean(judged) if judged else None, \"judge_n\": len(judged),\n                    \"judge_sd\": stdev(judged) if len(judged) > 1 else None}\n    return out\n\ndef judge_dropped(c, b, z=2.0):   # drop must be large relative to its own noise\n    se = math.sqrt(c[\"judge_sd\"] ** 2 / c[\"judge_n\"] + b[\"judge_sd\"] ** 2 / b[\"judge_n\"])\n    return b[\"judge_mean\"] - c[\"judge_mean\"] > max(LIMITS[\"judge_drop\"], z * se)\n\ndef alerts(current, baseline):\n    found = []\n    for key, c in current.items():\n        if c[\"n\"] < MIN_N:\n            continue\n        b = baseline.get(key)\n        for m in (\"refusal_rate\", \"schema_fail_rate\"):\n            if c[m] > LIMITS[m] and (b is None or c[m] > 2 * b[m]):\n                found.append((key, m, c[m], \"page\"))\n        if b and c[\"judge_n\"] >= 50 and b[\"judge_n\"] >= 50 and judge_dropped(c, b):\n            found.append((key, \"judge_mean\", c[\"judge_mean\"], \"ticket+examples\"))\n    return found\n# Run hourly: alerts(summarise(last_hour), summarise(same_hour_last_week)).\n# With sd ≈ 0.8 and 50 judged per side, se ≈ 0.16, so only drops above ~0.32 fire: the\n# floor of 50 judged requests is what bounds the smallest regression this slice can see.\n```\n\n### Burn-rate SLO and slice false alarms, in numbers\nPure Python for the SLO \"99% of extraction responses pass schema validation over 28 days\".\n\n```python\nSLO = 0.99\nWINDOW_H = 28 * 24                    # 672 hours in the SLO window\nbudget = 1 - SLO                      # 1% of requests may fail\n\ndef burn_rate(failed: int, total: int) -> float:\n    return (failed / total) / budget  # 1.0 means the budget lasts exactly the window\n\nfor label, failed, total in [(\"normal hour\", 9, 1200), (\"bad release\", 200, 1200)]:\n    r = burn_rate(failed, total)\n    print(f\"{label}: burn {r:.2f}x, budget gone in {WINDOW_H / r:.0f} h\")\n\nslices, false_rate = 200, 0.05\nprint(f\"false alerts per run, one window: {slices * false_rate:.1f}\")\nprint(f\"same, two consecutive windows: {slices * false_rate ** 2:.1f}\")   # if windows independent\n# normal hour: burn 0.75x, budget gone in 896 h\n# bad release: burn 16.67x, budget gone in 40 h\n# false alerts per run, one window: 10.0\n# same, two consecutive windows: 0.5\n```\n\n| Situation | Number | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| Normal hour, 9 of 1,200 fail | burn 0.75x | nothing |\n| Bad release, 200 of 1,200 fail | burn 16.67x, budget gone in 40 h | page (fast burn) |\n| 200 slices, single-window threshold | ~10 false alerts per run | too noisy to page on |\n| Same, persistence over 2 windows | ~0.5 per run (independence assumed) | ticket-worthy |","patterns":["**Version and model tags from the OpenTelemetry GenAI conventions.** Record `gen_ai.request.model` and `gen_ai.response.model` on each LLM span, plus token usage attributes; the response model shows the resolved snapshot behind an alias, which is how an overnight vendor update becomes visible. Monitor distinct response-model values per bundle.","**Bounded-cardinality metric labels.** Put bundle and model ids on Prometheus series, but keep high- cardinality dimensions (per-tenant for thousands of tenants, request ids) in traces or a columnar store and aggregate there. Monitor active series count per metric.","**Stratified judge sampling with a per-slice floor.** Sample a fixed percentage overall but guarantee a minimum number of judged requests per tenant and intent per day, so small tenants clear the 50-judged floor. Monitor judged count per slice against the floor.","**Multiwindow burn-rate alerts.** The Google SRE Workbook's approach pages when a short and a long window both show a high burn rate (its example pages at about 2% of a 30-day budget in one hour, a burn rate of 14.4) and tickets on slow burns. Apply it to schema-pass and refusal SLOs. Monitor alert precision after each incident review.","**Pinned, calibrated judge.** Pin the judge model snapshot and re-measure its agreement with human labels on a fixed set on a schedule and whenever the judge changes. Monitor judge-human agreement; a step change in every quality series on one day usually means the judge moved.","**Alert-to-eval loop.** Every confirmed quality ticket exports its traces as new golden-set cases before closing. Monitor the share of incidents that produced at least one new eval case."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Live request\"] --> T[(\"Trace with bundle, model, tenant tags\")]\n    T --> O[\"Layer 1: ops metrics, 100%\"]\n    T --> P[\"Layer 2: proxies, 100%\"]\n    T --> S[\"Stratified sampler\"]\n    S --> J[\"Layer 3: online judge, 1-5%\"]\n    J --> HR[\"Human review queue\"]\n    O --> C[\"Slice and compare vs baseline\"]\n    P --> C\n    J --> C\n    C --> G{\"Min sample and persistent?\"}\n    G -->|\"ops breach\"| PG[\"Page\"]\n    G -->|\"quality shift\"| TK[\"Ticket with examples\"]\n    HR --> GS[(\"Golden set\")]\n    TK --> GS","caption":"Every request produces a version-tagged trace; cheap layers cover all of it, the judge covers a stratified\nsample, and only slice-aware, sample-guarded shifts become pages or tickets."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a behavioural proxy and a quality metric, and why monitor both?","a":"- **A proxy** is a cheap, deterministic function of the output (refusal rate, schema failures, length,\n  tool-call mix) computed on 100% of traffic; its shift says something changed, not whether it got worse.\n- **A quality metric** (judge score, human label, outcome) measures goodness but is sampled, slow or sparse.\n- **Together:** proxies detect early and point to where to spend expensive judge and human labels."},{"q":"Overall judge score is flat but one enterprise tenant says answers got worse. How do you investigate?","a":"- **Slice by tenant and intent**, because an aggregate can hide a 40% drop in one segment behind a 2%\n  overall move.\n- **Check the slice's sample:** if that tenant has few judged requests, raise its stratified floor before\n  concluding anything.\n- **Pull the lowest-scoring traces** for the tenant, compare with the same hour last week, and check\n  whether its documents, language or intents differ from the golden set."},{"q":"You have 200 tenant-by-intent slices and a 5% false-positive threshold. What goes wrong and how do you fix it?","a":"- **About ten false alerts every run** from noise alone, so the channel gets muted.\n- **Fixes:** thresholds scaled to each slice's standard error, persistence across consecutive windows,\n  and paging only on SLO burn rates; slice anomalies open tickets."},{"q":"Why sample the online judge instead of scoring every response?","a":"- **Cost:** a judge call is another inference over the full prompt and response, often comparable to or\n  more than the request it scores, so 100% judging roughly doubles inference spend.\n- **Enough signal:** 1–5% (illustrative), stratified so small tenants still reach a minimum count, detects\n  meaningful shifts at a fraction of the cost.\n- **Cost of under-sampling:** with sd ≈ 0.8 and 50 judged per side, only drops above about 0.32 are\n  detectable."},{"q":"How would you turn \"schema validity\" into an alert that neither stays silent nor floods?","a":"- **Define an SLO:** for example 99% of extraction responses pass schema validation over 28 days, giving\n  a 1% error budget.\n- **Alert on burn rate:** page on a fast burn in a short and long window, ticket on a slow burn.\n- **Attach examples:** every alert links to the failing traces, and fixed failures become eval cases."}],"tradeoffs":["**1% judge sampling: cheap vs coverage.** A small tenant may get five scored requests a day; stratified sampling fixes coverage at the cost of a more complex sampler.","**Proxies: free and fast vs indirect.** A refusal-rate drop can mean a better model or a jailbroken one, so proxies trigger investigation, not conclusions.","**Fine slicing: catches per-tenant regressions vs alert volume.** It multiplies series and shrinks samples per slice; past a point, anomaly detection on the slice set beats fixed thresholds.","**Online judges: continuous signal vs judge bias and drift.** The judge drifts when its model updates; pin it and re-calibrate against human labels periodically.","**Full prompt and output capture: debuggable vs sensitive.** It creates a data store with retention and access obligations; hashing or redaction trades debuggability for safety."],"pitfalls":["**Monitoring only HTTP health.** Symptom: a week of green dashboards, then a customer escalation about answers wrong since the last model update. Fix: add proxies and sampled judging.","**Alerting on raw judge scores without a sample floor.** Symptom: 3 a.m. pages caused by twelve unlucky requests, then the alert gets muted. Fix: minimum sample and standard-error thresholds.","**Events not tagged with bundle, prompt and model version.** Symptom: a regression you can see but not attribute to a release, so rollback becomes guesswork. Fix: tags on every event.","**Treating thumbs-down counts as representative.** Symptom: optimising for the loudest segment while the silent majority's task completion falls. Fix: rates plus implicit and outcome signals.","**Dashboards without drill-down.** Symptom: \"quality down 8%\" and a two-hour hunt for one example. Fix: derive metrics from traces and attach examples.","**Letting the judge model auto-update.** Symptom: a step change in every quality series on the day the vendor shipped, with no change in your system. Fix: pin the judge.","**Fixed thresholds across hundreds of slices.** Symptom: a daily stream of one-window alerts on small tenants that never reproduce, until the channel is muted. Fix: per-slice standard errors and persistence."],"when":"- **From the first production deployment:** operational metrics and the cheapest proxies (schema\n  failures, refusals, length, finish reason) sliced by version cost almost nothing and catch loud failures.\n- **Add sampled judging** once you have a rubric that correlates with human labels; add feedback capture\n  as soon as the product surface allows.\n- **Invest in slicing** once you have more than a handful of tenants or intents.\n- **Measure what the system is for:** a RAG assistant needs groundedness and citation rate; an agent needs\n  tool-failure and step-count distributions; a structured extractor needs schema validity and field-level\n  agreement on a sample.\n- **Interviews:** distinguish proxies from quality, explain label latency, and show how an alert leads to\n  examples and then to a new eval case.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Emits version-tagged events, implements the proxy checks and the sampler, and builds the drill-down from an alert to the traces that caused it.","architect":"Chooses the signal set per use case, the judge sampling and calibration policy, the slicing dimensions, and which alerts page versus ticket, within the data-retention constraints.","fde":"Agrees with the customer which outcome metric counts as success, wires their feedback channels into the monitor, and reviews the per-tenant slice with them during hypercare."},"check":{"q":"Your support assistant's provider model alias updated overnight. Nothing crashed. Describe how a\nwell-designed monitor would surface the change within hours and what it would show you.","a":"Operational series would show a cost-per-request or latency step change tagged to the same bundle\nid, since the bundle did not change but the model behind the alias did. Behavioural proxies would move\nfirst: output length distribution, refusal rate, tool-call rate or schema-failure rate shifting at the\nsame timestamp across all tenants, which is the signature of an upstream change rather than a user\nmix change. The sampled online judge would show a score shift within a few hours once the minimum\nsample accrues, with the lowest-scoring traces attached for review. Explicit feedback would lag by a\nday. Where the provider returns the resolved model snapshot in response metadata, logging it would show\nthat value changing at the same timestamp, which is the root cause; the fix is to pin snapshots and\ntreat any model change, alias update included, as a release that goes through shadow or canary."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are behavioural proxies such as refusal rate and schema-failure rate monitored on every request while judge scores are sampled?","options":["Judge scores are only valid on requests with user feedback","Proxies are deterministic and nearly free to compute, whereas each judge call is another LLM inference, often as costly as the request it scores","Proxies are more accurate measures of answer quality than judges","Sampling is required by data-protection rules for judged content"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Proxies are cheap functions of the output, so full coverage is affordable; judging adds a second inference per scored request, so it is sampled and used to decide where expensive human labels go."},{"q":"A monitor reports overall judge score unchanged after a release, yet one enterprise customer complains loudly. What design gap most likely explains it?","options":["The judge model was not run at temperature zero","The release did not include a prompt change","Metrics were not sliced by tenant, so a large drop in one tenant was hidden by the aggregate","Explicit feedback was not collected from that customer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Aggregates average across tenants and intents. A regression confined to one segment can be invisible in the overall mean, which is why every series is sliced by version, tenant and intent."},{"q":"Which alert design most reduces false pages from a noisy quality metric?","options":["Alert on the metric's raw value every five minutes","Alert only when a human reviewer confirms the drop","Require a minimum sample per window and compare against a time-matched baseline before alerting","Replace the judge with a keyword match"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Judged metrics vary widely on small samples and with time of day. A sample floor and a time-matched baseline separate real shifts from noise and from weekly seasonality."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/drift","sources":["Gama et al., 2014 — A Survey on Concept Drift Adaptation","Quiñonero-Candela et al., 2009 — Dataset Shift in Machine Learning","Lopez-Paz & Oquab, 2017 — Revisiting Classifier Two-Sample Tests","Chen, Zaharia & Zou, 2023 — How Is ChatGPT's Behavior Changing over Time?"],"why":"- **Every evaluation is a snapshot.** A golden set, a canary and a benchmark all say \"acceptable on\n  *this* distribution at *this* time\"; nothing in that survives a change in either variable.\n- **The world moves quietly.** Users ask about a product launched after indexing; a new segment writes in\n  an untuned language; a regulation flips the right answer to \"can I return this?\" while the question\n  text does not; the vendor retrains the model behind your alias. None raises an error.\n- **Naive responses fail predictably.**\n  - \"Re-run the golden set weekly\" catches regressions on old questions, not failures on new ones, because\n    the set is frozen on the old distribution.\n  - \"Watch the quality metric\" is right in spirit but late: judged quality needs enough samples and labels\n    arrive days later, so Monday's drift alerts the following week.\n  - \"Retrain or re-index on a schedule\" is cost without diagnosis: it fixes some drifts, cannot fix others\n    (a prompt that is now wrong), and tells you nothing about which you had.\n- **The concept separates three questions:** *what* moved (inputs, outcomes, or a component), *how* it\n  moved (abrupt, gradual, seasonal), and *whether it matters* (did the error rate change on the affected\n  slice). Only the third justifies action, but you cannot answer it without the first two.","how":"### The statistical vocabulary\nTreat the system as a function from inputs **X** to outputs **Y**, validated on data from a joint\ndistribution P(X, Y). Drift is any change in it; name which factor moved:\n\n| Type | What changes | Example | Visible without labels? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Covariate shift** (data / input drift) | P(X); P(Y\\|X) stays | support bot gets shipping instead of billing questions; may be correct, but outside validated territory | yes |\n| **Prior / label shift** | P(Y); P(X\\|Y) stays | fraud goes from 1% to 3% of traffic; thresholds tuned for the old base rate are wrong even if scores are unchanged | partly |\n| **Concept drift** | P(Y\\|X) | policy, price, law or product name changes; same input, new correct output | no: the dangerous one, only ground truth reveals it |\n| **Prediction / output drift** | what the system emits | output length or refusal mix moves | yes: cheapest early warning, but ambiguous (happens under all of the above and when the system changes) |\n\n- **For generative systems Y is \"the correct behaviour\".** Label shift then means, for example, the share\n  of requests whose right response is \"escalate to a human\" or \"refuse\" has moved, which matters because\n  prompts and thresholds were tuned for the old mix.\n\n### Drift shape decides the detector (Gama et al. taxonomy)\n| Shape | Typical cause | Detector |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Sudden** | a release, a provider update | short window and a change-point test |\n| **Gradual** | new segment ramping up | long window and a trend test |\n| **Incremental** | slow vocabulary change | long window and a trend test |\n| **Recurring** | seasonality: tax season, Black Friday | baseline from the same season, not last week |\n\n### What drifts in an LLM application that did not drift in classic ML\nIn classic ML the model was yours and frozen; only the world moved. In LLM systems components move too,\neach with its own detector and fix:\n\n| Source | What happens | Detect by | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Upstream model** | provider updates the model behind an alias or changes a default; same prompt, new behaviour across *all* tenants at one timestamp | proxies shifting globally at once; the response's reported model id | pin snapshots; treat upgrades as releases |\n| **Prompt / configuration** | someone edits the system prompt, a tool description or temperature outside the release path | hash the resolved bundle per request; alert on hash change without a deploy | route edits through releases |\n| **Knowledge / corpus** | RAG index falls behind (stale prices, retired policies); answers confidently out of date | document age at retrieval; groundedness against the *current* source of truth, not the index | refresh the corpus |\n| **Embedding** | embedder upgraded but index built with the old one; vectors no longer share a space | collapse in retrieval scores on a fixed probe set | version embeddings with the index; re-index together |\n| **Judge** | the LLM-as-a-judge alias updates; every quality series steps that day | re-score a fixed, human-labelled calibration set on a schedule | re-calibrate and pin |\n| **Feedback loop** | users learn which phrasings work; outputs enter the system's own corpus or training data | only by comparing against held-out, never-served data | keep held-out data |\n\n### Detecting it\nDetection compares a **reference window** (the validated traffic, or the same period last cycle) with a\n**current window**, per feature and per slice:\n\n1. **Choose the signal.**\n   - Categorical features (intent, language, tenant, tool called, finish reason): bucket distribution.\n   - Numeric (input length, retrieval score, latency): buckets or the empirical CDF.\n   - Free text: embed it and compare the embedding clouds.\n2. **Pick a statistic.**\n   - **PSI** (population stability index) for buckets: `PSI = Σ_b (cur_b − ref_b) · ln(cur_b / ref_b)`, a\n     symmetric, interpretable number where 0 means identical.\n   - **PSI cut-offs (credit-risk rule of thumb):** under 0.1 stable, 0.1–0.25 worth a look, above 0.25 a\n     real shift. Convention, not theory: re-derive them from your own history.\n   - **KS test** for continuous features; **Jensen–Shannon divergence** for a bounded, symmetric measure.\n   - **Classifier two-sample test** for embeddings: train a classifier to tell reference from current. AUC\n     near 0.5 means indistinguishable, and its most confident \"current\" examples show *what* moved, which\n     a single divergence number never does.\n3. **Control for noise.** Minimum window sizes, like-with-like comparisons (same weekday, same season),\n   and slicing by tenant and intent, because a global PSI of 0.03 can hide one tenant at 0.6.\n4. **Separate \"the world moved\" from \"the system moved\".** Re-run a **frozen probe set** (a fixed slice of\n   the golden set with known-good outputs) on a schedule and whenever an alert fires. Its inputs never\n   change, so if its scores move, a component changed (model, prompt, embedder, judge); if it holds while\n   production moves, the world changed. This one check halves most drift investigations.\n5. **Separate \"moved\" from \"broke\".** A shift raises priority for slow signals: route more of that slice to\n   judging and human review and check whether its error rate changed. Input drift with stable outcomes is\n   information, not an incident. Concept drift is confirmed only by labels, so the detector decides where\n   to spend them.\n\n### Responding\nDiagnosis comes first because each type has a different fix:\n\n| Diagnosis | Fix |\n|---|---|\n| New inputs | extend the golden set and prompt coverage |\n| Concept drift | update the corpus, policy text or labels, then re-validate |\n| Upstream model change | pin, evaluate, canary |\n| Embedding mismatch | re-index |\n| Judge shift | re-calibrate and pin |\n\n- **Retraining fixes one of these, not all of them.**\n- **Move the reference window forward after the fix**, because the newly validated distribution is the\n  new baseline.","example":"### PSI on intent mix and a classifier two-sample test on embeddings\nTwo detectors on one week of support-assistant traffic: PSI on the intent mix, and a classifier\ntwo-sample test on input embeddings.\n- **The intent shift** is real but moderate.\n- **The embedding test on a synthetic shift** shows what a clearly separable week looks like (AUC ≈\n  0.87–0.88) versus an unshifted one (≈ 0.5 on the same code, give or take cross-validation noise).\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\n\ndef psi(expected, actual, eps=1e-6):\n    \"\"\"Population Stability Index between two bucket distributions (fractions summing to 1).\"\"\"\n    e, a = np.asarray(expected) + eps, np.asarray(actual) + eps\n    return float(np.sum((a - e) * np.log(a / e)))\n\n# Intent mix: reference week vs this week (fractions of requests)\nref = [0.30, 0.25, 0.20, 0.15, 0.10]      # billing, shipping, returns, account, other\ncur = [0.22, 0.20, 0.33, 0.15, 0.10]\nprint(f\"intent PSI = {psi(ref, cur):.3f}\")  # 0.101: 'returns' rose from 20% to 33%; look at that slice\n\ndef embedding_drift_auc(ref_emb, cur_emb):\n    \"\"\"Classifier two-sample test: can a model tell this week's inputs from the reference week's?\"\"\"\n    X = np.vstack([ref_emb, cur_emb])\n    y = np.r_[np.zeros(len(ref_emb)), np.ones(len(cur_emb))]\n    clf = LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000)\n    return cross_val_score(clf, X, y, cv=5, scoring=\"roc_auc\").mean()\n# AUC 0.5 = indistinguishable. Use a few thousand embeddings per window, then inspect the inputs the\n# classifier is most confident are 'current' to see what actually changed.\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nref_emb = rng.normal(0, 1, (2000, 64))\ncur_emb = rng.normal(0, 1, (2000, 64)); cur_emb[:, :8] += 0.6   # shift along a few directions\nprint(f\"embedding drift AUC = {embedding_drift_auc(ref_emb, cur_emb):.2f}\")   # ~0.87-0.88\n# Ceiling for a linear separator: Phi(0.6*sqrt(8)/sqrt(2)) = Phi(1.2) ~ 0.885\n```\n\nReading the PSI by hand:\n\n| Bucket | ref → cur | Contribution |\n|---|---|---|\n| returns | 0.20 → 0.33 | (0.33 − 0.20) · ln(1.65) ≈ 0.065 |\n| billing | 0.30 → 0.22 | (0.22 − 0.30) · ln(0.733) ≈ 0.025 |\n| shipping | 0.25 → 0.20 | ≈ 0.011 |\n| account, other | unchanged | 0 |\n| **Total** | | **≈ 0.10** |\n\n- **Interpretation:** the mix moved noticeably; whether it matters depends on whether the returns slice,\n  now a third of traffic, was well covered by the prompt and golden set.\n\n### Catching configuration drift with a bundle hash\nPure Python: hash the *resolved* bundle on every request and flag any hash that no deploy produced. This\ncatches prompt and parameter edits that bypassed the release path.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json\n\ndef bundle_hash(bundle: dict) -> str:\n    canon = json.dumps(bundle, sort_keys=True, separators=(\",\", \":\"))\n    return hashlib.sha256(canon.encode()).hexdigest()[:12]\n\nreleased = {\"model\": \"m-2026-05\", \"prompt\": \"Answer using the policy docs.\", \"temperature\": 0.2}\ndeployed_hashes = {bundle_hash(released)}           # written by the release pipeline\n\ndef check_request(resolved: dict):                 # None if the hash came from a deploy\n    h = bundle_hash(resolved)\n    return None if h in deployed_hashes else f\"config drift: hash {h} not from any deploy\"\n\nprint(check_request(dict(released)))                           # None\nprint(check_request({**released, \"temperature\": 0.7}))         # flagged: someone edited a default\n```\n\n### Worked triage: three alerts, three different fixes\nIllustrative week for a support assistant; the probe set splits each case in one step.\n\n| Alert | Frozen probe set | Other evidence | Diagnosis | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Refusal rate up on every tenant at 02:00 | dropped | reported model id changed | upstream model drift | pin snapshot, evaluate, canary |\n| Retrieval scores down, citations irrelevant | dropped on retrieval probes | embedder upgraded last night | embedding drift | re-index with the new embedder, or revert it |\n| Returns intent up to 33% (PSI 0.10) | stable | judged error rate on returns unchanged | covariate shift, harmless | add returns cases to the golden set, advance reference |","patterns":["**Resolved-bundle hash on every trace.** Compute a hash over model id, prompt text, tool schemas and decoding parameters at request time and attach it to the trace; alert when a hash appears that the release pipeline did not record. Monitor distinct hashes per environment per day.","**Scheduled frozen probe set.** Run a fixed set of a few dozen to a few hundred golden cases (size is illustrative) daily and on every drift alert, through the full production path. Monitor probe scores as their own time series; any move without a deploy means a component changed.","**Slice-level PSI with smoothing.** Compute PSI per tenant and intent with an epsilon or a pooled \"other\" bucket so new categories do not produce infinities, and rank slices by score instead of alerting on each. Monitor the top-ranked slices weekly and the count of unseen categories.","**Seasonal reference windows.** For recurring traffic, compare against the same weekday or the same period last cycle (month-end, tax season) rather than the previous week. Monitor alert recurrence: an alert that fires every Monday means the baseline is wrong.","**Embedding version stamped on the index.** Store the embedder id in index metadata and check it at query time, refusing or alerting on a mismatch, so an embedder upgrade cannot silently query an old index. Monitor mean top-k retrieval score on a fixed probe query set.","**Library-backed drift reports.** Tools such as Evidently compute per-column drift tests (PSI, KS, Jensen–Shannon and others) between a reference and current dataset; run them as a scheduled job on logged features. Monitor the share of drifted columns per slice, not a single global flag."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Drift alert on a slice\"] --> P[\"Re-run frozen probe set\"]\n    P -->|\"probe scores moved\"| S[\"System changed\"]\n    S --> K[\"Check model id, bundle hash, embedder, judge\"]\n    K --> F1[\"Pin, re-index or re-calibrate\"]\n    P -->|\"probe stable\"| W[\"World changed\"]\n    W --> L[\"Judge and review more of the slice\"]\n    L --> O{\"Error rate changed?\"}\n    O -->|\"no\"| I[\"Information: advance reference window\"]\n    O -->|\"yes\"| F2[\"Update corpus, prompt, golden set\"]\n    F1 --> V[\"Re-validate, advance reference\"]\n    F2 --> V","caption":"The frozen probe set is the first fork: it tells you whether to inspect components or the world, and only\na change in outcomes turns a shift into work."},"interview":[{"q":"Explain covariate shift versus concept drift with an LLM example, and why the difference matters.","a":"- **Covariate shift:** the questions change but each still has the same right answer, for example a\n  support bot suddenly receiving mostly returns questions. Detectable from inputs alone.\n- **Concept drift:** the same question has a new right answer, for example a return window shortened by\n  policy. Inputs look identical, so only labels or a current source of truth reveal it.\n- **Why it matters:** the fixes differ (extend coverage versus update corpus, policy text and labels),\n  and only one can be caught without labels."},{"q":"Every quality metric dropped overnight across all tenants and nobody deployed. What do you check?","a":"- **Re-run the frozen probe set:** if it dropped too, a component changed, not the world.\n- **Then check components in order:** the model id reported in responses (alias update), the resolved\n  bundle hash (an out-of-band prompt edit), the embedder version on the index, and the judge model.\n- **A global, single-timestamp step** is the signature of an upstream change rather than a user-mix change."},{"q":"Your intent PSI is 0.12 for one tenant. Do you retrain, re-prompt or do nothing?","a":"- **None yet.** PSI says inputs moved, not that outcomes got worse; acting on it alone can make the system\n  worse.\n- **Spend labels on the slice:** route more of that tenant to judging and human review and compare its\n  error rate with the reference window.\n- **Act only if outcomes changed;** if they did not, add the new cases to the golden set and advance the\n  reference."},{"q":"When would you use a classifier two-sample test instead of PSI?","a":"- **For high-dimensional inputs** such as text embeddings, where bucketing is meaningless.\n- **When you need to know what moved:** the classifier's most confident \"current\" examples are the new\n  kind of traffic, which a single divergence number never shows.\n- **Cost:** it needs a few thousand embeddings per window and a training run, so it is a scheduled job\n  rather than a per-request check."},{"q":"What does pinning every component cost, and when would you not pin?","a":"- **Gain:** drift becomes attributable, because only the world can move a pinned system.\n- **Cost:** a scheduled upgrade burden and the risk of running on a snapshot the provider is about to\n  retire.\n- **Exception:** a low-stakes internal tool may track a provider alias deliberately, with the frozen probe\n  set and response model id as the safety net."}],"tradeoffs":["**Label-free detectors: cheap and immediate vs blind to concept drift.** PSI, embedding tests and output proxies cannot see it; once ground truth lags by weeks you need both, and the detectors decide where scarce labels go.","**Short vs long windows.** Short windows catch sudden shifts fast but alert on noise; long windows are stable but late. Running both with different thresholds is usually cheaper than tuning one to do both.","**Fine slicing: exposes hidden drift vs shrinking samples.** Each slice multiplies alert series; beyond a few dozen slices, rank slices by drift score rather than alerting on each.","**Pinning every component: attributable vs upgrade burden.** Pinning the snapshot, embedder and judge removes a class of surprises, at the price of scheduled upgrades and the risk of running on a model the provider is about to retire.","**Advancing the reference window: relevant vs normalised drift.** It keeps detectors relevant, but moving it without validating quietly normalises the drift you were supposed to catch."],"pitfalls":["**Treating a drift alert as a defect.** Symptom: retraining or re-prompting for an input shift whose outcomes were fine, and a worse system afterwards. Fix: check outcomes on the slice first.","**Comparing week over week during seasonal traffic.** Symptom: a recurring alert every Monday or month-end that everyone ignores, which then masks a real shift. Fix: seasonal baselines.","**Monitoring inputs and outputs but not components.** Symptom: a global step change with no deploy in the log, traced days later to a provider alias update or judge upgrade. Fix: model id and bundle hash.","**Upgrading the embedding model without re-indexing.** Symptom: retrieval scores collapse on the probe set and answers cite irrelevant documents while the model is unchanged. Fix: version and re-index together.","**Letting the golden set stay frozen while traffic moves.** Symptom: a green eval suite alongside rising complaints from a segment with no representation in it. Fix: feed confirmed shifts into the set.","**PSI with empty buckets and no smoothing.** Symptom: infinite or NaN scores that crash the job or get silently clipped, hiding exactly the new categories that signal drift. Fix: epsilon smoothing."],"when":"- **Every production system needs it**, because every system runs longer than its validation data stays\n  representative.\n- **Day one:** intent and language mix, input length, output proxies, and a hash of the resolved bundle so\n  configuration drift is caught instantly.\n- **Later:** embedding-based input drift once you have a few thousand requests per window; judge\n  re-calibration once you rely on an LLM judge.\n- **Measure per slice and feed back:** each confirmed shift goes into the golden set, because the shift\n  that moved production is exactly the distribution your next evaluation must contain.\n- **Interviews:** distinguish covariate shift from concept drift, explain why model, prompt, corpus,\n  embedder and judge need pinning and hashing, and insist a detected shift is a question about outcomes,\n  not an answer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the window comparisons, bundle hashing and slice-level PSI jobs, and wires a drift score into the sampler so shifted slices get judged and reviewed first.","architect":"Decides which components are pinned, how reference windows are chosen and advanced, and the policy that turns a confirmed shift into a re-validation and a golden-set update.","fde":"Spots customer-side drift first: a new product line, a reorganised knowledge base or a changed policy, and gets it into the corpus and the regression suite before the complaints arrive."},"check":{"q":"A claims-extraction system's accuracy on its monthly human-reviewed sample fell from 94% to 87%.\nInput-length and field-mix PSIs are below 0.05, and no deploy happened. Walk through how you would find\nthe cause and what you would do for each possibility.","a":"First confirm the drop is real: on a review sample of a few hundred cases, seven points is\nwell outside sampling noise, but on fifty it may not be. Stable input statistics then rule out the\nobvious covariate shift, so look at the components and at concept drift. The fastest split is to\nre-run the frozen probe set: if it also dropped, the system changed even though nobody deployed; if it\nheld, the world changed. Check the model id reported in responses and the bundle hash per request: if the\nprovider alias moved, every tenant's proxies will have stepped at one timestamp, and the fix is to pin\nthe previous snapshot, evaluate the new one and canary it. Check the judge or review process: if the\nreviewers' guidelines changed, the \"drop\" is a measurement change. Then test for concept drift: pull\nthe failed cases and see whether the correct extraction changed, for example a new claim form layout\nor a renamed field; inputs can look statistically identical while the mapping changed. That fix is\nprompt and golden-set updates plus re-validation. Finally run the classifier two-sample test on\ndocument embeddings, because a layout change can be invisible to length and field-mix buckets while\nbeing obvious in embedding space."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which kind of drift cannot be detected by any statistic computed on inputs and outputs alone?","options":["Covariate shift, because input distributions are high-dimensional","Prediction drift, because outputs are free text","Concept drift, because the inputs and outputs can look identical while the correct output has changed","Upstream model drift, because the provider does not announce it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Concept drift is a change in P(Y|X): the same question now has a different right answer. Only labels or ground truth can reveal it. Input, output and model-id signals all detect the other types."},{"q":"An intent-mix PSI of 0.10 is reported for one tenant. What is the correct next step?","options":["Roll back to the previous bundle immediately","Retrain or re-prompt, since the distribution has moved","Route that tenant's slice to more judging and review to learn whether outcomes changed","Raise the threshold, since 0.10 is within normal variation for all systems"],"answer":2,"explanation":"PSI says the inputs moved, not that the system is wrong. A shift of this size justifies spending labels on that slice; action follows only if the error rate on the shifted traffic has changed."},{"q":"Why does the classifier two-sample test on embeddings add something a divergence score does not?","options":["It is the only test that works on categorical data","The classifier's most confident \"current\" examples show what moved, not just that something moved","It needs no reference window","It detects concept drift without labels"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A single divergence number says how far apart two windows are. A trained discriminator can be inspected: the inputs it is surest belong to the new window are the new kind of traffic, which is what you need to extend the golden set and prompt."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/model-lifecycle-and-deprecation-policy","sources":["Official docs: OpenAI model deprecations","Official docs: Anthropic model deprecations","Official docs: MLflow Model Registry","Sculley et al., 2015 — Hidden Technical Debt in Machine Learning Systems"],"why":"- **Models do not stay still.** Providers retire snapshots on published schedules and ship successors that\n  behave differently. Self-hosted weights are superseded by a better fine-tune, a cheaper quantisation or a\n  serving-stack security fix. Embedding upgrades invalidate every index built with the old model.\n- **Many things depend on each version.** Dozens of prompts, bundles and tenants, usually built by people\n  who have since moved to another project.\n- **\"Pin and upgrade when convenient\" fails** because supply is not under your control. A pinned provider\n  snapshot has a shutdown date; on it, requests fail or are silently redirected to a different-behaving\n  successor. Teams find out through an incident, migrate a dozen bundles in a week without evaluation, and\n  ship regressions to avoid an outage.\n- **\"Always use the latest alias\" fails the other way:** every provider release becomes an unreviewed\n  production change.\n- **The platform is a provider too.** A platform team serving twenty product teams that retires a\n  fine-tune without notice, freeze and migration path causes the same incident internally. The policy makes\n  \"this version will stop existing\" a planned, evaluated, dated, owned event in both directions of the\n  supply chain.","how":"### States and transitions\nA version moves through a small state machine; the registry lesson covers alias mechanics, this one the\npolicy around them.\n\n| State | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **registered** | artifact and lineage recorded, no claims made |\n| **evaluated** | offline gates passed, evidence attached |\n| **staged** | deployable to pre-production |\n| **canary** | serving a fraction of production |\n| **production** | the champion for one or more aliases |\n| **deprecated** | still served, no new consumers, sunset date set |\n| **retired** | not served; requests fail with a clear error |\n| **archived** | bytes kept for the retention period, then deleted |\n\n- **Transitions are restricted *because* each state is a promise to consumers.** Production → retired is\n  forbidden, not merely discouraged, so nobody can switch off a model that still has callers.\n- **Each transition records who, when and why**, which makes the lifecycle itself an audit trail.\n\n### Evidence per gate\n| Move to | Required evidence |\n|---|---|\n| evaluated | eval report against the current golden set, plus slice results |\n| staged | integration tests against the real serving config |\n| canary | rollback plan and the candidate's monitoring dashboard |\n| production | canary verdict and sign-off from the owning team |\n\n- **The policy fixes *what* is required, not *how good* the numbers must be;** thresholds belong to the\n  use case's evaluation gates. The split exists *because* the lifecycle is platform-wide while quality\n  bars are domain-specific.\n\n### Provider models have a lifecycle too\n- **Register a hosted model as a virtual version** carrying the provider's snapshot id, its announced\n  retirement date if any, and the recommended successor.\n- **Read the provider's current page, never assume a notice period.** Providers publish schedules and\n  typically give weeks to months of notice, but it varies by provider, model tier and GA versus preview,\n  and providers revise the terms.\n- **Mirror every external deprecation as an internal one**, *because* consumers should learn about\n  retirements from one place in one format.\n- **Decide explicitly on auto-upgrade.** Some clouds offer deployments that auto-upgrade to the successor\n  when a version retires; that is a silent model change in production, so the policy must allow or forbid\n  it.\n\n### The deprecation workflow\n1. **Decide and date.** Triggers: provider sunset, a validated successor, a security or compliance finding,\n   cost, or a version with no owner. Set the sunset at least the policy's notice period ahead, except\n   security retirements, which have an expedited path.\n2. **Announce.** Mark the version deprecated in the registry and notify every consumer through the channel\n   they registered. Targeted, not broadcast, *because* \"we emailed everyone\" is how migrations get missed.\n3. **Inventory consumers.** \"Who still calls this version\" comes from gateway per-request telemetry and\n   bundle-registry pins, never from asking around. Each consumer gets a tracked migration item with an\n   owner.\n4. **Freeze.** From a fixed date before sunset, no new bundle may pin the version and CI fails such pins;\n   existing traffic continues. This stops the list growing while you shrink it.\n5. **Migrate with evaluation.** Each consumer evaluates the successor on its own golden set, fixes prompts\n   where behaviour changed, and promotes through the normal canary path. The scheduled model-upgrade job\n   from the CI/CD lesson is the mechanism; the policy makes it mandatory.\n6. **Brownout.** In the last weeks, make the version unavailable for short, announced windows. Consumers\n   who ignored every notice find the dependency in a planned ten-minute window instead of at sunset,\n   *because* an error in a test window costs less than the same error on retirement day.\n7. **Retire and archive.** Serving stops; requests return a specific error naming the successor. Weights,\n   config and evaluation evidence stay archived for the policy's retention period, *because* audits,\n   incident investigations and reproducibility need to re-run the model in production on a given date.\n   Deletion is its own dated, recorded event.\n\n**\"Same quality on the golden set\" is not the whole migration check.** A successor usually also changes:\n- **Tokenizer**, so token counts, cost and effective context length move.\n- **Latency profile, rate limits and quota.**\n- **Refusal behaviour.**\n- **Structured-output and tool-calling support, and default parameters.**\n\nThe checklist covers each, *because* the regressions that slip through are typically a cost or a 429\nsurprise, not a quality drop.\n\n**Provider models cannot be archived as weights.** Once the vendor retires a snapshot, its behaviour can\nnever be re-run. Archive the bundle (prompts, parameters, tool schemas), the evaluation evidence and logged\ninputs and outputs on a representative sample, captured *before* the sunset while the model still answers.\n\n### Compatibility promises\n- **State what consumers may assume across versions:** which response fields are stable, whether output\n  format is guaranteed, and that behaviour is *not* guaranteed identical.\n- **Bound the support window.** Many platforms adopt an \"N−1\" or \"N−2\" rule: the current major version and\n  the one or two before it are supported; older ones are deprecated automatically. That bounds how many\n  versions anyone must keep evaluated, monitored and secure at once.","example":"### Lifecycle state machine and deprecation planner\nA lifecycle state machine with restricted transitions, and a deprecation planner that turns a sunset date\ninto dated obligations plus a list of consumers still pinned to the old version. Durations are\nillustrative policy values, not a standard.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date, timedelta\n\nTRANSITIONS = {                       # what may follow each state\n    \"registered\": {\"evaluated\", \"retired\"},\n    \"evaluated\":  {\"staged\", \"retired\"},\n    \"staged\":     {\"canary\", \"retired\"},\n    \"canary\":     {\"production\", \"staged\"},        # back to staged = candidate withdrawn\n    \"production\": {\"deprecated\"},                  # never straight to retired: consumers must move first\n    \"deprecated\": {\"retired\", \"production\"},       # un-deprecate allowed, with a recorded reason\n    \"retired\":    {\"archived\"},\n}\n\ndef move(version, new_state, actor, reason):\n    if new_state not in TRANSITIONS[version[\"state\"]]:\n        raise ValueError(f\"{version['id']}: {version['state']} -> {new_state} not allowed\")\n    version[\"history\"].append((date.today(), version[\"state\"], new_state, actor, reason))\n    version[\"state\"] = new_state\n\nPOLICY = {\"notice_days\": 90, \"freeze_days\": 60, \"brownout_days\": 14}   # illustrative\n\ndef deprecation_plan(version, sunset, consumers):\n    \"\"\"Sunset date -> dated obligations and the consumers still to chase.\"\"\"\n    todo = [c[\"bundle\"] for c in consumers\n            if c[\"pinned_model\"] == version[\"id\"] and not c.get(\"migration_pr\")]\n    return {\"announce_by\": sunset - timedelta(days=POLICY[\"notice_days\"]),\n            \"freeze_new_pins\": sunset - timedelta(days=POLICY[\"freeze_days\"]),\n            \"brownouts_from\": sunset - timedelta(days=POLICY[\"brownout_days\"]),\n            \"sunset\": sunset, \"unmigrated\": todo}\n\nv = {\"id\": \"chat-model@2025-03-01\", \"state\": \"production\", \"history\": []}\nmove(v, \"deprecated\", actor=\"platform-team\", reason=\"provider sunset announced\")\nplan = deprecation_plan(v, date(2026, 12, 15), consumers=[\n    {\"bundle\": \"support-assistant@41\", \"pinned_model\": v[\"id\"]},\n    {\"bundle\": \"claims-extractor@7\", \"pinned_model\": v[\"id\"], \"migration_pr\": 812},\n])\nprint(plan)   # announce_by 2026-09-16, freeze 2026-10-16, brownouts 2026-12-01, unmigrated: support-assistant@41\n```\n\n- **Where the consumer list comes from:** the bundle registry's pins joined with gateway telemetry.\n- **What happens to it:** each unmigrated entry becomes a ticket owned by the bundle's team, and the freeze\n  date is enforced by a CI check on pinned model ids.\n\n### The CI check that enforces the freeze\nPure Python: fail the build if any bundle pins a model past its freeze date. The deprecated list would be\nread from the registry; here it is inline.\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom datetime import date\n\nDEPRECATED = {\"chat-model@2025-03-01\": {\"freeze\": date(2026, 10, 16), \"successor\": \"chat-model@2026-06-01\"}}\n\ndef pin_violations(bundles, today):\n    out = []\n    for name, model in bundles.items():\n        d = DEPRECATED.get(model)\n        if d and today >= d[\"freeze\"]:\n            out.append(f\"{name} pins {model} (frozen since {d['freeze']}); use {d['successor']}\")\n    return out\n\nbundles = {\"support-assistant@42\": \"chat-model@2025-03-01\", \"faq-bot@3\": \"chat-model@2026-06-01\"}\nproblems = pin_violations(bundles, today=date(2026, 10, 20))\nprint(\"\\n\".join(problems) or \"ok\")\nsys.exit(1 if problems else 0)   # non-zero exit fails the pipeline\n```\n\n### Worked migration: the successor that passed every eval\nIllustrative numbers for one bundle moving to a successor model.\n\n| Check | Old snapshot | Successor | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Golden-set score | 4.3 | 4.3 | pass |\n| Input tokens per request (same prompts, new tokenizer) | 2,000 | 2,300 | +15% cost before any price change |\n| Effective context for retrieved chunks | 12 chunks | 10 chunks | prompt budget must be re-tuned |\n| Requests per minute quota on the account | 600 | 300 | 429s at peak unless quota is raised |\n| Tool-call format | supported | supported, stricter schema | two tool definitions need fixing |\n\n- **Takeaway:** quality alone would have promoted it; the checklist caught a cost rise, a context squeeze\n  and a quota cut before the canary did.","patterns":["**Inventory from gateway telemetry.** Group gateway request logs by resolved model id, bundle and owning team over a trailing window (for example 30 days) and join with registry pins; any caller missing from the registry is an unknown consumer to chase. Monitor request share still on the deprecated version, per team, weekly until it reaches zero.","**Registry tags for lifecycle state.** MLflow's registry moved from fixed stages to aliases plus free-form tags; teams record lifecycle state and sunset dates as model-version tags (for example `lifecycle=deprecated`, `sunset=2026-12-15`) that CI and dashboards read. Monitor versions by state and any deprecated version past its sunset still serving.","**Pin check in CI.** A pre-merge job compares every pinned model id with the deprecated list and fails after the freeze date, with an exception file whose entries carry an expiry. Monitor open exceptions and their expiry dates.","**Explicit upgrade policy on cloud deployments.** Azure OpenAI deployments, for example, have a version upgrade setting (auto-upgrade to a new default, upgrade when the current version expires, or no auto-upgrade); set it deliberately per deployment and record it in the bundle. Monitor the reported model version per deployment for unplanned changes.","**Announced brownouts.** In the final weeks, return the retirement error (naming the successor) for short, pre-announced windows, then lengthen them. Monitor which callers hit the error during each window; each one is a consumer the inventory missed.","**Pre-sunset record capture for provider models.** Before the sunset, replay a representative sample through the old snapshot and archive inputs, outputs, bundle and eval evidence. Monitor that the archive exists before retirement is approved."],"diagram":{"code":"stateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> registered\n    registered --> evaluated : offline gates pass\n    evaluated --> staged : integration tests pass\n    staged --> canary : rollback plan ready\n    canary --> staged : candidate withdrawn\n    canary --> production : canary verdict and sign-off\n    production --> deprecated : sunset date set\n    deprecated --> production : un-deprecate with reason\n    deprecated --> retired : consumers migrated\n    registered --> retired : abandoned\n    retired --> archived : retention period starts\n    archived --> [*] : dated deletion","caption":"The happy path runs top to bottom; note there is no edge from production to retired, so every retirement\npasses through deprecated, where consumers are inventoried and migrated."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a lifecycle policy and a deprecation policy?","a":"- **Lifecycle** names the states a version passes through, who may move it and what evidence each forward\n  move needs, from registered to production.\n- **Deprecation** is the exit half: notice period, freeze, consumer inventory, evaluated migration,\n  brownouts, retirement and archival.\n- **Why both:** without the exit half, retirements happen on the provider's calendar as incidents."},{"q":"How do you find every consumer of a model version before retiring it?","a":"- **Join two sources:** bundle-registry pins (declared use) and gateway per-request telemetry (actual use)\n  over a trailing window.\n- **Treat mismatches as findings:** traffic with no registered pin is an unknown consumer; pins with no\n  traffic may be dead bundles to clean up.\n- **Use brownouts as the last net:** short announced outages surface anything both sources missed."},{"q":"Would you let a cloud deployment auto-upgrade to the successor model? When?","a":"- **For low-stakes internal tools, possibly:** it removes the sunset incident at the cost of an unreviewed\n  behaviour change.\n- **Not for customer-facing or regulated flows,** where any model change must pass evaluation and canary.\n- **Either way, decide explicitly** per deployment and log the reported model version so an upgrade is\n  visible."},{"q":"A successor matches the old model on your golden set. What else could break in migration?","a":"- **Cost:** a new tokenizer can raise token counts per request, so spend rises even at the same price.\n- **Capacity:** rate limits and quota may differ, producing 429s at peak.\n- **Behaviour at the edges:** refusal rate, structured-output and tool-calling strictness, default\n  parameters and effective context length, each on the migration checklist."},{"q":"Why keep archived evidence for a provider model you can never run again?","a":"- **Audits and post-mortems** ask what the system did on a given date; the bundle, eval evidence and logged\n  sample outputs are the only record once the snapshot is gone.\n- **Capture it before sunset,** by replaying a representative sample while the model still answers.\n- **Bound it** by the compliance retention period, because the archive is sensitive data."}],"tradeoffs":["**Long notice vs version sprawl.** Consumers get time to evaluate properly, but more versions stay alive, each monitored, patched and paid for. Shorter notice suits versions that are cheap to keep; longer suits migrations needing re-prompting and re-indexing.","**Mirroring provider deprecations: one calendar vs messenger pressure.** The platform relays dates it does not control; when the provider shortens a window, the platform absorbs the pressure.","**Early freeze: shrinks the list vs blocks legitimate needs.** A team may need the old behaviour for one more release; an exception path with an expiry date handles that without reopening the freeze.","**Auto-upgrade deployments: no sunset incident vs unreviewed change.** Every retirement becomes a silent behaviour change; acceptable for low-stakes internal tools, not for regulated or customer-facing flows.","**Long archival: audit and reproducibility vs storage and sensitivity.** It creates a sensitive artifact store; follow the compliance requirement rather than defaulting to \"forever\"."],"pitfalls":["**Learning about a provider retirement from an error.** Symptom: a week of emergency migrations, skipped evaluations and a regression blamed on the new model rather than the process. Fix: mirror provider schedules internally.","**Deprecating by broadcast email.** Symptom: sunset day reveals several unknown consumers, found only because their error rate went to 100%. Fix: targeted notice plus brownouts.","**Retiring without an inventory.** Symptom: the platform team asks in chat \"does anyone still use model X?\" and is wrong. Fix: telemetry joined with pins.","**No freeze window.** Symptom: the migration list grows during the migration as new bundles keep pinning the version. Fix: CI pin check from the freeze date.","**Retiring and deleting the artifact in one step.** Symptom: an audit or post-mortem needs last quarter's behaviour and cannot reproduce it. Fix: archive with a separate, dated deletion.","**Treating an embedding model like a chat model.** Symptom: a successor promoted without re-indexing and retrieval quality collapsing on promotion day. Fix: embedder lifecycle tied to its indexes.","**Validating the successor on quality alone.** Symptom: a migration passes every eval, then blows the monthly cost forecast or hits 429s, because tokenization, pricing or quota differ. Fix: the full migration checklist."],"when":"- **Write the policy as soon as more than one team depends on a model** you serve or pin, or you depend on\n  a provider snapshot in production, which is almost immediately.\n- **Start small:** name the states, forbid dangerous transitions, require an inventory before any\n  retirement, and set notice and freeze periods you can honour.\n- **Measure:** retirements that happened as planned, dated events versus incidents, and consumers still\n  unmigrated at the freeze date.\n- **Day to day:** the upgrade job opening PRs, CI rejecting pins to deprecated versions, and a dashboard of\n  versions by state.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"how would you handle a provider sunset across many teams\"; the strong answer has\n  inventory, freeze, per-consumer evaluation, brownouts and archival, in that order.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Pins snapshot ids in bundles, runs the upgrade job's evaluation when a successor is announced, and adds the CI check that fails builds pinning a deprecated version.","architect":"Owns the state machine, the notice and freeze periods, the retention rule for retired artifacts, and the decision on whether auto-upgrading deployments are permitted anywhere.","fde":"Translates a provider's deprecation into a migration plan the customer can approve, runs the evaluation on the customer's golden set, and schedules the cutover inside their change window."},"check":{"q":"A provider announces that the snapshot behind your most-used model retires in four months. Twelve\nbundles across five teams pin it. Describe the plan, including what you would refuse to do.","a":"First register the deprecation internally with the sunset date and the recommended successor, so\nall five teams see one calendar. Build the inventory from bundle pins joined with gateway telemetry,\nthen open one tracked migration item per bundle with the owning team named. Set a freeze date, say\ntwo months out, after which CI rejects new pins to the old snapshot. Each team evaluates the successor\nagainst its own golden set and canaries it through the normal path, because the successor's behaviour\nis not identical; the platform provides the upgrade job but does not do the evaluation for them. In\nthe last weeks, run announced brownouts to flush unknown consumers. On sunset, retire with an error\nnaming the successor and archive evidence for the retention period. Refuse to enable silent\nauto-upgrade on customer-facing bundles and refuse to skip evaluation to meet the date; if a team\ncannot migrate in time, the fallback is a short, dated exception, not an unreviewed switch."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a sound lifecycle forbid moving a version directly from production to retired?","options":["Retired versions cannot be archived without a deprecated step in the history","The deprecated state is where consumers are inventoried and migrated; skipping it switches off a model that still has callers","Provider APIs require a deprecation flag before a snapshot can be deleted","It prevents the registry from reusing the version number"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Deprecated is a serving state with a sunset date, during which the consumer list shrinks to zero under observation. Retiring straight from production skips that work and causes an outage."},{"q":"What is the purpose of a freeze window before a model's sunset?","options":["To stop all traffic to the version so consumers notice early","To give the provider time to prepare the successor","To stop new bundles pinning the version so the migration list cannot grow while it is being worked down","To lock the golden set so evaluations are comparable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Freezing new pins, enforced in CI, bounds the migration; stopping traffic is what brownouts do later, briefly and by announcement, to find consumers the inventory missed."},{"q":"Why keep a retired self-hosted model's weights and evaluation evidence after it stops serving?","options":["So it can be promoted back if the successor fails","Because providers require it for billing reconciliation","Audits and incident investigations may need to reproduce the behaviour that was in production on a given date","To seed the training data for the next fine-tune"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Archival is about reproducibility and accountability for past decisions, for a defined retention period. Rolling back to a retired version is not a goal; the successor has its own rollback target."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/observability-stack","sources":["Official docs: OpenTelemetry Semantic Conventions for Generative AI","Official docs: OpenTelemetry Collector","Sigelman et al., 2010 — Dapper, a Large-Scale Distributed Systems Tracing Infrastructure","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"- **One team, one vendor SDK works; many teams break it in four places at once.**\n  - **No common answers:** each team instruments differently, so nobody can say \"what did we spend on\n    model X across the company\" or \"which tenants did the provider outage hit\".\n  - **Uncontrolled data:** prompts with customer data flow into whichever SaaS a team picked, with\n    retention nobody checked.\n  - **Fragmented requests:** one request through the gateway, application, retriever and three tool calls\n    appears as four unrelated records in four systems.\n  - **No eval join key:** pulling failed traces into a dataset and re-scoring them needs a key shared by\n    the trace store and the eval tool.\n- **\"Pick one vendor for everyone\" trades consistency for lock-in.** Every span is in their format, every\n  dashboard theirs, and migrating means re-instrumenting every service.\n- **\"Reuse the existing APM\" sees latency and errors only.** It has no tokens, cost, prompt versions,\n  judge scores or a way to open a span and read the completion, because classic APM has no notion of a\n  generation.\n- **The stack separates concerns:** a neutral wire format at the edge (instrument once), central policy\n  (redaction, sampling, routing), stores per signal, and LLM analysis tools that consume the data rather\n  than own it. Any alert, invoice line or quality score traces back to its requests, across teams, without\n  data leaving your control.\n- **Scope:** the deep topics live in the Observability module; this lesson is how they fit together as a\n  platform.","how":"### The five layers\n| Layer | Job | Typical owner |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Instrumentation | emit spans with GenAI and platform attributes | application teams |\n| 2. Collection | redact, sample, batch, fan out | platform |\n| 3. Storage by signal | traces, metrics, payloads in separate stores | platform and data governance |\n| 4. LLM analysis and evals | trace viewer, scores, datasets | AI engineering |\n| 5. Metrics, SLOs, alerting | dashboards and pages linked to traces | SRE and product owners |\n\n### Layer 1: instrumentation\n- **Traces, one tree of spans per request:** one span per model call, retrieval, tool call and guardrail\n  check.\n- **OpenTelemetry as SDK and wire format**, *because* it is vendor-neutral and already carries W3C trace\n  context across HTTP and queue boundaries, which makes the gateway's span and the application's span one\n  trace.\n- **GenAI semantic conventions** name the LLM attributes: operation, provider, requested and actual model,\n  input and output token counts, finish reasons, and optionally prompt and completion content as events.\n- **Pin a conventions version and keep a mapping layer**, because the conventions are still evolving and\n  attribute names have changed between versions.\n- **Mandatory platform keys on every span:** tenant, user (pseudonymous), bundle and prompt version, config\n  version, request id. Mandatory *because* every later question is a group-by on them.\n\n### Layer 2: collection\nServices send to an OpenTelemetry Collector, not straight to a backend. Platform policy runs there:\n1. **Redaction:** an attributes or redaction processor strips or hashes payload fields per the tenant's\n   data classification.\n2. **Tail sampling:** keep 100% of traces with errors, low scores or high latency and a small fraction of\n   the rest.\n3. **Batching:** a batch processor reduces write amplification.\n4. **Fan-out:** exporters send the same stream to several backends.\n\n- **Centralised *because*** each application team getting redaction right independently is what fails.\n- **Tail over head sampling *because*** the traces you want are rare and only identifiable once the trace\n  is complete.\n\n**Two easy-to-miss consequences of tail sampling:**\n- **\"Complete\" means \"no new spans for a configured wait\"**, typically seconds, and all spans of a trace\n  must reach the same collector instance (a trace-id-aware load balancer in front). A judge score arriving\n  minutes later, or an agent session trickling spans over an hour, is outside the window. Such signals need\n  their own path, for example keeping all payloads in a short-retention hot store and promoting the ones a\n  later score flags.\n- **Anything you *count* must be derived before the sampler drops spans.** Order the pipelines so metrics\n  and token-usage series come from the full stream; only the trace export is sampled.\n\n### Layer 3: storage, by signal\nThree signals, three stores, chosen by cardinality and retention:\n\n| Signal | Shape | Store | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Traces** | high volume, structured, weeks of retention | trace store | tail-sampled |\n| **Metrics** | low cardinality, long retention | time-series database | derived from spans, unsampled |\n| **Payloads** (prompts, completions) | sensitive, bulky | own store with its own access control and retention | referenced from spans by id |\n\n- **Span-derived metrics:** a spanmetrics connector in the collector derives request rate, error rate and\n  latency histograms per model, bundle and tenant, so nobody hand-instruments counters.\n- **It does not sum attribute values.** Token and cost series come from a token-usage metric emitted by the\n  instrumentation (the GenAI conventions define one) or a job over an *unsampled* copy of the model spans.\n  A job over the tail-sampled trace store undercounts spend by roughly the fraction of normal traces\n  discarded.\n- **Payload size is a budget decision.** A full RAG trace with retrieved chunks can be tens of kilobytes;\n  at a million requests a day that is tens of gigabytes a day (illustrative), so payload retention is not\n  a default.\n\n### Layer 4: LLM-specific analysis and the evaluation loop\n- **Tools:** Langfuse, LangSmith and Arize Phoenix: a trace viewer rendering prompts and completions,\n  sessions and users, prompt-version comparison.\n- **Scores attached to traces are the crucial part:** user feedback, human annotation and LLM-as-a-judge\n  results.\n- **The loop:** filter traces by low score → add them to a dataset → run the candidate bundle against it →\n  compare.\n- **Shared stream:** these tools increasingly accept OpenTelemetry ingestion, so they consume what the\n  collector exports instead of needing their own instrumentation. Self-host versus their cloud is a\n  data-residency decision.\n\n### Layer 5: metrics, SLOs and alerting\n- **Inputs:** latency percentiles, error classes, tokens and cost per tenant and feature, plus behavioural\n  proxies and judge aggregates from the monitoring lesson.\n- **Every alert links to a trace query**, *because* an alert without examples is a two-hour hunt.\n\n### What makes it a platform rather than a tool\n- **Trace context through the gateway**, so cost attribution and debugging work end to end.\n- **One redaction policy and one set of mandatory attributes.**\n- **One place where a judge score and a user's thumbs-down land on the same trace.**\n- **Swappable backends:** change an exporter, not the instrumentation.","example":"### Instrumenting one request with the OpenTelemetry SDK\nA root span for the request, child spans for retrieval and the model call, GenAI attributes on the model\nspan, and the platform's mandatory keys.\n- **`retrieve` and `call_model`** are the application's existing functions.\n- **The exporter sends to a collector** whose address comes from the standard\n  `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` variable.\n- **The completion preview is a custom event**, not a semantic-convention one, because the conventions'\n  content-capture names have changed several times and content capture is opt-in.\n\n```python\nfrom opentelemetry import trace\nfrom opentelemetry.sdk.trace import TracerProvider\nfrom opentelemetry.sdk.trace.export import BatchSpanProcessor\nfrom opentelemetry.exporter.otlp.proto.http.trace_exporter import OTLPSpanExporter\n\nprovider = TracerProvider()\nprovider.add_span_processor(BatchSpanProcessor(OTLPSpanExporter()))   # -> collector, not a vendor\ntrace.set_tracer_provider(provider)\ntracer = trace.get_tracer(\"support-assistant\")\n\nMODEL = \"model-id-from-bundle\"          # pinned in the bundle, never hard-coded here\n\ndef answer(question, tenant, bundle=\"support-assistant@41\", config_version=118):\n    keys = {\"app.tenant\": tenant, \"app.bundle\": bundle,               # mandatory platform keys,\n            \"app.config_version\": config_version}                     # on every span: attributes\n    with tracer.start_as_current_span(\"answer\", attributes=keys):    # are not inherited by children\n        with tracer.start_as_current_span(\"retrieve\", attributes=keys) as s:\n            docs = retrieve(question)\n            s.set_attribute(\"retrieval.doc_count\", len(docs))\n        with tracer.start_as_current_span(f\"chat {MODEL}\", attributes=keys) as s:  # GenAI span name\n            s.set_attributes({\"gen_ai.operation.name\": \"chat\",\n                              \"gen_ai.provider.name\": \"anthropic\",     # 'gen_ai.system' in older versions\n                              \"gen_ai.request.model\": MODEL})\n            resp = call_model(MODEL, question, docs)\n            s.set_attributes({\"gen_ai.response.model\": resp.model,\n                              \"gen_ai.usage.input_tokens\": resp.usage.input_tokens,\n                              \"gen_ai.usage.output_tokens\": resp.usage.output_tokens})\n            ref = payloads.put(tenant, question, docs, resp.text)  # full payload: own store, own TTL\n            s.set_attribute(\"app.payload_ref\", ref)                 # span carries only the pointer\n            s.add_event(\"app.completion_preview\", {\"preview\": resp.text[:200]})  # redacted downstream\n        return resp.text\n```\n\nThe collector pipeline for this stream, in prose:\n1. **An OTLP receiver.**\n2. **A redaction processor** that drops `preview` for tenants classified as confidential.\n3. **Tail sampling** keeping every trace with an error status or latency above the budget plus a 5% sample\n   (illustrative) of the rest.\n4. **A spanmetrics connector** producing per-model, per-bundle rate, error and latency series.\n5. **Exporters** to the trace store, the metrics database and the LLM analysis tool.\n\n### The same pipeline as Collector configuration\nA sketch using components from the OpenTelemetry Collector contrib distribution; check field names against\nthe collector version you run. Two trace pipelines read the same receiver: one feeds spanmetrics from the\nfull stream, the other tail-samples for storage.\n\n```yaml\nreceivers:\n  otlp:\n    protocols:\n      grpc: {}\n      http: {}\nprocessors:\n  attributes/redact:              # span attributes; event-level redaction needs the transform processor\n    actions:\n      - key: enduser.id\n        action: hash\n      - key: app.raw_prompt\n        action: delete\n  tail_sampling:\n    decision_wait: 10s            # \"complete\" = no new spans for this long\n    policies:\n      - name: errors\n        type: status_code\n        status_code: {status_codes: [ERROR]}\n      - name: slow\n        type: latency\n        latency: {threshold_ms: 8000}\n      - name: baseline\n        type: probabilistic\n        probabilistic: {sampling_percentage: 5}\n  batch: {}\nconnectors:\n  spanmetrics:\n    dimensions:                   # bounded-cardinality labels only\n      - name: gen_ai.request.model\n      - name: app.bundle\nexporters:\n  otlphttp/traces:\n    endpoint: https://traces.internal.example:4318\n  prometheusremotewrite:\n    endpoint: https://metrics.internal.example/api/v1/write\nservice:\n  pipelines:\n    traces/all:                   # unsampled: every span counts toward metrics\n      receivers: [otlp]\n      processors: [attributes/redact, batch]\n      exporters: [spanmetrics]\n    traces/sampled:\n      receivers: [otlp]\n      processors: [attributes/redact, tail_sampling, batch]\n      exporters: [otlphttp/traces]\n    metrics:\n      receivers: [spanmetrics]\n      processors: [batch]\n      exporters: [prometheusremotewrite]\n```\n\n### Worked numbers: payload budget and the sampled-cost trap\nIllustrative platform: 1,000,000 requests a day, 30 KB average payload, 2% of traces with errors or slow.\n\n| Quantity | Calculation | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Payload volume | 1,000,000 × 30 KB | ~30 GB a day |\n| 90-day payload retention | 30 GB × 90 | ~2.7 TB |\n| Traces kept by tail sampling | 2% + 5% of the other 98% | ~6.9% of traces |\n| Cost job run on the trace store | sees ~6.9% of calls, skewed to slow, expensive ones | attributed spend far below the invoice and shifts whenever sampling changes |\n| Cost from a token-usage metric on the full stream | every call counted before sampling | matches the invoice within pricing error |","patterns":["**Trace-aware load balancing in front of tail samplers.** Run a first collector tier with the `loadbalancing` exporter routing by trace id, so every span of a trace reaches the same sampling instance. Monitor late-arriving spans and sampling-decision counts per policy.","**Token usage as a metric, not a trace query.** Emit the GenAI conventions' client token-usage histogram (`gen_ai.client.token.usage`) from instrumentation, labelled by model and token type, and price it in the metrics layer. Monitor the gap between attributed spend and the provider invoice each month.","**Payload store with per-tenant TTL.** Write prompts and completions to object storage or a database keyed by a reference id, with retention set by data classification; spans carry only the reference. Monitor payload bytes per tenant per day and expired-object deletion lag.","**Opt-in content capture.** Leave prompt and completion capture off by default in instrumentation libraries (they gate it behind an explicit setting) and enable it per service after a data review. Monitor which services have it on.","**Scores written back by trace id.** Analysis tools such as Langfuse attach scores to a trace id; have the online judge and the feedback endpoint write scores keyed by the same id the gateway generated. Monitor the share of scores that join to a stored trace.","**Mandatory-attribute contract test.** A CI test runs the service against a stub model and asserts every model span carries tenant, bundle and config version; dashboards track the share of production model spans missing them. Monitor that share; above zero means attribution is leaking."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"App and gateway spans, OTel SDK\"] --> C[\"OTel Collector\"]\n    A -->|\"payload by ref\"| PS[(\"Payload store, own TTL\")]\n    C --> R[\"Redaction by data class\"]\n    R --> SM[\"Spanmetrics, full stream\"]\n    R --> TS[\"Tail sampling\"]\n    SM --> M[(\"Metrics DB\")]\n    TS --> T[(\"Trace store\")]\n    T --> L[\"LLM analysis and scores\"]\n    L -->|\"low-score traces\"| D[(\"Eval datasets\")]\n    M --> AL[\"Alerts and SLOs\"]\n    AL -.->|\"link to trace query\"| T","caption":"Note the fork after redaction: metrics are computed from the full stream, and only the trace export is\ntail-sampled; payloads sit in their own store, linked by reference."},"interview":[{"q":"Why put an OpenTelemetry Collector between services and backends?","a":"- **Central policy:** redaction, sampling and routing run once, instead of every team reimplementing them\n  and some getting it wrong.\n- **Fan-out and portability:** the same stream goes to the trace store, metrics database and LLM analysis\n  tool, and swapping a backend is an exporter change.\n- **Correct ordering:** metrics can be derived from the full stream before tail sampling drops traces."},{"q":"Your error-rate alert fires but there are no example traces. What went wrong?","a":"- **Likely head sampling:** a flat percentage decided at request start discarded most failures.\n- **Or a broken tail-sampling setup:** spans of one trace spread across collector instances, so no instance\n  saw the error span with its trace.\n- **Fix:** tail sampling with error and latency policies behind a trace-id-aware load balancer, and link\n  each alert to a trace query."},{"q":"How do you design payload storage for prompts and completions?","a":"- **Separate store, referenced from spans by id**, because payloads are bulky and sensitive.\n- **Retention and access per data classification:** for example short TTL and redaction for confidential\n  tenants; spans keep only the pointer.\n- **Budget it:** at a million requests a day and tens of kilobytes each, payloads are tens of gigabytes a\n  day (illustrative), so retention is a cost decision."},{"q":"Attributed spend is 30% below the invoice. What are the candidate causes?","a":"- **Missing attributes:** spans without tenant or bundle, so mandatory keys are not enforced.\n- **Bypass:** calls made outside the gateway with their own keys.\n- **Measurement:** cost computed from the tail-sampled trace store, or SDK retries hidden below one span."},{"q":"When is a single vendor SDK the right call instead of a full stack?","a":"- **One team, one tool, no residency constraints:** the SDK captures richer LLM detail with less code.\n- **Keep an exit:** use OpenTelemetry underneath where possible, so moving to the full stack later is a\n  configuration change rather than re-instrumentation.\n- **Switch** when a second team, a second provider or a compliance requirement appears."}],"tradeoffs":["**OpenTelemetry vs vendor SDKs.** OTel keeps instrumentation neutral and composable; vendor SDKs capture richer LLM detail with less code. The SDK wins only for a single team with one tool and no residency constraints.","**Tail vs head sampling.** Tail keeps every interesting trace but buffers whole traces in the collector, needing memory and sticky routing; head sampling is cheap but throws away the errors you wanted.","**Full payloads vs safety.** Storing prompts and completions makes incidents reproducible but creates a sensitive store with obligations; hashing or short previews trade debuggability for safety, differently per tenant classification.","**Span-derived metrics: consistency vs cardinality bill.** No hand-written counters, but the labels you allow (tenant × bundle × model) set the metrics bill.","**Self-hosted vs cloud analysis tool.** Self-hosting keeps data in your boundary but adds an operational surface; the vendor cloud is simpler until the first customer asks where their prompts are stored."],"pitfalls":["**Breaking trace context at the gateway or a queue.** Symptom: cost attributed to \"unknown\" and model spans with no parent, so a slow request cannot be followed end to end. Fix: propagate W3C trace context everywhere.","**Logging prompts as text lines in the application logger.** Symptom: customer data in the log SaaS, discovered during a retention audit, with no per-tenant redaction possible. Fix: payload store plus collector redaction.","**User id or request id as a metric label.** Symptom: the metrics database runs out of memory or the vendor bill multiplies as series count explodes. Fix: keep those in traces.","**Head sampling at a flat percentage.** Symptom: an error-rate alert with no example traces, because the failures were in the discarded 90%. Fix: tail sampling with error and latency policies.","**Mandatory attributes left optional.** Symptom: a regression visible in aggregate but not attributable to a bundle or prompt version, so rollback becomes guesswork. Fix: contract tests.","**Judge scores stored without the trace id.** Symptom: a quality dashboard that cannot be joined to the requests it describes. Fix: write scores keyed by trace id.","**Computing cost or volume from the sampled trace store.** Symptom: attributed spend that is a small, unstable fraction of the invoice and moves whenever someone changes the sampling rate. Fix: an unsampled token-usage metric."],"when":"- **Build the stack, not one tool,** as soon as a second team, a second model provider or a compliance\n  requirement appears.\n- **Before that,** a single LLM-tracing tool with OpenTelemetry under it is enough and migrates cleanly\n  later.\n- **Order:** tracing through the gateway and mandatory attributes, then the collector with redaction, then\n  derived metrics and scores.\n- **Measure:** share of model spend attributed to a tenant and feature, time from an alert to an example\n  trace, and whether a judge score joins to its request.\n- **Interviews:** draw the five layers and justify two choices: why redaction and sampling live in the\n  collector, and why payloads are stored separately from spans.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments model, retrieval and tool calls with the standard attributes, propagates trace context across services, and writes the collector configuration for sampling and redaction.","architect":"Chooses the wire format, the per-signal stores, the retention and redaction policy per data classification, and the mandatory attribute set every team must emit.","fde":"Connects the customer's existing APM and SIEM to the stack, agrees which payloads may be stored and for how long, and gives the customer a trace view for their own debugging."},"check":{"q":"Finance says the provider invoice is 30% higher than the sum of what each product team reports.\nCompliance separately asks whether any prompt containing a customer's account number was stored.\nWhich parts of a well-built observability stack answer each question, and what is missing if they\ncannot?","a":"The spend gap is a cost-attribution question answered by spans: every model span carries\ntokens, model and tenant and bundle attributes, and a cost series is computed from those token counts\n(a query or job over the trace store, or a token-usage metric emitted by the instrumentation, since\nspanmetrics only derives call counts and durations) priced per model and grouped by tenant and bundle. If the sum does not match the invoice, either some spans lack tenant or bundle, which means the\nmandatory attributes are not enforced, or some calls bypass the instrumented path, which means keys\nexist outside the gateway. Two quieter causes are worth checking: the cost job reads the tail-sampled\ntrace store rather than an unsampled metric, so it undercounts by design; or a client library retries\nbelow the instrumented span, so one span can stand for more than one billed call. The compliance question is answered by the collector's redaction policy\nand the payload store: if account numbers are redacted before export for the relevant classification,\nthe answer is a configuration document plus a query over the payload store for that tenant's\nretention window. If prompts were logged as plain log lines in a third-party log service, there is no\nreliable answer, which is the failure the stack exists to prevent."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a platform route telemetry through an OpenTelemetry Collector instead of exporting directly from each service to the backend?","options":["The collector compresses traces so storage is cheaper","Redaction, sampling and fan-out are enforced in one place rather than re-implemented by every team","Backends cannot accept connections from many services at once","It is the only way to derive metrics from spans"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each application getting redaction and sampling right independently is the thing that fails. A central collector applies policy once and can export the same stream to several backends."},{"q":"Why is tail-based sampling preferred over head-based sampling for LLM traces?","options":["It uses less memory in the collector","It samples a fixed fraction, which keeps costs predictable","The traces worth keeping (errors, low scores, slow requests) are only identifiable once the trace is complete","Head sampling is unsupported by the GenAI semantic conventions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A decision at the start of a request cannot know it will fail or score badly. Tail sampling waits for the whole trace, keeps all of the interesting ones and a small fraction of the rest, at the price of buffering."},{"q":"Why are prompts and completions stored separately from spans and metrics?","options":["Span attributes have a hard size limit of a few bytes","They are bulky and sensitive, so they need their own retention and access control, referenced from spans by id","Metrics databases cannot store strings","Analysis tools require a dedicated payload API"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Payloads dominate volume and carry customer data; keeping them in a dedicated store lets the platform apply per-tenant retention and access rules without touching the trace or metrics stores."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/ai-control-plane-vs-data-plane","sources":["Official docs: Kubernetes components","Official docs: Envoy xDS protocol","Official docs: Istio architecture"],"why":"- **The two halves have opposite requirements.**\n\n| | Request path | Management side |\n|---|---|---|\n| Volume | thousands of calls a second | a few changes an hour |\n| Latency | every millisecond added before the model starts lands in time-to-first-token | nobody cares if a change takes two seconds |\n| Must be | fast, up through partial failures | consistent, audited, permissioned |\n| Examples | route, call model, retrieve, guard | promote a bundle, change a canary percentage, add a tenant's model allow-list, rotate a key |\n\n- **Per-request lookups put the config store on the hot path.** If the request path reads alias, policy and\n  budget from the management database on every call, its latency joins every request's p99 and its\n  availability becomes the platform's; one config-store maintenance window is a total outage.\n- **Baking config into the service makes every change a redeploy.** Each alias move or rollout change\n  becomes a deploy, rollback takes as long as a build, and nobody can tell which configuration was live at\n  14:07 yesterday.\n- **The split is old and proven.**\n  - Routers separate the forwarding path from the routing protocols that compute its tables.\n  - Kubernetes separates the API server, store and controllers from the kubelets running pods; workloads\n    keep running while the API server is down, you just cannot change anything.\n  - Service meshes stream configuration from a control plane to sidecar proxies, which keep their last\n    configuration if the control plane disappears.\n- **AI platforms inherit it *because* an AI gateway is a proxy with policy**, and registries and rollout\n  state are exactly the configuration such a proxy needs. The reliable part stays simple and reads local\n  state; the auditable part stays small and changes slowly.","how":"### What lives where\n| Control plane (system of record) | Data plane (request path) |\n|---|---|\n| model registry and aliases | authenticate the caller against cached credentials |\n| prompt registry and bundle definitions | resolve tenant and use case |\n| rollout state: stable bundle, candidate, percentage, shadow on or off | pick the bundle according to rollout state |\n| per-tenant and per-use-case policy: allowed models, data-classification routing such as \"confidential tenants only to in-region models\", budgets, rate limits | render the prompt and enforce policy locally |\n| credential management | route to the model with fallbacks |\n| evaluation gates and release approvals | retrieve and execute tools |\n| deprecation calendar | run guardrail checks and cache |\n| cost accounting and the audit log of every change | emit telemetry |\n| low-QPS, strongly consistent, permissioned, boring | stateless with respect to config, horizontally scaled, no synchronous control-plane call per request |\n\n- **In policy terms:** the control plane is the decision point and the data plane the enforcement point.\n- **Decisions are precomputed into configuration** *because* a per-request decision call would put the\n  control plane back on the hot path.\n\n### Configuration as versioned snapshots\n1. **Publish.** The control plane emits its state as an immutable, monotonically numbered snapshot with\n   everything the data plane needs: aliases, bundles, rollouts, policies.\n2. **Fetch.** Replicas pull on a short interval or receive a push.\n3. **Verify** the snapshot's signature.\n4. **Swap atomically.**\n\n- **Whole snapshots, not per-key updates,** *because* a bundle and its model pin must change together; a\n  replica that applied the new prompt but not the new model for three seconds serves a combination nobody\n  evaluated.\n- **Monotonic versions** *because* two replicas fetching from two control-plane nodes must never regress.\n- **Every span carries the snapshot version**, which makes \"what was live at 14:07\" a query and makes\n  rollout and rollback attributable.\n\n### Fail static\n- **Unreachable control plane → keep serving the last snapshot.** Replicas also persist it locally, so a\n  cold start during an outage still comes up.\n- **The cost: nothing can change, including rollback.** So design a break-glass path: an operator-supplied\n  local override snapshot that replicas accept with a logged, time-limited exception.\n- **Same trade as the meshes:** availability of serving over freshness of configuration.\n\n**Fail static is not unconditional.**\n- **Most config is safe stale for hours:** aliases, rollout percentages, prompts.\n- **Some entries are security decisions whose staleness is itself a breach:** a revoked API key, an\n  offboarded tenant, a newly blocked model or region.\n- **Give the snapshot a staleness budget,** alert when a replica's config age exceeds it, and decide per\n  entry type what happens past it: keep serving (most of the snapshot) or deny (revocations).\n- **Revocations also travel on a separate, faster push channel,** so they do not wait for the next full\n  snapshot.\n\n### Telemetry flows up\n- **The data plane emits spans, usage and cost** tagged with tenant, bundle, model and snapshot version.\n- **The control plane consumes aggregates:** budgets are enforced against them, canary analysis reads the\n  candidate-versus-stable comparison, and a failed canary makes the control plane publish a snapshot with\n  the rollout reverted.\n- **The loop is asynchronous by design.** Per-request decisions use local counters; precise global quotas\n  use a shared fast store that is part of the data plane, not the control plane.\n\n### Where the gateway fits\n- **The AI gateway is data plane;** its admin API, configuration database and UI are control plane.\n- **Many gateway products ship both in one process**, fine for one team.\n- **At platform scale,** the gateway runs as stateless replicas consuming snapshots, and the admin side is\n  a separate service with its own availability target, access control and audit.","example":"### Fail-static data-plane handler\nA minimal data-plane handler:\n- **Starts from the snapshot persisted on local disk** and refreshes it in the background.\n- **Keeps serving the old snapshot** when the control plane is unreachable.\n- **Picks the bundle with a sticky canary bucket** and enforces tenant policy locally.\n- **Stamps the snapshot version** on telemetry.\n\nSignature verification is left out for brevity. `call_model` and `emit_span` are the application's\nexisting functions.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, os, threading, time, urllib.request\n\nLOCAL = \"/var/lib/gateway/last_snapshot.json\"\nCONF = json.load(open(LOCAL))      # last-known-good from disk, so a cold start survives an outage\n\ndef refresh(url=\"http://control-plane/config/current\", every=30):\n    \"\"\"Pull the control plane's snapshot; keep serving the old one if it is unreachable.\"\"\"\n    global CONF\n    while True:\n        try:\n            with urllib.request.urlopen(url, timeout=2) as r:\n                snap = json.load(r)\n            if snap[\"version\"] > CONF[\"version\"]:        # monotonic: a stale node never wins\n                CONF = snap                               # whole-snapshot atomic swap\n                with open(LOCAL + \".tmp\", \"w\") as f:      # write-then-rename: a crash mid-write\n                    json.dump(snap, f)                    # must not corrupt last-known-good\n                os.replace(LOCAL + \".tmp\", LOCAL)\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(f\"control plane unreachable ({e}); still serving config v{CONF['version']}\")\n        time.sleep(every)\n\nthreading.Thread(target=refresh, daemon=True).start()\n\ndef bucket(key):                                           # stable across replicas, unlike hash()\n    return int(hashlib.sha256(key.encode()).hexdigest(), 16) % 100\n\ndef resolve(c, use_case, tenant):\n    r = c[\"rollouts\"].get(use_case)      # {\"stable\": \"support@41\", \"candidate\": \"support@42\", \"percent\": 5}\n    if r and bucket(f\"{r['candidate']}:{tenant}\") < r[\"percent\"]:  # salted by candidate: each\n        return r[\"candidate\"]               # rollout picks different tenants; percent counts tenants\n    return r[\"stable\"] if r else c[\"aliases\"][use_case]\n\ndef handle(tenant, use_case, messages):\n    \"\"\"Data-plane path: every decision is a local lookup; no control-plane call per request.\"\"\"\n    c = CONF                                               # read the pointer once per request\n    bundle = resolve(c, use_case, tenant)\n    model, prompt = c[\"bundles\"][bundle][\"model\"], c[\"bundles\"][bundle][\"prompt\"]\n    allowed = c[\"policy\"].get(tenant, {}).get(\"allowed_models\")\n    if allowed is not None and model not in allowed:\n        return {\"error\": \"model not allowed for tenant\", \"config_version\": c[\"version\"]}\n    resp = call_model(model, prompt, messages)\n    emit_span(tenant=tenant, bundle=bundle, model=model, config_version=c[\"version\"], usage=resp.usage)\n    return resp\n# A canary is snapshot v+1 with percent=5; promotion sets stable=support@42, percent=0; rollback sets\n# stable=support@41. None of them redeploys this process.\n```\n\n### Staleness budget per entry type\nPure Python: decide what a replica does with each part of a stale snapshot. Budgets are illustrative\npolicy values.\n\n```python\nBUDGET_S = {\"aliases\": 6 * 3600, \"rollouts\": 6 * 3600, \"prompts\": 6 * 3600,\n            \"revocations\": 120, \"tenant_offboarding\": 120}\n\ndef stale_action(entry_type: str, config_age_s: float) -> str:\n    if config_age_s <= BUDGET_S[entry_type]:\n        return \"serve\"\n    if entry_type in (\"revocations\", \"tenant_offboarding\"):\n        return \"deny\"                       # unknown revocation state: fail closed\n    return \"serve+alert\"                    # availability over freshness\n\nfor age in (60, 900, 8 * 3600):\n    print(age, {k: stale_action(k, age) for k in (\"aliases\", \"revocations\")})\n# 60 {'aliases': 'serve', 'revocations': 'serve'}\n# 900 {'aliases': 'serve', 'revocations': 'deny'}\n# 28800 {'aliases': 'serve+alert', 'revocations': 'deny'}\n```\n\n\"Deny\" here means: refuse requests whose authorisation depends on the stale entry (for example any key\nnot re-confirmed by the fast revocation channel), not the whole service.\n\n### Worked outage: two hours without a control plane\nIllustrative midday outage of the control-plane database on a well-built platform.\n\n| Capability | During the outage | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Serving requests | works | replicas hold the last snapshot in memory and on disk |\n| New replicas starting | works | they load the persisted snapshot |\n| Telemetry | works | the collector is a data-plane concern |\n| Budgets | approximate | degrade to local counters |\n| Promotions, canary changes, new tenants, key rotation | stopped | they are control-plane writes |\n| Rollback via the normal path | stopped | needs a new snapshot |\n| Emergency rollback | works via break-glass | logged, time-limited local override |\n| \"Which config was live at 14:07?\" | answerable afterwards | spans carry the snapshot version |","patterns":["**Streaming config with last-known-good, as in Envoy xDS.** Envoy proxies receive listeners, routes and clusters from a management server over the xDS APIs and keep serving their current config if the management server goes away. Model an AI gateway's config feed the same way. Monitor the config version each replica reports and the time from publish to the last replica.","**Local evaluation of rules, as feature-flag SDKs do.** Server-side flag SDKs (LaunchDarkly, Unleash and similar) download the ruleset and evaluate flags in process, so a flag check never makes a network call. Apply this to rollout and policy lookups. Monitor SDK or snapshot staleness per replica.","**Atomic file swap for mounted config.** Kubernetes updates a mounted ConfigMap volume by swapping a symlink, so readers see the old or new set of files, never a mix; `subPath` mounts do not receive updates. Pair with an in-process version check before swapping. Monitor the lag between the ConfigMap change and the version the process reports.","**Config-age metric with a staleness alert.** Export each replica's snapshot age in seconds as a gauge and alert when it exceeds the budget; a fleet-wide rise means the control plane or its distribution is down. Monitor the maximum and the spread across replicas.","**Separate revocation channel.** Push key revocations and tenant offboarding over a low-latency pub/sub channel and fail closed when its heartbeat is missing for longer than the revocation budget. Monitor the time from revocation to the last replica denying the key.","**Audited break-glass override.** A signed local override file with an expiry, accepted only from an allow-listed operator identity and logged centrally once connectivity returns. Monitor active overrides and their remaining lifetime."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    OP[\"Operators and CI\"] --> CP[\"Control plane: registries, rollouts, policy\"]\n    CP --> AU[(\"Audit log\")]\n    CP -->|\"signed snapshot vN\"| DP[\"Data-plane replicas\"]\n    CP -.->|\"fast revocation push\"| DP\n    BG[\"Break-glass override\"] -.->|\"logged, time-limited\"| DP\n    DP --> LC[(\"Last snapshot on local disk\")]\n    DP --> MP[\"Model providers and tools\"]\n    DP -->|\"spans with config version\"| OC[\"Telemetry pipeline\"]\n    OC -->|\"aggregates\"| CP","caption":"Configuration flows down as signed, versioned snapshots; telemetry stamped with that version flows back\nup; only revocations and break-glass overrides take a shortcut."},"interview":[{"q":"Define control plane and data plane for an AI platform and say why you separate them.","a":"- **Data plane:** the per-request path (auth, bundle resolution, prompt rendering, model routing,\n  retrieval, tools, guardrails, caching, telemetry) that must be fast and stay up.\n- **Control plane:** the system of record (registries, aliases, rollouts, policies, keys, budgets, audit)\n  that must be consistent and permissioned.\n- **Why separate:** opposite requirements; per-request config lookups put the control plane's latency and\n  availability on every request, while baked-in config makes every change a redeploy."},{"q":"A prompt change caused a few seconds of bizarre answers on every deploy. What is the likely design flaw?","a":"- **Key-by-key hot reload:** replicas applied the new prompt before the matching model or tool schema, so\n  they briefly served an unevaluated combination.\n- **Fix:** publish whole, versioned snapshots and swap them atomically per replica.\n- **Verify** by checking that every span's config version maps to one consistent bundle."},{"q":"Should a replica keep serving if its config is six hours old?","a":"- **For most config, yes:** aliases, prompts and rollout percentages are safe stale; availability outranks\n  freshness.\n- **For security entries, no:** revocations and tenant offboarding past their budget should fail closed for\n  the affected requests.\n- **Either way, alert** on config age above the staleness budget, because it means distribution is broken."},{"q":"How fast does a rollback propagate, and how do you make it faster?","a":"- **Bound:** roughly the refresh interval plus fetch and swap time; with a 30-second pull, about half a\n  minute to the last replica.\n- **Faster:** push notifications that trigger an immediate fetch, or a shorter interval for a small\n  emergency subset of config.\n- **Measure:** time from publish to the last replica reporting the new version."},{"q":"When is a single gateway with an embedded admin database the right design?","a":"- **One team, one application,** where config changes are rare and both planes can share an on-call.\n- **Condition:** it already loads config into memory rather than querying per request.\n- **Split** when a second team shares the gateway or config changes outpace code deploys."}],"tradeoffs":["**Snapshots vs propagation delay.** No per-request dependency on the control plane, but changes land within the refresh interval; a kill switch that must act in seconds needs a push channel or a shorter interval for a small emergency subset.","**Local enforcement vs exact quotas.** Fast, but per-replica counters make budgets and rate limits approximate; precise global quotas need a shared data-plane counter, a dependency you must size and protect.","**Separate services vs operational surface.** The planes scale and fail independently at the cost of two deployables and two on-call surfaces; for one team and one application, a gateway with an embedded admin store is the right size until the second team arrives.","**Fail static vs frozen rollbacks.** Serving survives control-plane outages but rollbacks freeze too; a break-glass override restores agility and must itself be audited, or it becomes the way around every policy.","**Immutable snapshots vs volume.** Full attribution and reproducibility, but a busy platform publishes thousands of them, so retention and compaction become real operational tasks."],"pitfalls":["**Calling the registry or policy database on every request.** Symptom: request p99 tracks the config database's latency, and a config-store maintenance window becomes a serving outage. Fix: local snapshots.","**Hot-reloading configuration key by key.** Symptom: a burst of errors or odd answers for a few seconds after each change, when a new prompt ran against the old model or tool set. Fix: atomic whole-snapshot swaps.","**Bucketing the canary per request.** Symptom: a user flips between bundle versions mid-conversation, and support tickets nobody can reproduce. Fix: bucket per tenant or user.","**Bucketing per tenant but reading the percentage as traffic share.** Symptom: a \"5%\" canary carrying a third of all requests because one very large tenant hashed into it. Fix: weight by traffic or exclude whale tenants from early steps.","**Using Python's built-in `hash()` for buckets.** Symptom: \"sticky\" assignments that differ per replica, because string hashing is randomised per process. Fix: a stable hash such as SHA-256.","**Omitting the snapshot version from telemetry.** Symptom: canary analysis cannot tell which traffic ran under which percentage, and a rollback's effect cannot be dated. Fix: stamp it on every span.","**No break-glass path.** Symptom: a bad bundle live during a control-plane outage, everyone able to see it and nobody able to change the alias. Fix: an audited local override.","**Treating key revocation as ordinary snapshot config.** Symptom: a revoked key keeps working on some replicas for as long as they cannot fetch a fresh snapshot. Fix: fast channel and fail closed."],"when":"- **Make the split explicit** once configuration changes more often than code deploys, or more than one\n  application shares the gateway, registries and policies.\n- **Below that,** a single gateway process with its own small database is fine, provided it already reads\n  config into memory rather than per request.\n- **Measure two numbers:** control-plane calls per request (target zero) and time from an alias move to the\n  last replica serving it.\n- **Day to day:** \"promote\", \"canary to 5%\" and \"roll back\" are control-plane operations that take seconds\n  and leave an audit entry; the data plane is redeployed only for code changes.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"what still works when the control plane is down\" and \"how does a rollback happen\n  without a deploy\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements snapshot refresh, atomic swap, sticky bucketing and local policy checks in the gateway or application, and stamps the config version on every span.","architect":"Draws the boundary: which decisions are precomputed into snapshots, the refresh and push strategy, the fail-static and break-glass rules, and the availability target of each plane.","fde":"Deploys data-plane replicas inside the customer's network or region while the control plane stays central, and explains why their rollback is an alias move rather than a redeploy."},"check":{"q":"The control plane's database is unavailable for two hours in the middle of the day. In a\nwell-designed AI platform, what keeps working, what stops, and what does the answer tell you about\nthe design?","a":"Serving keeps working: every data-plane replica holds the last snapshot in memory and on local\ndisk, so requests resolve bundles, enforce policy and route to models as before, and new replicas can\nstill start from the persisted copy. Telemetry keeps flowing to the collector, since that is a\ndata-plane concern. What stops is change: no promotions, no canary adjustments, no new tenants, no key\nrotations, and, importantly, no rollback through the normal path. Budget enforcement degrades to\nlocal counters. If any of the serving path stopped, the design had a synchronous dependency on the\ncontrol plane that should not exist. If rollback was impossible for two hours, the design lacked a\nbreak-glass override. If nobody could later say which snapshot was live during the window, spans\nwere not stamped with the version. The outage is a test of exactly those three properties."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these belongs in the control plane rather than the data plane?","options":["Rendering the prompt template for a request","Running the guardrail classifier on a completion","Changing the canary percentage for a use case's candidate bundle","Choosing the fallback model after a provider timeout"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rollout state is configuration the control plane publishes; the data plane reads it. Rendering, guardrail inference and fallback selection all happen per request on the serving path."},{"q":"Why does the data plane consume whole versioned snapshots instead of hot-reloading individual keys?","options":["Snapshots are smaller on the wire than key-by-key updates","Related settings such as a bundle's prompt and model pin must change together, and a version number makes behaviour attributable","Control-plane databases cannot stream individual changes","Hot reloading is unsupported by gateway products"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Applying keys independently creates combinations nobody evaluated for a few seconds after each change. An atomic snapshot with a monotonic version avoids that and gives every span a config id."},{"q":"What should happen when the data plane cannot reach the control plane?","options":["Reject requests, since policy cannot be verified","Fall back to a default model with no policy","Keep serving the last known snapshot and log that configuration is stale","Redeploy the data plane with configuration baked in"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fail static is the design goal: availability of serving outranks freshness of configuration. The trade is that changes, including rollbacks, wait for the control plane or a break-glass override."}]},{"key":"llmops-platform/centralized-vs-federated-ai-platform","sources":["Skelton & Pais, 2019 — Team Topologies","Dehghani, 2022 — Data Mesh","Conway, 1968 — How Do Committees Invent?"],"why":"- **Adoption outruns any decision.** A team can reach production with a provider API key and a credit\n  card in an afternoon, so by the time anyone asks \"what AI do we run\", the honest answer is a survey. The\n  federated default is not a decision anyone made; it is what happens without one.\n- **That default has concrete costs:**\n  - spend spread over many accounts that never reach volume pricing;\n  - three home-grown gateways and four tracing setups;\n  - guardrails configured differently per team;\n  - no way to answer a regulator's or customer's question about which models process which data.\n- **The reflex fix, a central AI team or centre of excellence owning all access and reviewing every use\n  case, fails differently.**\n  - It cannot know the claims, support and legal domains well enough to own their prompts and quality\n    bars, so quality disputes replace quality.\n  - Every model or prompt request waits in a queue; the most urgent teams route around it, recreating the\n    shadow usage centralization meant to end.\n  - Measured by what it ships, it starts building applications for the loudest business unit and stops\n    being a platform.\n- **The decision exists because both failure modes are real and opposite.** Conway's law predicts the\n  platform will tend to follow the organisation's communication structure, so the question is which\n  responsibilities must be owned once and which must stay with the people who understand the domain.\n- **Two established answers translate directly.** Team Topologies: a platform team treating the platform\n  as a product consumed by stream-aligned teams. Data Mesh (for analytics): domain ownership with federated\n  computational governance. For AI: domains own prompts and evals, the platform owns the rails, and rules\n  are enforced by code rather than review.","how":"### The two poles\n| | Centralization | Federation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Gateway and keys | platform team runs the gateway with all provider keys | each team or business unit picks providers and holds keys |\n| Model list | one approved list | per team |\n| Registries, eval harness, judges | central | built or bought per team |\n| Tracing | one trace pipeline | per team |\n| Guardrails and governance | baseline guardrails, risk classification, model cards | each sets its own quality bar |\n| Applications | often built centrally too | built by product teams |\n| Central function | owns everything; teams request access and capacity | at most a policy document and a quarterly review |\n\n### What must be owned once (the mandatory core)\nThese only work if there is exactly one of them, *because* their value comes from completeness:\n- **Identity and model access through a single gateway**, so every call is authenticated, attributed and\n  rate-limited.\n- **Budgets and chargeback**, so finance sees one account and the volume discount applies.\n- **Inventory and audit log**, so \"which models touched this tenant's data\" is a query.\n- **The telemetry pipeline with its redaction policy**, so compliance holds for every team.\n- **The deprecation calendar**, so a provider sunset is handled once.\n- **The approved-model list with risk tiers.**\n\n- **Keep it thin *because*** every mandatory item is friction, and friction breeds workarounds.\n\n**Pooled keys pool quota.** Provider rate limits and quotas are typically set per account or project, so\nshared keys share the budget:\n- **The failure:** one team's overnight batch summarisation job consumes the tokens-per-minute budget a\n  customer-facing assistant needs, and the assistant sees 429s it did nothing to cause.\n- **The requirement:** a central gateway is only safe if it also owns per-team quotas and priority classes\n  (interactive before batch), so the shared pool is partitioned, not first-come-first-served.\n\n### What must stay with the domain\n- **Quality is domain knowledge.** Prompts, golden sets, rubrics, retrieval corpora, agent designs and the\n  acceptance thresholds for a claims extractor can only be owned by the claims team, *because* only they\n  can judge an output.\n- **Federating these is not a concession;** it is the only way the quality bar means anything.\n- **The platform supports, not imposes:** a shared eval harness, judge services, templates and training.\n\n### The paved road\nThe shared-but-optional layer between mandatory and federated:\n- **What it contains:** a reference application template with tracing, evals and guardrails pre-wired; a\n  managed judge; dataset tooling; golden-path documentation.\n- **It works only when easier than bypassing,** a product requirement on the platform team: self-service\n  onboarding, a published SLA for requests, and a roadmap driven by adoption metrics, not feature counts.\n- **Money is part of \"easier\".** A markup over provider prices makes bypassing cheaper, so charge back at\n  cost (or start with showback, reporting spend without billing it) until the pooled discount makes the\n  gateway visibly cheaper than a private key.\n- **Governance is computational.** CI conformance checks (spans carry mandatory attributes, model pins are\n  on the approved list, bundles are registered) replace review meetings, *because* review scales with\n  headcount and checks scale with code.\n\n### Mapping to control plane and data plane\n- **The split often lands on the control-plane boundary:** centralize the control plane (policy, keys,\n  inventory, rollout state, deprecations) and allow federated data-plane deployments.\n- **Why:** a business unit with residency or isolation requirements runs its own gateway replicas in its\n  own region or network while consuming central configuration.\n- **The reverse,** federated policy with a shared gateway, is rarely stable.\n\n### Moving the line as the organisation grows\n| Stage | Right move |\n|---|---|\n| One to three teams | no platform, only the most mature team's stack; make that stack reusable |\n| A handful of teams | a shared gateway and telemetry pipeline, worth a part-time owner |\n| Past roughly ten teams, or the first regulated use case | a dedicated platform team with the mandatory core pays for itself |\n\nSignals that the line is in the wrong place are measurable:\n- **Share of model spend** flowing through the gateway.\n- **Median time** from \"we want to use a model\" to the first production call.\n- **Age of the platform's request queue.**\n- **Unregistered use cases** found per audit.","example":"### Worked scenario: an insurer with 14 teams\nAll figures illustrative.\n\n**Starting point:**\n- **14 product teams** building on LLMs: three bought their own provider keys, two built gateways, one has\n  an evaluation harness, and the rest copy prompts between repositories.\n- **Spend:** around $180k a month across seven accounts, so no account reaches the provider's volume tier\n  and finance cannot attribute spend to products.\n- **Compliance** asks which models process policyholder data and receives an estimate.\n\n| Option | What happens | Cost | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. Full centralization** | a six-person platform team takes every key, builds gateway, registries, harness and tracing, and reviews each team's prompts | roughly 25 requests a month × two engineer-days each = about 50 of the team's 120 engineer-days on review | little left for the platform; the queue grows; the two teams with deadlines keep their keys |\n| **B. Leave it federated** | each team builds its own gateway, tracing and harness | about six engineer-weeks × 14 teams ≈ 84 engineer-weeks of duplicated work | 14 guardrail configurations and still no inventory |\n| **C. Hub and spoke** | a quarter spent on the mandatory core only: gateway with keys, budgets and audit; trace pipeline with redaction; approved-model list; deprecation calendar; paved-road template. Keys outside the gateway revoked on a dated schedule | one quarter of platform work | spend in one account, so even a 10% volume discount is worth about $18k a month; inventory becomes a query; teams keep prompts, evals and corpora and inherit tracing from the template |\n\n**Option C's own success metrics:**\n- **Share of spend through the gateway:** target near 100%.\n- **Median onboarding:** under two days via self-service.\n- **Unregistered use cases at the next audit:** zero.\n\n### Computational governance: the CI conformance check\nPure Python: what replaces the review meeting. Each team's bundle manifest is checked against the\nplatform's rules in CI; the approved list and registry would be fetched from the control plane.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\nAPPROVED = {\"chat-model@2026-06-01\": \"tier-2\", \"small-model@2026-04-15\": \"tier-1\"}\nREGISTERED = {\"claims-extractor\", \"support-assistant\"}\nMANDATORY_SPAN_KEYS = {\"app.tenant\", \"app.bundle\", \"app.config_version\"}\n\ndef conformance(manifest: dict) -> list:\n    errs = []\n    if manifest[\"name\"] not in REGISTERED:\n        errs.append(\"bundle not registered in the inventory\")\n    tier = APPROVED.get(manifest[\"model\"])\n    if tier is None:\n        errs.append(f\"model {manifest['model']} not on the approved list\")\n    elif manifest[\"risk_tier\"] == \"high\" and tier != \"tier-2\":\n        errs.append(\"high-risk use case needs a tier-2 approved model\")\n    missing = MANDATORY_SPAN_KEYS - set(manifest[\"span_keys\"])\n    if missing:\n        errs.append(f\"spans missing mandatory keys: {sorted(missing)}\")\n    if manifest.get(\"provider_key\"):\n        errs.append(\"direct provider key found; route through the gateway\")\n    return errs\n\nm = {\"name\": \"claims-extractor\", \"model\": \"chat-model@2026-06-01\", \"risk_tier\": \"high\",\n     \"span_keys\": [\"app.tenant\", \"app.bundle\"], \"provider_key\": None}\nerrs = conformance(m)\nprint(\"\\n\".join(errs) or \"conformant\")   # spans missing mandatory keys: ['app.config_version']\nsys.exit(1 if errs else 0)\n```","patterns":["**Gateway as the only egress to providers.** Revoke direct provider keys on a dated schedule and block provider API domains at the network egress proxy except from the gateway. Monitor the share of provider invoice spend that the gateway can attribute; the gap is bypass traffic.","**Per-team virtual keys with budgets and rate limits.** Gateways such as LiteLLM's proxy issue virtual keys per team with spend budgets and tokens-per-minute and requests-per-minute limits, which partitions a pooled provider quota. Add a priority class so interactive traffic is served before batch. Monitor 429s per team and quota headroom at peak.","**Showback before chargeback.** Publish monthly spend per team and feature from gateway telemetry at cost for a quarter before billing it, so teams trust the numbers. Monitor attribution coverage (spend with a team and feature label) before turning on billing.","**Policy as code on manifests.** Express platform rules (approved models, mandatory attributes, risk tiers) as tests that run in every team's CI, for example Rego policies run with Conftest or plain test scripts like the one above. Monitor conformance failures per team and the time to fix them.","**Self-service onboarding with a published lead time.** A form or API that issues a gateway key, a telemetry project and the paved-road template, with an SLO such as \"first production call within two days\". Monitor median and p90 onboarding time and the request-queue age.","**Enabling engineers, time-boxed.** Platform engineers embed with a team for a few weeks to adopt the paved road, then leave. Monitor how many teams are on the template and whether they stay after the embed ends."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T1[\"Claims team: prompts, evals, corpus\"] --> PR[\"Paved road: template, harness, judge\"]\n    T2[\"Support team: prompts, evals, agents\"] --> PR\n    T1 --> CI[\"CI conformance checks\"]\n    T2 --> CI\n    PR --> CI\n    CI --> CORE[\"Mandatory core: gateway, keys, budgets\"]\n    CORE --> INV[(\"Inventory and audit log\")]\n    CORE --> TEL[\"Telemetry with redaction\"]\n    CORE --> MP[\"Model providers\"]\n    CORE -->|\"central config\"| RG[\"Regional data-plane replicas\"]","caption":"Domain teams own quality and reach the mandatory core either through the paved road or directly, but always\nthrough CI conformance checks; regional replicas run the data plane on central configuration."},"interview":[{"q":"What belongs in the mandatory core of an AI platform, and why keep it thin?","a":"- **Core:** identity and model access through one gateway, budgets and chargeback, inventory and audit,\n  telemetry with redaction, the deprecation calendar and the approved-model list.\n- **Test for inclusion:** the item only delivers value when complete; a partial inventory or partial\n  budget is useless.\n- **Keep it thin** because every mandatory item is friction, and friction produces shadow keys."},{"q":"Six months after mandating a central gateway, a third of spend still bypasses it. How do you diagnose it?","a":"- **Compare paths:** measure onboarding lead time and price through the gateway against a private key; if\n  the sanctioned path is slower or marked up, bypass is rational.\n- **Find the traffic:** reconcile provider invoices per account against gateway-attributed spend to locate\n  the bypassing teams.\n- **Fix the product, then enforce:** self-service onboarding, at-cost charging, then dated key revocation\n  and egress blocking."},{"q":"Why not centralize prompts and golden sets along with everything else?","a":"- **Quality is domain knowledge:** only the claims team can judge a claims extraction, so a central owner\n  produces quality disputes instead of quality.\n- **Queues form:** every prompt change waits on a team that cannot prioritise across domains.\n- **Instead:** offer a shared harness, judge services and templates, and let domains own content and\n  thresholds."},{"q":"A shared gateway pools provider keys. What new failure does that create?","a":"- **Shared quota:** provider rate limits are typically per account or project, so one team's batch job can\n  starve another team's interactive traffic.\n- **Mitigation:** per-team quotas, priority classes (interactive before batch) and separate pools or\n  projects for heavy batch work.\n- **Monitor:** 429s per team and quota headroom at peak."},{"q":"When would you deliberately allow a federated data plane?","a":"- **Residency or isolation requirements:** a business unit that must keep traffic in a region or network\n  runs its own gateway replicas.\n- **Keep the control plane central:** policy, keys, inventory and rollout state stay single, so governance\n  still holds.\n- **Justify each by regulation, not preference,** because each deployment is an operational surface to\n  support."}],"tradeoffs":["**Centralized access: pooled pricing and one enforcement point vs bottleneck risk.** You also get a complete inventory, but a single point of failure and a possible queue; it tips back toward federation only when gateway outages or queue cost more than the duplication did.","**Federated prompts and evals: domain quality vs duplication.** Harnesses and standards duplicate; offering the harness as a paved road recovers most consistency without taking ownership away.","**Long mandatory list: compliance vs workarounds.** Every item added to the core should be justified by something that only works when complete.","**Central chargeback: precise vs meaningless.** Precise when all traffic flows through one gateway, meaningless when 40% bypasses it; attribution quality is a direct function of how mandatory the gateway really is.","**Federated data-plane deployments: residency and isolation vs operational surface.** Each one is a surface the platform must support; justify it by regulation, not preference."],"pitfalls":["**The platform team building applications for one business unit.** Symptom: a roadmap full of that unit's features while other teams wait, and the platform stagnating. Fix: measure adoption, not features.","**Mandating the gateway without self-service onboarding.** Symptom: shadow keys reappear within a quarter, because the sanctioned path was slower than a credit card. Fix: self-service with a published lead time.","**Federation without an inventory.** Symptom: a compliance questionnaire answered by survey, and a provider sunset discovered separately by each team. Fix: inventory in the mandatory core.","**Measuring the platform by features shipped.** Symptom: a rich platform with a third of spend bypassing it. Fix: spend-through-gateway as the headline metric.","**Centralizing prompts and golden sets.** Symptom: a central team defending a quality bar for a domain it does not understand, and the domain team disowning the result. Fix: domain ownership.","**Governance by review meeting.** Symptom: a monthly committee that cannot keep up, and approvals that become rubber stamps. Fix: CI conformance checks.","**Pooling provider keys without per-team quotas.** Symptom: a customer-facing assistant throwing 429s every night when another team's batch job runs, then that team asking for its own key back. Fix: per-team quotas and priority classes."],"when":"- **Decide explicitly when the second team starts an LLM project;** the cheapest moment to make the gateway\n  and telemetry mandatory is before anyone has to migrate.\n- **Centralize what must be complete to be useful, federate what needs domain judgement,** and put\n  everything else on the paved road.\n- **Revisit the line** when the number of teams, the regulatory load or the spend concentration changes.\n- **Measure:** spend through the gateway, time to first production call, queue age and audit findings.\n  Day to day it shows up as the argument over who owns a prompt, who pays for a judge, and whether a team\n  may hold its own key.\n- **Interviews:** name the mandatory core, explain why quality ownership cannot be centralized, and describe\n  computational governance instead of committees.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds on the paved-road template, registers bundles and pins approved models, and adds the CI conformance checks that make federated governance enforceable.","architect":"Draws the line between the mandatory core, the paved road and domain ownership, and defines the adoption metrics that tell the organisation when to move it.","fde":"Fits a customer deployment into their platform model, running data-plane components in the customer's boundary while respecting their central policy, keys and inventory."},"check":{"q":"After a compliance scare, a CTO proposes moving all AI work into one central team. What would\nyou centralize, what would you refuse to centralize, and how would you stop the central team from\nbecoming a bottleneck?","a":"Centralize what the scare actually needs: model access through one gateway with keys, budgets\nand audit, the telemetry pipeline with redaction, the inventory and risk classification of use cases,\nthe approved-model list and the deprecation calendar. Those answer the compliance question completely\nonly if they are complete, which is the argument for making them mandatory. Refuse to centralize\nprompts, golden sets, rubrics, corpora and agent designs, because quality judgement lives with the\ndomain and a central team owning them would produce either a queue or an unowned quality bar. To\navoid the bottleneck, run the platform as a product: self-service onboarding with a published lead\ntime, a paved-road template that is faster than building, conformance checks in CI instead of review\nmeetings, and adoption metrics such as spend through the gateway and time to first production call\nas the team's success criteria. Offer enabling engineers to embed with teams temporarily rather than\ntaking their work over."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which responsibility is strongest as a candidate for the mandatory central core?","options":["The golden datasets for each product's evaluation","Model access, keys and budgets through a single gateway","The agent design for the support assistant","The retrieval corpus for the legal team"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Access and budgets only deliver inventory, attribution and pooled pricing when they are complete, which requires a single enforcement point. Golden sets, agents and corpora need domain judgement."},{"q":"Which symptom most clearly indicates over-centralization rather than under-centralization?","options":["Compliance cannot list which models process customer data","Three teams run three different gateways","Teams with deadlines keep their own provider keys because the sanctioned path takes weeks","Provider spend is spread across many accounts"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Shadow keys reappearing despite a mandate means the central path is slower than bypassing it. The other three symptoms are what federation without a core looks like."},{"q":"What distinguishes a paved road from a mandate in a federated platform?","options":["A paved road is enforced by CI, a mandate by policy documents","A paved road applies only to new teams","A paved road is adopted because it is easier than the alternative, while a mandate is required regardless","A paved road covers infrastructure, a mandate covers prompts"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The paved road competes with building it yourself and wins on convenience; mandates are reserved for the few things that must be complete to work. Both can be checked in CI."}]}]});
